\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: IMM Shows JPY Longs Piled Up; US Data Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,359 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 2010\par 04:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] IMM commitment of traders report shows net JPY long non-commercial positions vs USD rose to 52,183 contracts in week to September 7 vs 49,904 contracts week earlier, showing trend-followers may begin reducing JPY long positions in near future as investors are known to be unwilling to pile up JPY positions too far from 50,000. Daisuke Karakama, senior economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  says as U.S.-Japan bond yields gap widening, U.S. economic indicators such as retail sales data due tomorrow may give market cue to square their positions if those indicators turn out better than expected. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP moving lower, keeping in step with weaker USD vs EUR, other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rising on risk appetite after China industrial data; pair last at 43.94 -- first time below 44 since Aug. 7, 2008 -- after settling at 44.12 late Thursday; support at 43.75, resistance 44.30; "The sentiment is really bullish for the peso and we're getting help from strong Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the euro," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares tipped higher following Wall Street gains, higher regional markets, with telecoms expected to return to focus ahead of scheduled auction next week of 3G mobile licenses; National Telecommunications Commission due to announce today which bidders are qualified for auction. State-owned \uc2 CAT Telecom\uc2  due to seek injunction today against auction, though analysts say success unlikely. Market otherwise still fairly buoyant, efforts to sell down have been limited. "It still doesn't look like it wants to have any major correction on the downside," says Andrew Yates, head of sales for international equities at Asia Plus Securities. May be some caution ahead of planned rally by so-called Red Shirts this Sunday. Resistance at 930, support at 920. Index closed +0.3% at 924.57 Friday. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0201 GMT [Dow Jones] September HSI futures +0.9% at 21,400, indicating cash market to open higher after Wall Street's gains Friday. Contract ended at 58-point discount to cash, suggesting modestly negative bias near-term after cash market's 7th session gains in past 8. Open interest +3,941 lots at 100,556. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0200 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY down sharply again, falling to intraday low in modern era, after PBOC set fixing at fresh record low for second consecutive session at 6.7509 vs 6.7625 Friday. On OTC, USD/CNY at 6.7634, after falling as low as 6.7568 shortly after open, vs previous modern-era intraday low of 6.7644 Aug. 9, 6.7692 last close. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is intentionally moving the yuan higher," says Guangzhou-based trader at foreign bank. "We think it's not really due to political pressure, but rather likely aims to ease import price pressure." Trading thin so far; USD/CNY tipped in 6.7550-6.7650 band for session. 1-year USD/CNY NDF down to 6.6570/6.6600 vs 6.6732/6.6782 late Friday. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares up early, led by oil producers, automakers, but banks tempering index gains; Shanghai Composite Index +0.2% at 2668.82, tipped to trade in 2650-2750 band for now. "Upward momentum for the Shanghai index should remain intact after major economic indicators showed China's growth picked up in August, though it's not expected to see a major rise because of continued pressure on banks to set aside funds for a possible new loan-reserve rule," says Zhang Yongfeng at \uc2 Sinolink Securities\uc2  Wealth Management Center. August CPI +3.5% on year vs July's +3.3%, fastest in nearly two years but in line with expectations. Industrial-output growth +13.9% on year vs July's +13.4%. Among actives, \uc2 China Oilfield Services\uc2  (601808.SH) +1.9% at CNY14.31, ICBC (601398.SH) down 0.3% at CNY4.06, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (601939.SH) down 0.4% at CNY4.61. Shenzhen index +0.4% at 1191.97. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0155 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD drifts lower to 31.800 vs 31.910 at close on Friday, driven by foreign inflows to local stock market, says local bank trader. "Right now, the economic outlook is optimistic and the stock market is up, which is supporting the drop in the pair." Tips pair in 31.740-31.900 band for session. Taiex last up 1.2%. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0145 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.7% at new 2010 high of 1448.88 on broad-based gains, with blue chips leading advance, says dealer with local brokerage. "With worries over a possible slowdown in the U.S. subsiding, people are now more upbeat," says dealer; thinks 1450 resistance may hold as many people still away on extended Idul Fitri holiday break with next barrier at 1468 (January 4, 2008 peak). Volume thin with only 86 million shares transacted (vs more than 100 million last week in first half-hour of trade). Blue chips gaining include Tenaga (5347.KU) +1.1% at MYR9.05, Maybank (1155.KU) +0.7% at MYR8.48, \uc2 KL Kepong\uc2  (2445.KU) +0.6% at MYR17.20. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0144 GMT [Dow Jones] China \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's record-low fixing of USD/CNY, at 6.7509, makes it harder for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make a strong case that \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is needed to contain the strong JPY, says Douglas Borthwick, managing director of Faros Trading. "As China is now telegraphing they are comfortable with further CNY strength, the region's central banks will also be able to let their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthen," further isolating Tokyo in its efforts to put a floor on USD/JPY, Borthwick adds. Notes Faros now looking for significant drops in USD/Asians. USD/JPY last at 84.17 vs 84.21 late Friday in NY trade. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0140 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD lower on offshore cues, says senior trader at Chinese bank. Tips pair in 7.7630-7.7690 band near term. Adds "the recent rise of Chinese yuan is supportive to the Hong Kong dollar as the freely-convertible local unit is often traded as a proxy to the yuan." Pair last at 7.7664 vs 7.7685 late Friday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0136 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/JPY hits 1-month high at 78.54 as investors increasingly show preference for commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Minoru Shioiri, senior dealer at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. Adds markets saw no immediate action by Chinese authorities to curb inflation after China's strong CPI data Saturday, leaving a bullish view on commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  intact. Tips resistance in near term at 79.50 vs last 78.30. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0135 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares firmer, riding uptick in other Asian markets after Wall Street's Friday advance. Gainers outnumbering decliners by 2.6 to 1. STI +0.3% at 3031.64 after setting new year-to-date high of 3045.61 in early trade. Firm resistance at lower end of 3085-3146 breakdown gap formed in June 2008. "It should be safe to say that as long as the 3000 psychological level prevails, the short-term trend remains up," says CIMB. As in recent sessions, small caps still most sought-after, with 8 out of top 10 most active stocks in market worth under S$1.00, including Genting Hong Kong (S21.SG), +2.3% at US$0.45, \uc2 Passion Holdings\uc2  (I3L.SG), off 2.4% at S$0.205, Top Global (519.SG), flat at S$0.01, Hisaka (FV2.SG), +2.9% at S$0.355. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com) [ 09-12-10 2210ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100913e69d0001c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
