\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: China Record Fixing Makes JPY  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Harder\b0\par\par\uc2 1,344 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 2010\par 03:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0144 GMT [Dow Jones] China \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's record-low fixing of USD/CNY, at 6.7509, makes it harder for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to make a strong case that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is needed to contain the strong JPY, says Douglas Borthwick, managing director of Faros Trading. "As China is now telegraphing they are comfortable with further CNY strength, the region's central banks will also be able to let their respective \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strengthen," further isolating Tokyo in its efforts to put a floor on USD/JPY, Borthwick adds. Notes Faros now looking for significant drops in USD/Asians. USD/JPY last at 84.17 vs 84.21 late Friday in NY trade. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0140 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD lower on offshore cues, says senior trader at Chinese bank. Tips pair in 7.7630-7.7690 band near term. Adds "the recent rise of Chinese yuan is supportive to the Hong Kong dollar as the freely-convertible local unit is often traded as a proxy to the yuan." Pair last at 7.7664 vs 7.7685 late Friday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0136 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/JPY hits 1-month high at 78.54 as investors increasingly show preference for commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Minoru Shioiri, senior dealer at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. Adds markets saw no immediate action by Chinese authorities to curb inflation after China's strong CPI data Saturday, leaving a bullish view on commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  intact. Tips resistance in near term at 79.50 vs last 78.30. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0135 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares firmer, riding uptick in other Asian markets after Wall Street's Friday advance. Gainers outnumbering decliners by 2.6 to 1. STI +0.3% at 3031.64 after setting new year-to-date high of 3045.61 in early trade. Firm resistance at lower end of 3085-3146 breakdown gap formed in June 2008. "It should be safe to say that as long as the 3000 psychological level prevails, the short-term trend remains up," says CIMB. As in recent sessions, small caps still most sought-after, with 8 out of top 10 most active stocks in market worth under S$1.00, including Genting Hong Kong (S21.SG), +2.3% at US$0.45, \uc2 Passion Holdings\uc2  (I3L.SG), off 2.4% at S$0.205, Top Global (519.SG), flat at S$0.01, Hisaka (FV2.SG), +2.9% at S$0.355. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares tipped higher, with strong overseas markets last Friday and positive sentiment after successful Philippine PHP global bond issuance Thursday. Benchmark PSEi may initially rise to 3950 after setting a new closing high 3902.56 Thursday (Manila markets closed Friday for holiday); next stop 4000, with support at 3850. Banking, property stocks likely to lead expected market advance; "I think the bull will continue its run, with the 4000 level as the likely target this week. This is entirely new territory for us," says Astro del Castillo, managing director at First Grade Holdings. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0123 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures flattish despite losses in U.S. Treasurys Friday, upbeat U.S. wholesale inventory data, with bonds supported by finance minister Yoon Jeung-hyun's comments Sunday that downside risk to global economic recovery looks greater now amid signs of slowing economic activity in some large economies; 3-year yield flat at 3.40%, 5-year yield down 1 bp at 3.89%, September futures +4 ticks at 112.72. There's no reason why 3-year yield shouldn't aim for 2009 closing low level of 3.26% in near term, says bond manager at local securities firm. Tips futures in wide 112.50-112.90 range for day. Kospi's strong performance, with index trading above 1800 mark, may weigh on bonds, but finance minister's "relatively dovish" remarks to support, he adds. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0118 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI may rise tracking modest gains on Wall Street Friday, while China's August CPI data roughly inline, unlikely to inflame any lingering China policy tightening worries, says Francis Lun at Fulbright. Note, China August CPI +3.5% on-year vs +3.3% July, matching +3.5% tipped in Dow Jones poll. "The CPI data is pretty much inline with market expectation and I don't expect China to raise interest rates in the near-term," Lun says. Tips 21,500 as near-term cap for HSI; index ended +0.4% at 21,257.39 Friday. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0118 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB slips to 30.75 vs 30.81 late Friday in Asia as USD falls against regionals after EUR picks up gains; trader says MYR hitting fresh 13-year high vs USD also prompting selling in USD/THB. \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  suspected of stepping in at 30.75 level, although not heavily yet, traders say. Market still mulling over possibility BOT may impose measures to stem THB's rise amid increasing noise, with Federation of Thai Industries Friday saying action needs to be taken to support businesses, warning USD/THB could drop to 29.50 by end-October. Support pegged at 30.70, resistance at 30.80. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0110 GMT [Dow Jones] 10-year cash JGB yield may again target 1.195%, level touched last Monday, if U.S. Treasury yields maintain uptrend, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  chief strategist Chotaro Morita; thus, market focused on 20-year JGB auction tomorrow as "one key supply-demand event." 10-year JGB yield currently +1.0 bp at 1.160%. Adds if DPJ heavyweight and fiscal expansionist "Ozawa, manages an upset victory (at DPJ presidential election tomorrow), it could lead to a short-term sell-off in the JGB market, but we would not expect a lot of downside considering the actual effect such an outcome would likely have on supply and demand." Local media reports over weekend that PM Kan remains in lead. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0106 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds likely steady, trading in range on lack of market-moving news as most market players away on extended Idrul holidays, says dealer with local brokerage. "We expect a quiet day for bond trades today, with yields likely ending rangebound," says dealer; adds stronger MYR (USD/MYR last at 3.100 vs 3.1090 late Friday) may spur some buying interest in Bank Negara notes, but volume should stay well below the MYR1.0 billion mark, and is likely to only recover later this week. Last Thursday, yields on August 2015 MGS flat at 3.32%, September 2017 MGS down 2 bps at 3.55% and November 2019 MGS down 2 bps at 3.68%. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0106 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped lower, likely taking cues from advancing EUR, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; pair may slip to 44.00 psychological support after settling at 44.12 late Thursday. Next support 43.75, while resistance pegged at 44.30. But Manila-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader notes that demand for USD for requirements of banks that subscribed to peso global bond may provide USD/PHP some support. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0104 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR at fresh 13-year low of 3.1000, vs 3.1090 late Thursday, tracking falls in USD/Asians, say dealers with local banks. "News over the extended weekend that Prime Minister Najib will allow the ringgit to trade offshore isn't having much impact as his comments were expected," says one dealer; adds "I don't expect much activity today as most people are still away on extended holidays." Tips next support at 3.000. Market was closed for Idul Fitri holiday Friday. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com) [ 09-12-10 2144ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100913e69d00013\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
