\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks End Week Higher\b0\par\par\uc2 2,683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 2010\par 00:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Indonesian markets are closed Monday for the Ied-ul-Fitr Shawwal holiday.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10462.77 +47.53  +0.46% \par Nasdaq     2242.48  +6.28  +0.28% \par S&P 500    1109.55  +5.37  +0.49% \par FTSE 100   5501.64  +7.48  +0.14% \par Xetra DAX  6214.77  -6.75  -0.11% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  99 20/32   flat 0.571%  flat \par 5Y Tsy  98 13/32  -3/32 1.582% +2.2BP \par 10Y Tsy 98 17/32 -10/32 2.794% +3.7BP \par  \par USD/JPY       84.23-27     +0.10% \par Range         84.42-84.18 \par EUR/USD       1.2721-26    +0.32% \par Range         1.2735-1.2678 \par AUD/USD       0.9303-09    +0.44% \par Range         0.9307-0.9268 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    11.50-11.90% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    10.39-10.81% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    11.50-12.10% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,246.50   -$4.40 \par Comex Gold Range    $1.237.90-$1,253.00 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $76.45   +$2.20 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks rose Friday, putting the market into positive territory for the second-straight week. Energy companies including \uc2 Chevron\uc2 , Halliburton and Schlumberger led the climb, boosted by increased expectations for global oil demand.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average advanced 47.53 points, or 0.46%, to 10462.77, its highest close since Aug. 10. For the week, the measure eked out a gain of 0.14%, its second-straight week in the black. The increase helped the Dow erase its declines for the year. It is now up 0.33% for 2010.\par \par \uc2 Chevron\uc2  was the Dow's best performer Friday, up 1.46, or 1.9%, to 78.82, after the International Energy Agency revised slightly higher its estimate for global oil demand for 2010.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite edged up 6.28, or 0.28%, to 2242.48. It climbed 0.39% this week but is still off for 2010 with a decline of 1.18%.\par \par \uc2 The Standard & Poor's 500 index rose 5.37, or 0.49%, to 1109.55, putting its weekly gain at 0.46% although it is down 0.5% for the year.\par \par \uc2 The energy sector led the S&P 500's Friday gains, with Halliburton up 66 cents, or 2.2%, at 30.88, and Schlumberger up 78 cents, or 1.3%, at 59.31.\par \par \uc2 However, the utilities sector fell, weighed down by a drop of 4.03, or 8.4%, to 44.21, in \uc2 PG&E\uc2 , following an explosion in San Francisco involving a natural gas pipeline owned and operated by the California utility.\par \par \uc2 Friday's climb across the rest of the market came as data showed bigger-than-expected Chinese imports and a larger-than-forecast increase in inventories at U.S. wholesalers. The reports followed other economic releases over the past two weeks that have beat expectations.\par \par \uc2 "The recent economic reports are confirming that while the rate of recovery in our economy has slowed, it's still on a positive trajectory," said Elizabeth Miller, president of Summit Place Financial Advisors.\par \par \uc2 Miller said Friday's inventory data followed that trend.\par \par \uc2 "That is a sign that businesses are now starting to restock. We've actually been waiting a good many months for them to start to do this. Usually they won't start restocking until they see the demand coming."\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended higher Friday led by banks, as concerns that Beijing could introduce further tightening measures eased after official data showed China's trade surplus narrowed.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo shares rose Friday as a slightly weaker yen and an upward revision to Japan's second quarter economic growth rate fueled buying in exporters, while investors kept a hopeful eye on possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei jumped over 2.0% during the morning session as sentiment was buoyed by the dollar's rise to the Y84 level on better-than-expected U.S. employment data, followed by a widely-anticipated revision to Japan's April-June GDP figure to an annualized 1.5%, up from the initial reading of a 0.4% rise.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese government also unveiled a Y915 billion stimulus package, along with a statement calling for the Bank of Japan to carry out additional policy measures to support the economy, and promised to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when necessary.\par \par \uc2 "Although long overdue, there is a growing sense of crisis (among Japanese authorities)," said Toshikazu Horiuchi, equity strategist at Cosmo Securities. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is increasingly in a situation where \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot be avoided, he added.\par \par \uc2 Horiuchi put the Nikkei range for the week at 9000 to 9500.\par \par \uc2 Crude futures settled above $76 a barrel Friday for the first time since mid-August, after a pipeline leak in Illinois rattled traders worried about its effect on supplies.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures retreated farther from record levels Friday as investors' desire for the perceived safety of the metal continued to wane, trumping optimism that the International Monetary Fund found a buyer for more than $400 million of the commodity.\par \par \uc2 Base metals closed lower on the London Metal Exchange Friday as concerns of a possible interest rate rise in China and ongoing anxiety over a potential probe into trading in the rubber market kept cautious investors sidelined.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar weakened against commodity-backed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the Australian and New Zealand dollars Friday as positive Chinese economic data fueled bets on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  closely tied to the pace of global growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A narrowing in China's trade surplus, with a stronger-than-expected increase in imports, boosted investor confidence that Chinese demand is holding up even as China has made moves to put a brake on its growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Chinese data continued a rally sparked Thursday on the heels of better-than-expected economic data in the U.S. and Australia, allaying some concerns that a global recovery could be slowing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gains in growth-oriented \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were modest, though, with investors likely exercising caution ahead of a weekend full of possible market-moving events, including the release of additional key Chinese data, said Dan Cook, senior market analyst at IG Markets in Chicago.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro joined the ride of riskier \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moving higher, but its gains were likely capped by caution ahead of a meeting of international banking regulators in Basel, Switzerland, analysts said\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Friday, the euro was at $1.2712 from $1.2703 late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y84.21 from Y83.83, while the euro was at Y107.03 from Y106.49. The U.K. pound was at $1.5358 from $1.5438.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against a trade-weighted basket of others, was at 82.723 from 82.649.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Developments over the weekend are likely to keep investors' appetite for risk well supported early this week, limiting the downside in "growth-sensitive" \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," BNZ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Strategist Mike Jones said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most notably, Saturday's slew of Chinese data showed economic growth has begun to stabilize, after a wobbly start to the September quarter, Jones said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among the data, China's consumer price index rose 3.5% from a year earlier on a jump in food prices, accelerating from July's 3.3% increase for the biggest gain since October 2008. The August rise matched the median 3.5% increase forecast in a Dow Jones Newswires survey.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Industrial production rose 13.9%, up from 13.4% in July, and well above expectations for a 12.9% rise, which would have continued a slowing trend in recent months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Second, uncertainty about the capital adequacy of European banks is likely to fade after the Basel Committee reached a deal to increase capital ratios to 7% this morning. The increase was slightly less than some had feared and banks will have until the end of 2018 to implement the new rules," Jones said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 International financial officials Sunday reached agreement on the much-awaited new rules, which aim to reduce the risk of future market turmoil by significantly increasing the amount of capital that banks have to hold.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Basel Committee on Banking Supervision announced that the total capital that banks need to set aside against potential losses will remain at at least 8% of their risk-weighted assets. But it will be strengthened by an additional capital buffer representing 2.5% of risk-weighted assets, taking it to 10.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Of that, common equity, which is the most loss-absorbing form of capital, must represent 4.5% of risk-weighted assets. Including the 2.5% conservation buffer, which will be of the same quality, banks will be required to hold a minimum common equity ratio of 7%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tier 1 capital will rise to 6.0% from 4.0% currently.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China aims to let the yuan move more freely in line with economic needs, but this a long-term goal, Xia Bin, an adviser to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Xia told Dow Jones Newswires Saturday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The authorities intend to let the exchange rate reflect "as much as possible" the needs of the real economy, which calls for greater yuan flexibility, Xia said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But he said this is the "long-term direction" and that it's up to the People's Bank of China to decide on how to implement the country's exchange-rate reform.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Xia said the economic situation doesn't justify any sharp policy changes. The government should fine-tune macroeconomic policy but "won't and shouldn't" change the basic policy direction, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's yuan jumped to a one-month high against the U.S. dollar intraday Friday after the People's Bank of China surprised the market by setting the dollar-yuan central parity rate at a record low. The fixing came despite a modest rise in the U.S. Dollar Index overnight.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner said in an interview with The Wall Street Journal Friday that China hasn't done enough to allow its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the yuan, to rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "China took the very important step in June of signaling that they're going to let the exchange rate start to reflect market forces. But they've done very, very little, they've let it move very, very little in the interim," Geithner said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economists said Saturday's data don't show a great need for yuan appreciation. [ 12-09-10 2210GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A rapid, "one-way" rise isn't consistent with China's stated goal of having "two-way" flexibility in the exchange rate, said Galaxy Securities chief economist Zuo Xiaolei. "The yuan will undergo a fluctuating upward trend in the future, but the pace of appreciation won't be too great. We expect the yuan to rise by 2%-3% by the end of the year."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys slipped Friday for a third-straight session as fear of a double-dip recession in the U.S. economy eased, reducing demand for safe assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Government bonds in Germany and the U.K. also lost ground as investors turned to riskier assets--ranging from corporate bonds to U.S. stocks--for better returns.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 10-year Treasury note posted a third-straight weekly loss. The last time the note fell for three consecutive weeks was in June 2009.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of 2000 GMT, the benchmark 10-year Treasury note was down 10/32 in price and its yield rose to 2.794%. A week ago it was at 2.711%. The 30-year bond was 18/32 lower and the yield was up to 3.870%, compared with 3.788% a week earlier. Bond yields move inversely to prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 10-year Treasury note's yield touched 2.823%, the highest level since 2.841% set on Aug. 10.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors have sold Treasurys in recent sessions after a flurry of U.S. data was less dire than expected, including Thursday's reports on improvement in jobless claims and the U.S. trade deficit. Friday, stronger-than-forecast imports in China, one of the main engines of the global economy, lifted optimism.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended lower Friday as the National Bureau of Statistics' unusual move to advance the release of August CPI data intensified concern that CPI growth might exceed market expectations, possibly triggering imminent interest rate hikes, traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds fell Friday after a weaker-than-expected five-year auction prompted selling in the belly of the curve, which also came under pressure due to higher Japanese share prices and weaker U.S. Treasurys overnight.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors also sold JGBs ahead of Tuesday's election that will determine whether Prime Minister Naoto Kan, who favors fiscal consolidation, will remain Japan's leader. His challenger in the ruling Democratic Party of Japan presidential vote is party power-broker Ichiro Ozawa, who advocates more fiscal spending.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts said that if Ozawa succeeds in ousting Kan, bond yields, which move inversely to prices, could continue to rise. Polls of DPJ members, the only ones to vote in the election, suggest Kan has only a marginal lead, meaning the election could fall either way.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Should Ozawa win, the benchmark 10-year JGB yield could climb to 1.200% next week, said RuiXue Xu, debt markets strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Royal Bank of Scotland\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The looming DPJ vote likely made investors cautious in Friday's tender as the election results "will have a huge impact on the JGB market," Xu said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par Basel Committee Announces Tighter New Capital Standards \par IMF G-20 Report: Risks To Economic Recovery Have Intensified \par WSJ: Economy's Biggest Challenge Is Washington's Paralysis - Geithner \par WSJ: BAE Systems Commercial Aerospace Assets On The Block -Sources \par SEC May Move To Prevent Companies From Hiding Debt \par =WSJ: China Has Done 'Very, Very Little' On Exchange Rate-Geithner \par US Treasury Official Sees Sustained Yuan Move \par China Inflation, Output Up, But Rate Hike Unlikely \par PBOC Adviser: China Needs Yuan Flexibility Long-Term \par Japan PM Kan Keeps Lead In Presidential Poll--Mainichi \par China August CPI Up 3.5% On Year; Market Expected Up 3.5% \par China August Industrial Output Up 13.9% On Year; Expected Up 12.9% \par China August PPI Up 4.3% On Year; Market Expected Up 4.5% \par China August Retail Sales Up 18.4% On Year \par China August New Yuan Loans CNY545.2 Bln \par OIL DATA: China August Crude Output 17.43 Mln Tons \par PBOC Adviser Calls For Deposit-Rate Hike--Xinhua \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  Seeks EUR9.8B Capital To Buy Postbank, Lift Ratios \par WSJ: Japan Election To Be Strongly Felt By Financial Markets \par Obama Says More Needs To Be Done To Lift Economy \par Regulators Probe California Gas-line Explosion \par US Wholesale Inventories Rise 1.3% In July \par Godaddy.com Is Up For Sale \par China Facing Huge Unemployment Pressures \par Chinese Investors Looking At Prudential Bid - Newspaper \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2245  New Zealand  Aug Food Price Index \par 0130  Australia    Jul Lending Finance \par 0300  S Korea      Aug Import price index \par 0300  S Korea      Aug Export price index \par 0645  France       Jul Balance of payments \par 0830  Hong Kong    2Q  Quarterly Index of Industrial Production & \par                    Quarterly Producer Price Indices for \par                    Manufacturing Industries \par 0830  UK           Jul CML Regulated Mortgage Survey \par 1000  France       Jul OECD Composite Leading Indicators \par 1400  US           2Q  Quarterly Financial Report: Manufacturers, Mining & \par                    Wholesale Trade \par 1630  US           Atlanta Fed Pres Lockhart moderates Q&A session with UK \par                    Ambassador in Atlanta \par 1800  US           Aug Monthly Treasury Statement of Receipts & Outlays of the \par                    U.S. Govt \par 2301  UK           Aug Nationwide Consumer Confidence Index \par 2301  UK           Aug RICS UK Housing Market Survey \par N/A   China        World Economic Forum Annual Meeting of the New Champions opens \par N/A   France       OECD Higher Education International Conference \par N/A   EU           Council of the European Union President Herman \par                    Van Rompuy meets Ukraine President Viktor Yanukovych \par N/A   EU           EU Economic & Financial Affairs - EU interim economic \par         