\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ/The Source: Japanese Authorities Looking Mousey\b0\par\par\uc2 714 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2010\par 12:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (This article has been posted on The Source, the Wall Street Journal Online's site for European real-time analysis {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/source"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/source }}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Posted by Nicholas Hastings \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The cat is starting to look like the mouse. Instead of the Japanese authorities appearing in control, threatening \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and keeping yen buyers on the hop, they are swiftly losing credibility.\par \par \uc2 Even if Prime Minister Naoto Kanis is toppled in DPJ leadership elections next Tuesday, and is replaced by the more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 -friendly Ichiro Ozawa, it is hardly likely that an exercise to push the yen lower will work.\par \par \uc2 In fact, it is the markets--not the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or the Bank of Japan--that will be in control. There are at least three reasons for this. First is flows. Tokyo may like it, or it might not, but in this uncertain world, with the global recovery sputtering, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains a safe haven for the international investor.\par \par \uc2 If that wasn't bad enough, China now seems keen to jump on the bandwagon, putting additional upward pressure on the yen, which is already up at levels last seen in 1995. New data this week shows that Beijing invested the equivalent of another Y583 billion into Japanese assets markets in July, even more than the Y457 billion it did in June, when the yen was nearly 10% cheaper.\par \par \uc2 Second, there is the lack of international co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The markets have long surmised that Japan is unlikely to get support from other Group-of-3 members--that is, the U.S. and the euro zone--while these countries are also struggling to keep their export-driven economic recoveries on track. In other words, neither the Federal Reserve or the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  are going to help Japan push the yen lower if that means putting their own recoveries at risk.\par \par \uc2 In a rather coincidental side-swipe at Tokyo, U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner called on China this week to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate more rapidly. Geithner is hardly likely to welcome Japan doing just the opposite.\par \par \uc2 Thirdly, there is the lack of domestic co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Of course, if the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  decides that it will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the yen's rise, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be forced to do its bidding. However, the MOF is well aware that, as the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  illustrated with its franc \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  attempts back in May, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is only a success when it is conducted in tandem with a similar monetary policy move. In other words, the Bank of Japan would need to introduce further monetary easing around the time of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help ensure that the yen is pushed lower.\par \par \uc2 So far, though, there is little sign that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is keen to do such a thing again just yet. On the contrary, BOJ Governor Masaaki Shirakawa has been making it clear this week that while past quantitative easing may have helped the financial system, it has had a limited impact on the economy.\par \par \uc2 So while the BOJ may still ease policy some more if financial markets start tumbling again, there is little reassurance that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will co-operate purely in an effort to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It isn't surprising, that as it waits for the DPJ election results, the MOF has found its hands tied, with little to do other than resort to warnings that it is carrying out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "simulations" in the hope that this will put off yen buyers.\par \par \uc2 Instead of being the powerful cat keeping the mousey markets guessing, the MOF is looking more like the mouse itself.\par \par \uc2 Early Friday, the yen was a little lower with a new Y915 billion stimulus package from Finance Minister Naoto Kan failing to have much impact on market sentiment. Instead, the dollar appeared to be still benefiting from Thursday's news that the U.S. trade gap had narrowed 14% in July and that jobless claims fell much more than expected last week. The euro, which remains under the cloud of sovereign debt, failed to make any gains.\par \par \uc2 -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com }}}\uc2 . [ 10-09-10 1002GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100910e69a000w0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
