\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ(9/10) Japan Blasts China On Yen Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 1,056 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2010\par 02:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A10\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda raised questions Thursday about China's intentions behind purchasing Japanese government bonds, a factor cited by many analysts behind the rising value of the Japanese yen. A World News article and headline in some editions on Friday overstated the level of concern that Mr. Noda expressed and incorrectly suggested that Mr. Noda complained directly about China's role in the yen's rise.\par \par \uc2 (WSJ September 14, 2010)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 09, 2010 20:01 ET (00:01 GMT)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL) \par    By Alex Frangos \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo raised the heat on Beijing for contributing to a strong yen, adding to tensions between Asia's two largest economies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 As the yen hovered near a 15-year high against the U.S. dollar Thursday, Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda called for talks with China over its recent yen-buying spree, which has helped drive the yen higher, making Japanese goods less competitive with China's.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Noda's comments, while perhaps aimed at a domestic audience, are nevertheless a sign of frustration among China's neighbors. Their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  increasingly have become coveted by China as it seeks to diversify its massive reserves and reduce its reliance on the dollar. At the same time, China's critics, not least in the U.S., have long said it is giving an unfair advantage to its exporters by holding down the level of the yuan.\par \par \uc2 Chinese buying is just one factor in the yen's strength, but it has important psychological overtones -- as does any shift in China's use of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. Private investors, sensing that China's moves could continue, have followed into the strong yen trade.\par \par \uc2 Japanese government data this week showed China's 2010 yen purchases now equal $27 billion, more than six times China's combined yen buying in the previous five years.\par \par \uc2 "I don't know the true intention" of China regarding its growing appetite for yen-denominated bonds, Mr. Noda said Thursday before the Japanese parliament's upper house. He promised Tokyo would "closely cooperate" with Beijing on the subject. "We are paying close attention," Mr. Noda said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Noda reiterated his government's pledges to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stem the yen's rise. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't entered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since 2004, the last time it sold yen for dollars. If \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  does \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the world's two largest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -reserve holders could find themselves operating in global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets at the same time.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue adds another complication to the China-Japan relationship, which already was on the boil this week.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, China stepped up its rhetoric over Japan's arrest and possible prosecution of the captain of a Chinese fishing boat. The vessel had collided with Japanese patrol vessels near a chain of islands both nations claim.\par \par \uc2 Jiang Yu, spokeswoman for China's Foreign Ministry, blasted the arrest, saying that the use of Japanese law to address actions in disputed territory is "absurd, illegal, and invalid."\par \par \uc2 "Disputes over sovereign territory are highly sensitive," Ms. Jiang said. "Handled improperly, it could cause a serious shock to overall China-Japan relations. The Japanese side should clearly understand this."\par \par \uc2 Relations between China and Japan have swung frequently between tension and accommodation over the years as China's power has grown rapidly and that of Japan has waned. China is on pace to surpass Japan this year as the world's second largest economy after the U.S.\par \par \uc2 A more protracted economic conflict from China's role in the yen's rise is unlikely, however, as deepening trade links draw China and Japan closer together.\par \par \uc2 Japan's trade with China totaled about $27.4 billion in July, according to Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The value of Japan's exports to and imports from China is roughly equal, making China both a key producer of goods sold in Japan and a significant consumer of Japanese products.\par \par \uc2 Christian Carrillo, head of Asia-Pacific interest-rate strategy for \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  in Tokyo, saw Mr. Noda's words as more "for domestic consumption purposes" than a shot across the sea at China. "Noda wanted to be seen to do something, because everyone is screaming at the government to act."\par \par \uc2 In addition to China's yen buying, South Korea recently reported increased Chinese buying of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The moves haven't resulted in a won bounce, but South Korea has had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising against the dollar to protect the competitiveness of its exports against China's.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Jiang, the Chinese Foreign Ministry spokeswoman, on Thursday repeated Beijing's position that in managing its reserves, China is "undertaking a diversified strategy under the principles of security, liquidity, and returns," adding that the foreign ministry isn't directly involved in reserves management. China's State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which administers China's reserves, doesn't comment on specific holdings.\par \par \uc2 In the past, Mr. Noda welcomed Chinese buying of yen because it diversifies the investor base for Japan's massive government-borrowing needs. But with the Japanese economy slowing and the yen soaring, the government is under pressure by exporters to take action.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Noda's more strident rhetoric did little to convince investors that Japan has the firepower to stop the yen's ascent. "The market is fed up with warnings," said Yoshinori Nagano, a senior strategist at \uc2 Daiwa Asset Management\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dollar traded at 83.82 yen late Thursday in New York, down from 83.92 yen late Wednesday. The dollar is down about 10% against the yen this year. Because China has effectively kept the yuan pegged to the dollar, the yuan has fallen against the yen by a similar percentage.\par \par \uc2 China has slightly loosened restrictions on foreign ownership of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months, but isn't likely to give Japan enough access to Chinese securities to counterbalance the effect of its yen buying.\par \par \uc2 Japan is even more unlikely to restrict its capital market by forbidding Chinese purchases of Japanese bonds, which could have a chilling effect on international investment. "They'd have to change their own regulations, which they aren't going to do," Mr. Carrillo said.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Jason Dean and Kosaku Narioka contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100910e69a0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
