\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE: Japan Unveils Y915 Billion Stimulus, Urges BOJ To Ease Further\b0\par\par\uc2 1,045 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2010\par 12:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments by economy minister.)\par \par \uc2 By Takashi Mochizuki and Juro Osawa\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japan's government Friday unveiled a Y915 billion stimulus package and turned up the heat on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to work harder to stop deflation as policy makers try to help the export-dependent economy deal with a surging yen and a slowdown in overseas markets.\par \par \uc2 The government warned a rapid or prolonged strengthening in the yen--which is already near 15-year highs against the dollar--could disrupt \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy and financial conditions, and again threatened to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when necessary.\par \par \uc2 The economic program, which draws on existing funding to avoid the need to add to Japan's already giant debt load, was dismissed by economists as too small to have much impact given the scope of Japan's economic malaise.\par \par \uc2 The plan includes incentives and regulatory reforms to prop up employment, consumer spending and corporate investment at home. The Y915 billion size accounts for just 0.2% of gross domestic product, though the government expects it to lift GDP by about 0.3%, said Kenji Umetani, director general for economic and fiscal management at the Cabinet Office. He declined to comment on the future possibility of additional government bond issuance.\par \par \uc2 The government was spurred into action by concerns a sharp slowdown in growth could turn into a renewed downturn. Revised GDP figures Friday showed the economy expanded at an annualized pace of just 1.5% during the April-June period, down sharply from 5.0% growth in the previous quarter.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Naoto Kan is urging his Cabinet to act promptly on the stimulus to eliminate negative risks to the economy. The Cabinet Office, in its economic report for September released Friday, said the economy continues to pick up, keeping its basic view unchanged, but it cautioned the environment is becoming more severe.\par \par \uc2 "We are aware that there are downside risks, including the possibility of the economy falling into a lull," said economy minister Satoshi Arai. "The stronger yen is significantly hurting exports."\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the steps in the economic program will have little immediate impact on the economy.\par \par \uc2 "These steps are safety-net measures and not ones that a government should implement when the economy's downside risks are increasing," said Hiroshi Watanabe, an economist at \uc2 Daiwa Institute of Research\uc2 . "I understand it's ahead of the DPJ leadership race, but everything the government does lately is a halfway job," Watanabe said.\par \par \uc2 Even if the government considers a second package, Watanabe said at least several trillion yen would be necessary to have a discernable positive effect on the economy.\par \par \uc2 The government has been reluctant to ramp up spending in order to avoid worsening its already strained finances. Japan's years of deficit spending have put public debt at nearly 200% of annual GDP, the highest among industrialized nations. Ratings agencies are watching, and a downgrade in Japan's credit rating could make it more costly to service its debt.\par \par \uc2 The need to help the economy has become a focal point of the ruling party leadership race. Analysts say fiscal and perhaps \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy could shift depending on the result of the election that will effectively determine who will be prime minister after Kan's term as the head of the Democratic Party of Japan ends next Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Kan is locked in a tight race with party heavyweight Ichiro Ozawa, who has advocated increased spending, possibly financed by additional Japanese government bond sales. Local polls show Kan has a small edge but Ozawa is gaining.\par \par \uc2 The yen's rise--which has fueled worries the economy may experience even slower growth down the road, sending Tokyo shares into bear market territory 20% below this year's peak--has been a lightning-rod for policy proposals.\par \par \uc2 Ozawa wants the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the first time in more than six years to curb the yen's rise. Kan's administration has repeatedly threatened \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and is trying to coax the independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ease its super-loose monetary policy to put an end to deflation, which has haunted Japan on and off for years and hindered any sustainable recovery.\par \par \uc2 The government, in a statement with the economic package, said it expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "to take additional necessary policy steps" and conduct an "appropriate and flexible" monetary policy to support Japan's economy.\par \par \uc2 The Cabinet Office's Umetani said the package is aimed at addressing urgent concerns over the yen's strength and deflation, but also stressed that the government has a longer-term vision for promoting economic growth.\par \par \uc2 Depending on the economic and employment conditions, the government said it may also consider a second stimulus utilizing a system that allows it to take on extra liabilities of as much as Y1 trillion outside of its budget, Umetani said.\par \par \uc2 The government is also considering a plan to lower corporate tax rates when it revises tax systems and compiles the budget for next fiscal year. Japan's marginal rate is much higher than its Asian competitors such as Singapore and Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 Among the programs in the package, the government plans to spend the largest part, about Y450 billion, to extend its existing subsidy programs and other measures to promote sales of environmentally friendly housing and consumer electronics.\par \par \uc2 Other measures include: internship and other programs to help new graduates secure jobs; financial support for small businesses and measures to help them find sales channels overseas; and promotion of "green" businesses such as rechargeable batteries, low-emission cars and energy efficient lighting equipment.\par \par \uc2 Some economists said the government needs to do more and should focus on exchange rates rather than fiscal discipline.\par \par \uc2 Aggressive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  coupled with further monetary policy easing would be the best way to address the current economic woes, said Hideo Kumano, chief economist at Dai-ichi Life Research Institute.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Mochizuki and Juro Osawa, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2782; takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Tomoyuki Tachikawa and Arran Scott contributed to this article.)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 10, 2010 06:17 ET (10:17 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100910e69a001zt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
