\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Bourses Seen Lower; Yen Lower On Warning\b0\par\par\uc2 2,891 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2010\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SNAPSHOT: \par ======== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European bourses seen lower, govt debt up; yen lower vs majors; spot gold, oil futures up\f28 \par \par \uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Government: To \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  If Necessary\par \par \uc2 -WestLB To Decide Sept 20 On WestImmo Sales Process -Sources\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 TUI\uc2  Board Unlikely To Make Decision At Meeting -Source\par \par \uc2 -Steag Sale Goes To Next Round With At Least 3 Bidders -Sources\par \par \uc2 -Trichet: Could Suspend Voting Rights For Fiscal Rule Breaches -FT\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  Eyes Cash Call Of Up To EUR9 BLN\par \par \uc2 -Germany's Bundesbank: Sarrazin Has Asked To Step Down\par \par \uc2 -US Treasury Geithner: Extending Tax Cuts For Wealthy Irresponsible\par \par \uc2 -AIA Inks Bancassurance Deal With China's ICBC: Source -FT\par \par \uc2 -Several Senior Employees May Leave CICC -FT\par \par \uc2 -EU Plans To Rein In "Crazy Risks" In UK's City -Report\par \par \uc2 -French PM: Pension Reform Will Need To Be Modified In Future\par \par \uc2 -China Aug Copper Imports Up On-Month; May Rise More\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Texas Instruments\uc2  Narrows 3Q View; Midpoint Same\par \par \uc2 -Japan Lifts 2Q GDP As Firms Ramp Up Capital Spending\par \par \uc2 -Japan Introduces Fresh Stimulus Package To Battle Downside Risks\par \par \uc2 -China Trade Surplus Narrows; Surplus With US Still High\par \par \uc2 Watch for: 0830 GMT UK Aug producer prices; 0900 GMT EU Q2 Balance of payments 1st release; 1500 GMT US President Obama speaks on the economy\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & US/ASIAN SUMMARIES: \par ======== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stock markets are mixed, while investors thin out trading ahead of the weekend amid shifting views of economic recovery. The European bourses are likely to start cautiously lower, while government debt nudges higher. The yen is lower after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  warned of possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Spot gold and oil futures are higher.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCKS: \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European bourses are likely to start lower on Friday, while strategists ponder whether markets have any staying power left for the short term.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For Friday's opening, IG Markets is calling the FTSE down 37 at 5457, the DAX off 52 at 6170, and the CAC off 27 at 3695.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Material names could be the main drag after the U.S. basic materials sector gave up 0.6%, IG Markets said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Markets will be locked in a trading range with investors putting on risk when things brighten and taking off risk when the data weakens or adverse events surface," says Mizuho Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Christoph Riniker, head of strategy research at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Julius Baer & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., said he expects markets to see rangebound activity over the next few weeks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One reason is that September is known as the most difficult month of the year and indices are at the upper bound of the trading range, leaving "only limited room for further short-term increases," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He has a cautious outlook for both the U.S. and Europe, but he remains "more constructive on the European market than for the U.S."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the data front Friday, France's industrial production data come at 0645 GMT and UK PPI data at 0830 GMT.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the UK, the FTSE is now at its highest level since the tumble in May, notes \f28 \uc2 \f2 Spreadex\f28 \uc2 \f2 . While there still seems to be a divide on the bull/bear front, it appears the bullish outlook is starting to win, adds \f28 \uc2 \f2 Spreadex\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "Whilst economic data has been patchy at best, the formation of higher lows between the start of July and the end of August is indicating that this could be the start of an new upward trend," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Spreadex\f28 \uc2 \f2  says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wall Street futures are lower on Friday, after markets climbed Thursday, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan Chase\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Johnson & Johnson\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2  pacing the broad advance following better-than-expected data on employment and the U.S. trade deficit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stock markets are mixed Friday, getting a boost from a larger-than-expected fall in U.S. jobless claims, while Japanese shares got a fillip from rising imports in China.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The U.S. jobs market is showing signs of recovery, so that's easing worries about macroeconomic conditions and leading to more risk appetite," said Taurus Investment & Securities' Oh Tae-dong in South Korea.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar traded above the Y84 after Japan Friday announced a Y915 billion stimulus package aimed at dealing with domestic deflation and the surging yen. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also said it would take "decisive steps, including \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , when necessary" to curb the yen's strength.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Even if it's lip service, in reality, speculators have to be cautious if authorities are pledging to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the case of dramatic (yen) movement," said Kenichi Hirano, operating officer at Tachibana Securities.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  investors continue to focus on the ever-changing economic picture.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real question has been: Will global growth still hold up? And the answer so far appears to be 'yes,'" said Aroop Chatterjee, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  quantitative strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the U.S., monthly trade data and a weekly report on initial jobless claims helped fan the spark of optimism that benefited higher-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . That took the shine of the U.S. dollar, which typically benefits as a safe haven when investors fear the recovery is turning sluggish.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As for the euro, "The narrative in the markets has to be more consistent to sustain a rally, and right now it's lacking that consistency," said Andrew Busch, global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at BMO Capital Markets in Chicago, citing lingering concerns over the health of the European banking system as a ceiling on the gains of higher-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We remain nervous about risk appetite, as increased concerns about U.S. and global growth are likely to linger, as are worries about Greece and the periphery," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brown Brothers Harriman\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K. pound is lower, and was left out of the rally Thursday in riskier \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , falling against its rivals after the U.K. trade deficit grew to its widest on record. The Bank of England also left key interest rates unchanged. Sterling is unlikely to make any dramatic movements on Friday and is seen holding in narrow ranges against the dollar and euro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BONDS: \par ===== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bunds and gilts are likely to edge higher on Friday, amid warnings from key officials that European governments and banks have to rein in risky policies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Jean-Claude Trichet said euro-zone members that break the group's rules on public finances could have their voting rights temporarily revoked, as a way of cracking down on fiscal profligacy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In an interview with the Financial Times published Thursday, Trichet highlighted the need for a "quantum leap" in fiscal surveillance, following the euro-zone sovereign debt crisis. But he rejected the idea that a member could be thrown out for not complying with the rules.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't call for expelling members, but a temporary suspension of voting rights is something that should be explored," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On monetary policy, Trichet reiterated that the ECB was in the process of progressively phasing out the nonstandard measures that it introduced to deal with crisis-related strains, reiterating that this was "a process which takes time."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We are in a situation where central banks in particular, and also other authorities, have to remain alert and have to know that we are in an uncertain universe," Trichet said. "We always have to be prepared for new challenges that can not necessarily be foreseen and that might be in some respect unpredictable."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But he also suggested that the gloom over the U.S. economic outlook might be exaggerated. "Now there is a mood which seems to me too negative. That's my own personal feeling," Trichet said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the UK, Michel Barnier, Europe's single market commissioner, wants "smart regulation" for the London City financial district, according to The Daily Telegraph on Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We want to know who is doing what. The EU authorities are going to look at every product. They can restrict leverage, or in exceptional circumstances even ban a product temporarily," he said, speaking to The Daily Telegraph.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasurys are higher on Friday, after markets dropped for a second straight session Thursday as the latest U.S. jobs and trade data eased worries about the health of the U.S. economy, sapping demand for a $13 billion 30-year bond sale.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The deluge of new corporate bond debt sales this week, which provide higher yields, also lured many investors away from the Treasury market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The sky is not falling," said Chris Rupkey, chief financial economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\f28 \uc2 \f2  UFJ in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "You are seeing a healthy correction of the recent rally in the bond market," said Mary Ann Hurley, vice president of trading in Seattle at \f28 \uc2 \f2 D.A. Davidson & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "People still want to own bonds given the weak economic outlook but they want to own bonds off the ultra-low yield levels."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Tokyo Friday, lead December Japanese government bond futures were off 0.20 at 141.42 points, weighed down by falls in U.S. Treasurys Thursday and the Nikkei's gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But investors were not aggressively trading ahead of the Democratic Party of Japan's leadership race next week, said Mizuho Securities chief market analyst Tetsuya Miura. Prime Minister Naoto Kan and Ichiro Ozawa are vying for the post and the winner would likely be Japan's next premier and determine government spending and JGB issuance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 10-year cash JGB yield was up 1.5 basis points at 1.135%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Concern over recent yen gains had already increased among Bank of Japan policy board members at their August meeting before its easing decision later that month, according to minutes of the meeting released Friday by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ENERGY: \par ======= \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Front-month October Nymex crude remains positive, while other oil futures extended overnight losses despite broad gains in Asian shares.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts wonder about the cause of the rise in October crude, but many think the driver is arbitrage selling to take advantage of spreads with later months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fear of hurricanes may support the market around current levels, but "not strong enough to give it a further boost," Nihon Unicom analyst Hiroyuki Kikukawa says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October Nymex crude is up 51 cents at $74.76 a barrel on Globex.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October Brent is down 31 cents at $77.16 a barrel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par METALS: \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 10-09-10 0500GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold was at $1,245.60 per troy ounce, up $1.60 from Thursday's New York close and trading in a narrow $1,243.60-$1,246.90 range amid a tug of war between safe-haven demand and appetite for risk. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  remains bullish on gold and precious metals generally, but reckons gold might have to correct lower a little before its uptrend extends beyond record $1,265. It puts support at $1,238.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LME base metal prices are mostly down, unable to regain upward momentum after yesterday's Shanghai rubber rout on false market chatter. LME three-month copper is down $25 to $7,530/metric ton.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; dennis.baker@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 10-09-10 0500GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10415.24  +28.23  +0.27% \par Nasdaq     2236.20   +7.33  +0.33% \par S&P 500    1104.18   +5.31  +0.48% \par FTSE 100   5494.16