\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Chinese Data Fuel Bets On Growth-Positive  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bradley Davis   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 779 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2010\par 22:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The U.S. dollar weakened against commodity-backed \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Australian and New Zealand dollars Friday as positive Chinese economic data fueled bets on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  closely tied to the pace of global growth.\par \par \uc2 A narrowing in China's trade surplus, with a stronger-than-expected increase in imports, boosted investor confidence that Chinese demand is holding up even as China has made moves to put a brake on its growth.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese data continue a rally sparked Thursday on the heels of better-than-expected economic data in the U.S. and Australia, allaying some concerns that a global recovery could be slowing.\par \par \uc2 But even as growth-oriented \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose, they gained modestly, with investors likely exercising some caution ahead of a weekend full of possible market-moving events, including the release of additional key Chinese data, said Dan Cook, senior market analyst at IG Markets in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 International banking regulators also meet this weekend in Basel, Switzerland, as concern builds over the health of the European banking sector. The euro joined the ride of riskier \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  moving higher, but its gains are likely to be capped by caution ahead of the meetings, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Late Friday, the euro was at $1.2712 from $1.2703 late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y84.21 from Y83.83, while the euro was at Y107.03 from Y106.49. The U.K. pound was at $1.5358 from $1.5438. The dollar was at CHF1.0197 from CHF1.1052.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a trade-weighted basket of others, was at 82.723 from 82.649.\par \par \uc2 "I wouldn't say there's kind of rampant mood of 'risk-on' sentiment in the market," said Steven Barrow, head of G10 strategy at Standard Bank in London. "It's actually focused a little more on those commodity-type \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," such as the Australian and New Zealand dollars, he said.\par \par \uc2 To see the Australian dollar's performance against the U.S. dollar, please see:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4587"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4587}}}\par \par \uc2 The commodity-linked Canadian dollar gave back its earlier gains spurred by better-than-expected August jobs figures after \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  Gov. Mark Carney threw some cold water on expectations for future rises in key Canadian rates this year. He said future rate increases by Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be "carefully considered in light of the unusual uncertainty surrounding the outlook," repeating language from a statement following the bank's third consecutive interest rate hike two days ago.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  remains the only \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations to raise interest rates since the onset of the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar had dipped to a low at C$1.0288, its lowest level since Aug. 19, according to EBS via CQG, after positive Canadian jobs figures were released Friday morning, but rebounded to gain about 0.15% on the Canadian dollar in the wake of Carney's comments.\par \par \uc2 Separately, China's yuan jumped to a one-month high against the U.S. dollar intraday after the People's Bank of China surprised the market by setting the dollar-yuan central parity rate at a record low. The fixing came despite a modest rise in the U.S. Dollar Index overnight, leading traders to speculate that Beijing may be responding to both international pressure over the yuan's exchange rate and concerns about inflationary pressure at home.\par \par \uc2 The dollar gained against the yen on a day when Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan called on other nations not to interfere with Japan's possible effort to lower the yen, should Tokyo decided to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been increasing the prospect of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets amid loud calls from export to act on the surging yen, which is sitting near a 15-year high against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 With the ICE Dollar Index strengthening slightly, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 's PowerShares U.S. Dollar Index Bearish exchange-traded fund was down 0.12% from late Thursday, while its PowerShares U.S. Dollar Index Bullish was up 0.13%. The two exchange-traded funds are based on \uc2 Deutsche Bank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures indexes, whose composition mirrors that of the ICE's Dollar Index.\par \par \uc2 -By Bradley Davis, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2654; bradley.davis@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Andrew Batson and Liu Li in Beijing contributed to this article.) [ 09-10-10 1606ET ]\par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100910e69a000eq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
