\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Thai Shares +0.5% Midday; 930 Resistance Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,596 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2010\par 07:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0549 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +0.5% at 926.10 midday with big cap energy, petrochemical stocks dominating trading volume. Fund inflows continue amid rising THB but many investors now cautious as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  could impose measures to stem THB's rise after local unit hit 13-year high vs USD. Still, "it's difficult to reverse the trend. What the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can do is slowing down the baht's rise. I don't think they will use any drastic measures," says analyst with BFIT Securities Anupon Sriard. Resistance put at 930, support at 920. Among top actives, Banpu (BANPU.TH) flat at THB650, PTT (PTT.TH) +1.4% at THB289, Thai Oil (TOP.TH) +4.6% at THB50.75, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) +1.7% at THB121.50, TMB Bank (TMB.TH) +3.3% at THB2.48. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0548 GMT [Dow Jones] Economists tip China import, export growth both softer in coming months. China August trade data shows domestic economic growth slowdown not as bad as feared, but import rise above expectations still not high compared to past months, says \uc2 UBS\uc2  China economist Wang Tao. China imports tipped to decelerate further this year as domestic investment slows, Wang says. China export growth could stay soft in coming months amid global manufacturing sector downshift, says \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  economist Wang Qian in note; adds, Chinese exporters also likely front-loaded activity before July cut in export value-added tax rebates, but possible above-trend global economic growth from early next year would support steady growth of China export sector. (owen.fletcher@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0547 GMT [Dow Jones] LME 3-month copper's 50-day moving average moves above 200-day average, first such golden cross since June 2, 2009 at start of metal's climb from $5,050/ton; chartists see move as a bullish sign that trend is moving upwards. 50-day moving average last at $7149.23, 200-day at $7132.40. Still, some traders sceptical about indicator: converse "death cross", with 50-day moving average dipping below 200-day, last came on June 25 this year ahead of copper's rally from $6,770/ton then to $7,525/ton last, down $29 on yesterday's PM kerb. "That longer-term average needs to confirm itself over a week or so before you read too much into it," says Hong Kong-based trader. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0542 GMT [Dow Jones] STOCK CALL: \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  MUFG says Japanese dividends are widely underestimated, says total cash dividends paid by Topix companies will be "highest ever" in 2010 and likely to increase further in 2011 and 2012. "Dividend payout ratios are rising back to pre-bubble norms and will stay there. Dividends are responding properly to earnings growth...this is different from the 1990s and early 2000s when dividends were allowed to lag behind earnings recoveries." Says Nikkei 225 dividends per share likely to be in range of Y181/share for 2010, Y188/share for 2011 and Y195/share for 2012. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0535 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi last up 1.0% at 1802.27, slightly pares earlier gains on strong profit-taking interest by individual players despite heavy buying by foreign investors. "The positive trend will likely remain intact going forward, as the market will continue to cling on to hopes for economy-boosting measures for the U.S., which will also ease investors' strong appetite for safe-haven assets," and encourage them to turn to riskier assets instead, says Kim Dong-ha, an analyst at \uc2 Kyobo Securities\uc2 . \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) +1.3% at KRW766,000, helping lift broader market, while securities firms gaining sharply as index firmly breaches strong resistance of 1800, spurs hopes that this may lead to more transactions, boost their bottom lines; \uc2 Daewoo Securities\uc2  (006800.SE) +3.4% at KRW24,100, \uc2 Samsung Securities\uc2  (016360.SE) +4.0% at KRW62,900. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0524 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD unlikely to retreat much from current levels, but will need push to make new highs for year, says NAB \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist John Kyriakopoulos. Notes recent evidence showing China's economy bottoming; reckons this suggests AUD/USD will establish base above 0.9000, not retreat to mid-0.8000s. "However, it will likely take a resumption of expectations for the RBA to hike rates by 50 basis points or more over the six months if AUD/USD is to break above 0.9400 to a fresh high for the year. Alternatively, the restarting of quantitative easing in the U.S has the potential to drive the U.S. dollar lower across the board, which is another potential catalyst to get AUD/USD above 0.9400," says Kyriakopoulos. AUD/USD recently at 0.9221 vs morning low of 0.9206. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0521 GMT [Dow Jones] As Australian PM Gillard gets ready to name her Cabinet, all eyes are on post of foreign affairs minister: Kevin Rudd, Gillard's predecessor, widely tipped to get it. Gillard, who deposed Rudd in June party coup, has already promised him a senior role, and foreign affairs is top of his list. She may have little choice; this could be the only way to ensure Rudd remains loyal to her knife-edge minority government, where she controls the lower house by just one vote. Still, risks undermining her own efforts on international stage. See =FOCUS: Rudd Looms Over Gillard's Ministry Decisions (rachel.pannett@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0511 GMT [Dow Jones] Given recent drop in Australian bonds is largely result of growing rate-hike expectations, could be more weakness in months ahead, says Su-Lin Ong, senior IR strategist with \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 . "The market has been given a little bit of a reality check in the last few weeks with a raft of numbers -- retail sales, GDP and jobs data all highlighting a resilience in the local economy. Policy will need to move into restrictive territory at some point, the question is timing," says Ong. Aussie 3-year last at 95.35, off another 3 ticks on session, down nearly 40 ticks this month. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0503 GMT [Dow Jones] China's August imports of scrap copper, frequently-used cheap substitute of refined copper, shows on-year, on-month rises, due to higher cathode prices last month, says Minmetals Futures analyst Zhuo Guiqiu; adds, average price difference in August at CNY3,000-CNY4,000/ton, enough to attract buying of scrap metal. Says anticipation of strong downstream demand also contributes to more purchases, expecting more scrap imports in September as demand picks up in traditionally high consumption season. China brings in 400,000 tons of scrap copper last month, up 5.2% on-month, up 2.6% on-year, according to Customs data. (yue.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0442 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI flat, down 0.04% at 21,159.30 midday, weighed by declines in China markets (Shanghai Composite down 0.6%), while investors staying cautious amid absence of leads ahead of weekend, says Prudential's associate director Alvin Cheung; "the current levels of the Hang Seng Index are not particularly attractive in terms of valuation." Says HSI now trading at about 12.6X historical P/E; suggests reentry level at around 20,500. Likes China consumption plays given steady growth outlook tipped. ICBC (1398.HK) down 0.9% at HK$5.76 after it signs bancassurance agreement with AIA, while China Mobile (0941.HK) down 1.1% at HK$76.60, extending recent falls after \uc2 Vodafone\uc2 's stake sale. Volume modest at HK$37.84 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0429 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options declined to 11.90%/12.60% vs 11.95%/12.65% overnight in New York, as spot stays in late New York range, reducing hedging demands against fluctuation. Options dealer at major Japan bank says if spot stays around current levels, vols may decline 20 basis points, but further drops unlikely since "too low to exit (downside protections)" for spot. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0423 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei +1.1% at 9196.22, trimming gains from midday close of 9279.81, after decline in Chinese shares on caution ahead of August CPI data due Saturday; worries also spurred by now slightly weakening USD/JPY (84.05) despite written government pledge to carry out \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when necessary. "Although long overdue, there is a growing sense of crisis (among Japanese authorities)," says Toshikazu Horiuchi, equity strategist at Cosmo Securities. "Whether the option is unilateral or not, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is increasingly in a situation where \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot be avoided." Investors turning attention to cues on whether China will carry out rate hike from tomorrow's string of data and outcome of ruling DPJ's presidential election on Tuesday. Puts next week's Nikkei range at 9000-9500. 29/33 Topix subindexes higher. \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  (9983.TO) continues to lead positively-weighted index movers, now up 4.0% at Y11,620. Chipmakers outperforming, with \uc2 Tokyo Electron\uc2  (8035.TO) up 3.2% to Y4,135, Advantest (6857.TO) up 1.9% at Y1,620. Hitachi Construction (6305.TO) up 2.0% at Y1,761 on upbeat FY1H outlook in Nikkei report. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com) [ 09-10-10 0149ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100910e69a0006v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pi