\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Nikkei +1.1%; China CPI, DPJ Election Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2010\par 06:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0423 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei +1.1% at 9196.22, trimming gains from midday close of 9279.81, after decline in Chinese shares on caution ahead of August CPI data due Saturday; worries also spurred by now slightly weakening USD/JPY (84.05) despite written government pledge to carry out \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when necessary. "Although long overdue, there is a growing sense of crisis (among Japanese authorities)," says Toshikazu Horiuchi, equity strategist at Cosmo Securities. "Whether the option is unilateral or not, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is increasingly in a situation where \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot be avoided." Investors turning attention to cues on whether China will carry out rate hike from tomorrow's string of data and outcome of ruling DPJ's presidential election on Tuesday. Puts next week's Nikkei range at 9000-9500. 29/33 Topix subindexes higher. \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  (9983.TO) continues to lead positively-weighted index movers, now up 4.0% at Y11,620. Chipmakers outperforming, with \uc2 Tokyo Electron\uc2  (8035.TO) up 3.2% to Y4,135, Advantest (6857.TO) up 1.9% at Y1,620. Hitachi Construction (6305.TO) up 2.0% at Y1,761 on upbeat FY1H outlook in Nikkei report. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0424 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end down 2.7% at 451.39 vs day's high at 464.05 with investors selling in final minutes of trade, on concerns that banks won't cut interest rates after IMF yesterday said that local banks' cutting of lending rates may be counter-productive. "Sentiment turned negative because players were worried that banks will keep interest rates above 16% for loans issued for stock investment after such (IMF's) comment," says Hanoi-based trader. But adds, correction likely short-lived because buying appetite remains strong after government last week forecast GDP +7.2% in 3Q vs +6.4% in 2Q. Volume moderate with 53 million shares valued at VND1.32 trillion changing hands. Among most active, SSI (SSI.VH) ends down 4.8% at VND27,600, Investment and Trading of Real Estate JSC (ITC.VH) ends down 5.0% at VND24,900, REE (REE.VH) end down 3.5% at VND16,700. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0424 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY sharply lower after PBOC sets dollar-yuan fixing at fresh record low of 6.7625 vs 6.7817 yesterday, largest single-day fall since June 22, when fixing was set 0.0295 lower (from previous session). On OTC, USD/CNY at 6.7698 vs 6.7832 last close; may consolidate around 6.7700; 1-year USD/CNY 6.6850/6.6900 vs 6.6980/6.7008 late yesterday. "Even though the fixing is at a fresh record low, the level is still in line with the broad trend," says Shanghai-based trader at foreign bank. "It appears the fixing is largely affected by other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' strength against the dollar. Still, we don't think the yuan will be heading in one direction. The PBOC's theme of allowing wider two-way trade of the yuan remains." (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0416 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares down midday with banks, property developers leading losses on unabated concerns Beijing would roll out further tightening measures after August trade data came in line with expectations, while August inflation expected to hit new high. Shanghai Composite Index down 0.6% at 2639.53, tipped to stay in 2600-2700 range ahead of August inflation data due tomorrow. "The trade data indicates that China's economy is slowing at a normal, expected pace," says Zhou Xu at Nanjing Securities. August exports +34.4% on-month vs +35.0% expected, imports +35.2% on-month vs +25.0% expected. Dow Jones poll tips August CPI +3.5%, highest in nearly 2 years, vs July's 3.3% increase. ICBC down 0.3% at CNY4.05, Bank of Communications (601328.SH) down 1.2% at CNY5.90. \uc2 China Vanke\uc2  (000002.SZ) down 2.3% at CNY8.09, \uc2 Poly Real Estate Group\uc2  (600048.SH) down 2.1% at CNY11.08. Shenzhen Composite Index down 0.8% at 1169.73. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0415 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD marks fresh intraday low at 1.2642 as Asian hedge funds sell pair, says senior dealer at major Japan bank. Dealer at another major Japan bank says hedge fund's selling triggered stop-loss orders around 1.2660-1.2665. EUR/USD last 1.2662.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD rangebound though moving to low end of range following release of weaker-than-expected Chinese trade data, says ANZ \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager Murray Hindley; "The weaker Chinese trade data saw a softer euro and softer commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." Customs data showed China's trade surplus in August narrowed sharply to $20.03 billion vs $28.7 billion surplus in July, below $30.0 billion economists had expected. Pair last 0.7230 vs 0.7251 early this morning. Adds, plenty of local data next week to give NZD/USD direction with Food Price Index, Retail Trade, RBNZ policy statement all on agenda. Tips support at 0.7210 with resistance at last night's high of 0.7280. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0407 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ shares heading to positive close as investors cheered by fairly positive close on Wall Street; benchmark index last +0.4% at 3163.75. "It's been a comfortable day on the market," says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker James Porteous; notes index bolstered by 1.5% gain in Telecom (TEL.NZ) to NZ$2.05 on some bargain-hunting after stock's early dip to NZ$1.96. The Warehouse (WHS.NZ) +0.3% at NZ$3.70 after FY result largely in line with expectations. Says overall, more buying interest in market as investors look at alternative places to put their money as long-term interest rates are dropping; "people are starting to focus on the yield they can get out of the market." (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0405 GMT [Dow Jones] MOF sells Y2.192 trillion of 0.4% 5-year JGBs with lowest price of 100.20, yielding 0.359%. Bid-to-cover ratio deteriorates to 2.79 from 4.68, while tail lengthens to 0.04 vs 0.01 previous tender. Lead JGB futures down 0.30 at 141.32. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0352 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore USD/CNY NDFs down significantly after PBOC set USD/CNY fixing at fresh record low, in line with USD/CNY onshore spot rate move; 1-year NDF at 6.6864/6.6914 vs 6.6980/6.7008 late yesterday; tipped in 6.68000-6.6900 range for session. Hong Kong-based trader at European bank says record low fixing could be Beijing's good-will gesture to U.S., after recent visits of U.S. economic official Larry Summers and former president Jimmy Carter. "Lately, I think the U.S-China relationship has been improving, and the low fixing is indicative of that," trader says; adds U.S. midterm elections might be factor to push USD/CNY level lower in near term. Still, he notes Chinese companies' offshore units currently buying USD/CNY NDF, providing USD support. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0346 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD down as major U.K. bank sells after lower USD/CNY fixing, says senior trader at local bank. Tips pair in 7.7650-7.7710 band near term. "I've spotted a major U.K. bank selling the U.S. dollar at the 7.7700 level, dragging the pair down to near 7.7670, with the total amount of sales over US$300 million. But other banks buy back on bargain-hunting, pushing the pair up a bit," trader says. Adds, local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market more volatile today on thinner trading volume as Singapore markets closed today. Pair last at 7.7682 vs 7.7717 yesterday.(chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0339 GMT [Dow Jones] Front-month October Nymex crude remains positive, while other oil futures extend overnight losses despite broad gains in Asian shares; analysts wondering about cause of rise in October crude, but many think driver is arbitrage selling to take advantage of spreads with later months; fear of hurricanes may support market around current levels, but "not strong enough to give it a further boost," Nihon Unicom analyst Hiroyuki Kikukawa says. October Nymex crude +51 cents at $74.76 a barrel on Globex. (mari.iwata@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0339 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +0.1% at 922.32 in active trade. Sharp THB rise puts investors in cautious mood with focus on whether \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  would impose any measures to stem local unit's rise. "It's unlikely that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would impose its capital control measure again (after it did that in 2006) but the market is expecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may do something," says Far East Securities' analyst Weerachai Krongsamsri. Adds, downside likely limited as retail investors had cashed in profit, ready to return to bargain-hunt while inflows to Asia may continue as risk over U.S., European economies remain. Support tipped at 915, resistance at 930. Among top actives, PTT (PTT.TH) +0.4% at THB286, TMB Bank (TMB.TH) +1.7% at THB2.44, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) +0.8% at THB120.50, Banpu (BANPU.TH) flat at THB650, \uc2 Krung Thai Bank\uc2  (KTB.TH) down 0.7% at THB14.80. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com) [ 09-10-10 0025ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100910e69a0006c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
