\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:Kospi +1.2%;Breaches 1800 Cap On Wall Street Gains\b0\par\par\uc2 1,643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2010\par 02:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi last +1.2% at 1804.96, finally breaches months-long 1800 firm resistance on U.S. stocks' gains overnight, spurring foreign, institutional investors' buying in local shares. "All sectors across the board are rising today, helping the Kospi to rise strongly," says \uc2 SK Securities\uc2 ' analyst Won Jong-hyuck. "On the back of abundant liquidity and hopes of economic stimulus measures from the Obama administration in the U.S., the Kospi may even rise to 1850 in September," says \uc2 Korea Investment & Securities\uc2 ' Park So-yeon. Kospi tipped in 1795-1810 range today. Blue-chip stocks lifting broader market, with \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) +0.9% at KRW763,000, \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) +0.7% at KRW152,000, KB Financial (105560.SE) +1.4% at KRW49,200. KT (030200.SE) also up 1.5% at KRW42,950 on positive analysts' reports about its earnings outlook as telecom firm rolls out Apple's iPhone 4 on local market today.(jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0042 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY rises to touch 84.13 after stop-loss orders around 84.05-84.10 set by short-term speculators triggered, says senior dealer at major Japan bank. Adds, Japan importers' USD buy orders for their regular settlement might have fueled pair's rise. Says pair may rise to 84.50 in global day if another chunk of stop-loss orders around 84.30 triggered. However, he notes, rises above 84.60 unlikely because Japan exporters' USD sells expected above that level. Pair last 84.07. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0040 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY may consolidate with upward bias on slightly higher dollar index overnight. On OTC, pair tipped to trade between 6.7800-6.7900 range vs 6.7832 close yesterday. "There could be some dollar demand in the morning, and then afternoon, some profit-taking activity could surface, resembling recent sessions," says Shanghai-based trader at foreign bank. Fixing tipped between 6.7700-6.8000 vs 6.7817 yesterday. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0037 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei +1.2% at 9204.01 as weaker yen (USD/JPY now 84.08) lifts high-tech and auto exporters. Reaction limited to widely-anticipated revision of Japan's April-June annualized GDP to +1.5 vs preliminary +0.4%. "There is caution that a series of upcoming Chinese data (including trade, CPI, industrial output) may turn out disappointing so investors are likely to take a wait-and-see attitude after initial buying in the morning," says Hideyuki Ishiguro, strategist at \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2 . Puts today's Nikkei range at 9050-9200. 27/33 Topix subindexes higher; Canon (7751.TO) up 3.8% at Y3,685 after announcing share buy back plan. \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) up 1.0% at Y2,960. Banking shares underperform after Incubator Bank of Japan reportedly filed for bankruptcy; MUFG (8306.TO) up 0.5% at Y404, \uc2 Shinsei Bank\uc2  (8303.TO) down 3.0% at Y64, \uc2 Chiba Bank\uc2  (8337.TO) off 1.0% at Y502. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0037 GMT [Dow Jones] Front-month October Nymex crude rises, while most other crude futures slip, extending overnight losses in New York. "It is perhaps because of arbitrage trading between front month and later contracts," says Tomokazu Amano, analyst with \uc2 Mitsubishi Corporation\uc2  Futures. Adds, this is one-off arb opportunity as contango was quite wide; October-December price differential last around $2.20/bbl, 80 cents narrower than at overnight settlement. Amano says overall sentiment remains weak on still-high U.S. oil inventories; equity prices likely to set market tone today. October Nymex crude up 56 cents at $74.81/bbl on Globex. (mari.iwata@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0034 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand's lower-than-expected growth in 2Q terms of trade data suggest recovery in export incomes may be more subdued than RBNZ factored in, says ASB economist Jane Turner; "Today's data suggest the terms of trade may not even return to 2008 levels, and suggest strong downside risk to the RBNZ's growth forecasts made in June." Says, reinforces view RBNZ will keep OCR unchanged for rest of the year. Adds, export sector has benefited from ongoing recovery in trading partner growth, export volumes continued to rise in 2Q; "The net export contribution to 2Q GDP is likely to be a touch stronger than we had previously forecast in our 2Q GDP calculations" due to ongoing recovery in trading partners. Says remains circumspect on future prospects of export growth, given recent doubts on strength of global recovery. NZ 2Q merchandise terms of trade index +2.1% on-quarter vs 1Q's revised +6.1%, +3.5% forecast. See: UPDATE: NZ 2Q Terms Of Trade +2.1% Vs 1Q; Market Expected +3.5% (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0032 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW tad lower at 1,165.7 vs 1,167.4 late yesterday in Seoul on Kospi's 1.0% gain; pair testing 1,165 support in early trade, whether it gives way could be key for KRW trade today, traders say. Add players likely to grow increasingly cautious over possible USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities at levels below KRW1,165. "The euro remains wobbly, but the won doesn't seem to be responding as sensitively to euro moves as before," says local bank trader. Adds "despite BOK's rate freeze decision yesterday, offshore players seem to hold a generally bullish view on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," including KRW. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0031 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs falling as investors taking cue from weak U.S. Treasurys, positive local shares (Nikkei last +0.9%); but trade likely rangebound as investors await outcome of DPJ-leadership race next week, with winner--current premier Naoto Kan or influential politician Ichiro Ozawa-- likely to be next prime minister, says Mizuho Securities chief market analyst Tetsuya Miura. Adds, investors to pay attention to 5-year JGB tender later; if results strong, then should provide some support to JGB market, he says. Lead December futures last at 141.43, down 0.19. Miura tips 141.35-141.62 range. 10-year yield not traded yet, tipped in 1.120%-1.150% range.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0013 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ 2Q terms of trade data +2.1%, but net exports likely to make negative contribution to 2Q GDP, says ANZ economist Mark Smith; adds data "was highlighting the terms of trading were continuing to provide support to the New Zealand economy and we expect this to continue going forward." Says given recent events, monetary policy implications of 2Q GDP data limited. Notes on negative side, capital imports, excluding transport, very weak; "the implication of that is businesses aren't really investing a lot and that could really weigh on the recovery next year." Adds going forward, key for RBNZ remains extent to which producers use proceeds to repair balance sheets, as opposed to use for spending. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0006 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ market drifting lower in very tepid trade, last down 0.2% at 3146.42; "I am a little bit surprised the market is down, given the tone of international markets," says \uc2 UBS\uc2 's NZ managing director Campbell Stuart. Index largely weighed by 1.5% fall in Telecom (TEL.NZ) to NZ$1.99 as market disappointed after government yesterday announced Telecom one of 14 companies short-listed for NZ$3 billion ultra-fast broadband initiative but not in top 3 prioritized for negotiation. Warehouse Group (WHS.NZ) flat at NZ$3.60, Stuart says company's lower FY profit largely in-line with expectations with trading conditions remaining challenging. Given dearth of volume, says difficult to predict if market will move higher. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0000 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 should stay quiet before the weekend, barring surprise from China's August trade data, due Friday. IG Markets tips 0.5% opening gain to 4604, with financials expected to push the market higher in early trading after U.S. financials led gains on Wall Street. Charts also favor gains, with recent buy signals on daily and weekly MACD trend indicators. Inverse head & shoulders pattern will give potential target of 5050 on a sustained break above neckline resistance at 4588, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. However, unless the China trade data surprise on the upside, S&P/ASX 200 may lack catalysts for sustained gains today, with quiet trading likely to be accentuated by holidays in much of Asia. Reports of potential EUR9 billion capital raising by \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  could encourage profit taking before the weekend. Index last 4582.2. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2359 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may rise to 84.30 in Asian morning if pair breaks 84.05, triggers stop-loss orders around that level, says senior dealer at major Japan trust bank; Takeshi Yasukawa, senior dealer at \uc2 Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , notes some market participants expect an "overwhelming amount of USD buy orders" coming in from Japan importers for their settlement. Meanwhile, trust bank dealer says downside limited to around 83.50 as investors cautious about possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities; pair last 83.97. EUR biased down as Europe sovereign risk still a lingering concern. EUR/JPY may trade in 106.00-107.00 band vs 106.50 last; EUR/USD may trade in 1.2650-1.2730 vs 1.2684 last. Says markets to seek cues on risk appetite from performance of Asian shares. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com) [ 09-09-10 2044ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100910e69a0000h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
