nventories; equity prices likely to set market tone today. October Nymex crude up 56 cents at $74.81/bbl on Globex. (mari.iwata@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0034 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand's lower-than-expected growth in 2Q terms of trade data suggest recovery in export incomes may be more subdued than RBNZ factored in, says ASB economist Jane Turner; "Today's data suggest the terms of trade may not even return to 2008 levels, and suggest strong downside risk to the RBNZ's growth forecasts made in June." Says, reinforces view RBNZ will keep OCR unchanged for rest of the year. Adds, export sector has benefited from ongoing recovery in trading partner growth, export volumes continued to rise in 2Q; "The net export contribution to 2Q GDP is likely to be a touch stronger than we had previously forecast in our 2Q GDP calculations" due to ongoing recovery in trading partners. Says remains circumspect on future prospects of export growth, given recent doubts on strength of global recovery. NZ 2Q merchandise terms of trade index +2.1% on-quarter vs 1Q's revised +6.1%, +3.5% forecast. See: UPDATE: NZ 2Q Terms Of Trade +2.1% Vs 1Q; Market Expected +3.5% (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0032 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW tad lower at 1,165.7 vs 1,167.4 late yesterday in Seoul on Kospi's 1.0% gain; pair testing 1,165 support in early trade, whether it gives way could be key for KRW trade today, traders say. Add players likely to grow increasingly cautious over possible USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities at levels below KRW1,165. "The euro remains wobbly, but the won doesn't seem to be responding as sensitively to euro moves as before," says local bank trader. Adds "despite BOK's rate freeze decision yesterday, offshore players seem to hold a generally bullish view on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," including KRW. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0031 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs falling as investors taking cue from weak U.S. Treasurys, positive local shares (Nikkei last +0.9%); but trade likely rangebound as investors await outcome of DPJ-leadership race next week, with winner--current premier Naoto Kan or influential politician Ichiro Ozawa-- likely to be next prime minister, says Mizuho Securities chief market analyst Tetsuya Miura. Adds, investors to pay attention to 5-year JGB tender later; if results strong, then should provide some support to JGB market, he says. Lead December futures last at 141.43, down 0.19. Miura tips 141.35-141.62 range. 10-year yield not traded yet, tipped in 1.120%-1.150% range.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0013 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ 2Q terms of trade data +2.1%, but net exports likely to make negative contribution to 2Q GDP, says ANZ economist Mark Smith; adds data "was highlighting the terms of trading were continuing to provide support to the New Zealand economy and we expect this to continue going forward." Says given recent events, monetary policy implications of 2Q GDP data limited. Notes on negative side, capital imports, excluding transport, very weak; "the implication of that is businesses aren't really investing a lot and that could really weigh on the recovery next year." Adds going forward, key for RBNZ remains extent to which producers use proceeds to repair balance sheets, as opposed to use for spending. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0006 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ market drifting lower in very tepid trade, last down 0.2% at 3146.42; "I am a little bit surprised the market is down, given the tone of international markets," says \uc2 UBS\uc2 's NZ managing director Campbell Stuart. Index largely weighed by 1.5% fall in Telecom (TEL.NZ) to NZ$1.99 as market disappointed after government yesterday announced Telecom one of 14 companies short-listed for NZ$3 billion ultra-fast broadband initiative but not in top 3 prioritized for negotiation. Warehouse Group (WHS.NZ) flat at NZ$3.60, Stuart says company's lower FY profit largely in-line with expectations with trading conditions remaining challenging. Given dearth of volume, says difficult to predict if market will move higher. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0000 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 should stay quiet before the weekend, barring surprise from China's August trade data, due Friday. IG Markets tips 0.5% opening gain to 4604, with financials expected to push the market higher in early trading after U.S. financials led gains on Wall Street. Charts also favor gains, with recent buy signals on daily and weekly MACD trend indicators. Inverse head & shoulders pattern will give potential target of 5050 on a sustained break above neckline resistance at 4588, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. However, unless the China trade data surprise on the upside, S&P/ASX 200 may lack catalysts for sustained gains today, with quiet trading likely to be accentuated by holidays in much of Asia. Reports of potential EUR9 billion capital raising by \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  could encourage profit taking before the weekend. Index last 4582.2. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2359 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may rise to 84.30 in Asian morning if pair breaks 84.05, triggers stop-loss orders around that level, says senior dealer at major Japan trust bank; Takeshi Yasukawa, senior dealer at \uc2 Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , notes some market participants expect an "overwhelming amount of USD buy orders" coming in from Japan importers for their settlement. Meanwhile, trust bank dealer says downside limited to around 83.50 as investors cautious about possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities; pair last 83.97. EUR biased down as Europe sovereign risk still a lingering concern. EUR/JPY may trade in 106.00-107.00 band vs 106.50 last; EUR/USD may trade in 1.2650-1.2730 vs 1.2684 last. Says markets to seek cues on risk appetite from performance of Asian shares. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2354 GMT [Dow Jones] Nervousness remains in base metals markets after sharp selloff yesterday on speculation of probe by Chinese authorities into rubber market positions, says Triland Metals, which noted that some ground recovered during the day was given up again towards the close of trading, with zinc losing most but copper also affected. As for aluminum, which also fell overnight, "we are likely going through a temporary correction with buying interest expected to re-emerge soon," Triland says in daily note. LME 3-month copper fell $119 on day to $7,555/metric ton, aluminum tanked $55 at $2,105, lead lost $33 at $2,201, zinc down $66 at $2,153, nickel declined $150 to $22,750, while tin bucked trend, climbing $25 at $21,700. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2344 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi likely to sustain rise after closing up 0.3% at 1784.36 yesterday, helped by gains in U.S. stocks, likely foreign inflows following improved sentiment, analysts say. "Kospi is likely to test 1800 today as worries over a possible double-dip recession are largely subdued," as underscored by above-consensus U.S. economic data, BOK governor's comments yesterday, says MiraeAsset Securities analyst Lee Jin-woo. Foreigners also expected to return to net buying, especially in techs following recent weakness, mainly on concerns over global economic health. Kospi tipped in 1770-1800 range. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2342 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW may rise vs 1,167.4 late Thursday in Seoul, taking breather ahead of weekend after slipping to 1-month low yesterday on active USD selling by offshore players, analysts say. With pair's breach below the lower-end of recent trading ranges at 1,170 yesterday, next key support now at 1,160 says Samsung Futures analyst Jeon Seung-ji. But given still-unstable overseas financial market conditions, potential USD-buying related to M&A deals, and chance of USD-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities, "there's a good chance," that pair will recover to the 1,170 support, she adds. Tips pair in 1,165-1,175 range for day. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com) [ 09-09-10 2039ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100910e69a0000e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
