ittle bit surprised the market is down, given the tone of international markets," says \uc2 UBS\uc2 's NZ managing director Campbell Stuart. Index largely weighed by 1.5% fall in Telecom (TEL.NZ) to NZ$1.99 as market disappointed after government yesterday announced Telecom one of 14 companies short-listed for NZ$3 billion ultra-fast broadband initiative but not in top 3 prioritized for negotiation. Warehouse Group (WHS.NZ) flat at NZ$3.60, Stuart says company's lower FY profit largely in-line with expectations with trading conditions remaining challenging. Given dearth of volume, says difficult to predict if market will move higher. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0000 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 should stay quiet before the weekend, barring surprise from China's August trade data, due Friday. IG Markets tips 0.5% opening gain to 4604, with financials expected to push the market higher in early trading after U.S. financials led gains on Wall Street. Charts also favor gains, with recent buy signals on daily and weekly MACD trend indicators. Inverse head & shoulders pattern will give potential target of 5050 on a sustained break above neckline resistance at 4588, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. However, unless the China trade data surprise on the upside, S&P/ASX 200 may lack catalysts for sustained gains today, with quiet trading likely to be accentuated by holidays in much of Asia. Reports of potential EUR9 billion capital raising by \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  could encourage profit taking before the weekend. Index last 4582.2. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2359 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may rise to 84.30 in Asian morning if pair breaks 84.05, triggers stop-loss orders around that level, says senior dealer at major Japan trust bank; Takeshi Yasukawa, senior dealer at \uc2 Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , notes some market participants expect an "overwhelming amount of USD buy orders" coming in from Japan importers for their settlement. Meanwhile, trust bank dealer says downside limited to around 83.50 as investors cautious about possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities; pair last 83.97. EUR biased down as Europe sovereign risk still a lingering concern. EUR/JPY may trade in 106.00-107.00 band vs 106.50 last; EUR/USD may trade in 1.2650-1.2730 vs 1.2684 last. Says markets to seek cues on risk appetite from performance of Asian shares. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2354 GMT [Dow Jones] Nervousness remains in base metals markets after sharp selloff yesterday on speculation of probe by Chinese authorities into rubber market positions, says Triland Metals, which noted that some ground recovered during the day was given up again towards the close of trading, with zinc losing most but copper also affected. As for aluminum, which also fell overnight, "we are likely going through a temporary correction with buying interest expected to re-emerge soon," Triland says in daily note. LME 3-month copper fell $119 on day to $7,555/metric ton, aluminum tanked $55 at $2,105, lead lost $33 at $2,201, zinc down $66 at $2,153, nickel declined $150 to $22,750, while tin bucked trend, climbing $25 at $21,700. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2344 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi likely to sustain rise after closing up 0.3% at 1784.36 yesterday, helped by gains in U.S. stocks, likely foreign inflows following improved sentiment, analysts say. "Kospi is likely to test 1800 today as worries over a possible double-dip recession are largely subdued," as underscored by above-consensus U.S. economic data, BOK governor's comments yesterday, says MiraeAsset Securities analyst Lee Jin-woo. Foreigners also expected to return to net buying, especially in techs following recent weakness, mainly on concerns over global economic health. Kospi tipped in 1770-1800 range. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2342 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW may rise vs 1,167.4 late Thursday in Seoul, taking breather ahead of weekend after slipping to 1-month low yesterday on active USD selling by offshore players, analysts say. With pair's breach below the lower-end of recent trading ranges at 1,170 yesterday, next key support now at 1,160 says Samsung Futures analyst Jeon Seung-ji. But given still-unstable overseas financial market conditions, potential USD-buying related to M&A deals, and chance of USD-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities, "there's a good chance," that pair will recover to the 1,170 support, she adds. Tips pair in 1,165-1,175 range for day. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2337 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume +9.7% at HK$4.492 billion yesterday, as percentage of total market volume higher than recent averages at 7.3% but amount remain dominated by China Mobile (0941.HK), thus investors perhaps should avoid reading too much into higher ratio. China Mobile led with HK$1.55 billion, 34.6% of total amount shorted, as near-term pressure remains after \uc2 Vodafone\uc2 's stake disposal. Agricultural Bank of China (1288.HK) in second place with HK$264.35 million, then HKEx (0388.HK) with HK$152.10 million, A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) with HK$133.31 million, Hutchison (0013.HK) with HK$127.70 million. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2335 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian net retail fund flows of A$1 billion across the market for the June quarter were disappointing, \uc2 UBS\uc2  analysts say, with super flows of A$2.2 billion well down on the A$4.6 billion recorded for the same quarter a year ago, and outflows in investment funds were more than A$1.1 billion. "The combination of falling equities, deteriorating sentiment and ongoing regulatory uncertainty clearly impacted the quarter's flows, the worst we've seen for a long time," \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. Notes with flows providing little counter to a near 12% fall in Australian equities, funds under management fell by 5% and is now back below September 2009 levels. (lyndal.mcfarland@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2333 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. 10-year Treasury yield may rise due to eased risk-aversion as European sovereign bond problems look to be stabilizing, says CIBC World Markets Japan executive director Kazuaki Oh'E. Tips yield to trade in 2.73%-2.78% range vs 2.755% last. Adds, for outlook, investors will keep paying attention to U.S. economic data, also whether Obama administration's stimulus package will pass Congress, he says.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2330 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to consolidate with risks skewed to upside after hitting 3-week high $75.96/bbl yesterday, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics in bullish mode; 5-day moving average above 15-day moving average and rising. Breach of $75.96 resistance would target $76.10 (Aug. 19 high), then $76.30 (confluence of 55-day & 100-day moving averages), $76.63 (Aug. 17 high), $76.86 (50% Fibonacci correction of $82.97-$70.76 Aug. 4-Aug. 25 decline) and $77.47 (200-day moving average). Support at $73.88 (yesterday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, exposing downside to $73.37 (Wednesday's low), then $72.63 (Tuesday's reaction low), $71.53 (Aug. 31 reaction low) and $70.76 (Aug. 25 trough). October crude last up 62 cents at $74.87/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com) [ 09-09-10 2031ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100910e69a00009\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
