\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-POLL-Ozawa win would boost stocks short term, hit JGBs, yen\b0\par\par\uc2 997 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2010\par 06:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Repeats to attach text to alerts)\par \par \uc2  * Ozawa seen favouring econ stimulus over fiscal discipline\par \par \uc2  * Kan, slightly ahead in race, seen continuing current policy\par \par \uc2  * Ozawa seen aiding stocks, weakening yen near-term\par \par \uc2  * Ozawa's long-term impact in doubt due to old-style ways\par \par \uc2  By Yoshiyasu Shida\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 10 (Reuters) - A win by Ichiro Ozawa in a party vote next week that will decide who is prime minister would likely give a short-term boost to stocks while weakening Japanese government bonds and the yen, a Reuters poll showed on Friday.\par \par \uc2  Many market participants fear, however, that Ozawa's old-style policies could leave Japan no better off once any initial boost wears off, with more than two-thirds of poll respondents saying he would be neutral or negative for the economy over the long haul.\par \par \uc2  "Strong arm policies may be welcomed by the markets short-term but in the longer term the preservation of the old economic and political set-up may only serve to strengthen the sense of hopelessness," said Masamichi Adachi, senior economist at JP Morgan Securities Japan.\par \par \uc2  Surveys give Prime Minister Naoto Kan only a slight edge over Ozawa ahead of the Sept. 14 vote to lead the Democratic Party of Japan (DPJ), in a showdown that could trigger a shift in government fiscal priorities but may also spell policy confusion.\par \par \uc2  Financial markets have been bracing for a possible victory by Ozawa, who has said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken the yen and consider issuing more bonds to boost the economy, drawing a contrast with Kan's efforts to rein in the country's massive public debt.\par \par \uc2  In a poll of 54 economists, analysts, traders and fund managers this week, the majority of respondents said an Ozawa win would provide a short-term lift to the stock market while driving up long-term bond yields and pressuring the yen.\par \par \uc2  At the same time many warned an Ozawa government would not address the deep structural issues facing the Japanese economy, making any market impact short-lived.\par \par \uc2  "He will take steps to stimulate the economy and give that priority over fiscal discipline," said Susumu Kato, chief Japan economist at \uc2 Credit Agricole Corporate and Investment Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  "At the same time he is a skilful politician and there is a very good chance he'll be kind to the stock market."\par \par \uc2  The winner of the vote will almost certainly become premier by virtue of the ruling party's majority in parliament's powerful lower house, taking the helm with an economy on the ropes due to slowing overseas demand and the yen's surge to a 15-year high on the dollar, which makes exporters less competitive.\par \par \uc2  <^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par \par \uc2  Graphics on the Reuters poll:  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/daw42p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/daw42p}}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/baw42p"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/baw42p}}}\uc2 \f2 \par  PDF on DPJ leadership race:       \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/tad39n"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/tad39n}}}\uc2 \f2 \par  PDF on the yen's rise             \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/nef47n"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/nef47n}}}\uc2 \f2 \par  For more stories on Japanese politics:            [ID:nPOLJP]\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^>\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  LONG-TERM PAIN?\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  Markets have already started to price in the prospect of a\par new prime minister, with the benchmark 10-year yield\par <JP10YTN=JBTC> touching a 2-\u189\'00\'BD   month high of 1.195 percent early\par this week. The Nikkei stock average <.N225> is up nearly 5\par percent since Ozawa said he would run on Aug. 31.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  But the Reuters poll suggests an Ozawa victory is far from\par being factored in. Just over half of respondents, or 28, said\par they thought Kan would win, while 18 are betting on Ozawa and\par eight said it was too close to call.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  The poll showed a strong consensus that an Ozawa government\par would help to weaken the yen, with some 60 percent of market\par players predicting a short-term drop in the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par Only five said the yen would rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  Many respondents also said ramped-up government spending\par under Ozawa would push the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to take aggressive\par actions of its own, putting downward pressure on the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  "Compared with the current leadership, the perceived threat\par of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would increase," said Takumi Tsunoda, economist\par at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shinkin \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Research.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  "If fiscal expansion doesn't lead to economic growth then\par monetary easing would be the only option left," said a fund\par manager at a Japanese asset management firm.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  Roughly half of all respondents said Kan keeping his job\par would be neutral for stocks, bonds and the yen, since he is still\par seen likely to win the contest and would not likely revise his\par current policy course.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  But of those market players who predicted an impact, an\par overwhelming number believe a Kan government would be bad for\par shares, at 24 respondents, push bond yields lower, at 30\par respondents, and pave the way for further gains in the yen, at 23\par respondents.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  Kan has vowed to cap new bond issuance for the fiscal year\par from April at this year's level of around 44 trillion yen to rein\par in public debt, which at about twice the size of the $5 trillion\par economy is the worst among developed countries.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  Kan has also called for a future debate of a possible sales\par tax hike, which contributed to the Democrats' defeat in the upper\par house election in July and has forced the party to seek\par opposition help to pass bills through parliament.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  "A contractionary fiscal policy and policy paralysis," said\par Shoji Hirakawa, chief strategist at UBS Securities Japan, on the\par impact of a Kan government on stocks and bonds. "And a step back\par in the response to combat the strong yen."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  Ozawa, once a protege of Kakuei Tanaka, a former premier and\par the architect of Japan's postwar political regime of pork barrel,\par party factions and vested interests, has said he would consider\par issuing new debt if the economy deteriorates.\par  (Editing by Nathan Layne and Edmund Klamann)\par \par \par \par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-POLITICS/POLL (REPEAT)|LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|U|D|M|NAT|G|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|O|OIL   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100910e69a00054\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
