\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,445 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2010\par 13:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OECD: FURTHER STIMULUS MAY BE NEEDED TO COUNTER SLOWDOWN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Central banks may have to provide additional stimulus, and in some case governments may have to delay their plans to cut budget deficits, to counter a slowdown in the global economy during the second half of this year, the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development said Thursday. (published 0933 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OECD PADOAN: HIGHER RISK OF ECONOMIC CONTRACTION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There is an increased risk that some countries will experience another period of economic contraction, the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development's Chief Economist said Thursday. (published 0930 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OECD PADOAN: CENTRAL BANKS MUST NORMALIZE MONETARY POLICY QUICKLY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Central banks should move fast to normalize monetary conditions, even if quantitative easing is useful in the short term, chief economist of Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development said Thursday. (published 1009 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ECB: GOVERNMENTS NEED TO PREPARE CUTTING DEBT BURDEN MORE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro-zone member states should stand ready to implement more measures to reduce their debt burden should the economy grow less than expected, the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said in its monthly bulletin Thursday. (published 0928 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ECB'S MERSCH: STRICTER BANK CAPITAL RULES WILL BENEFIT ECONOMY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stricter capital rules for banks will eventually be beneficial to the global economy as they will induce banks to adopt more prudent behavior, Yves Mersch, governor of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Luxembourg and member of the governing council of the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Thursday. (published 0853 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMANY'S IFW SEES ECONOMY GROWING 3.4% IN '10, 1.7% IN '11 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany's economic recovery will be decisive this year, though the pace of growth is already slowing as foreign demand recedes and will continue at a modest rate in 2011, the Kiel-based Institute for World Economic Studies said in a study Thursday. (published 0951 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PBOC ADVISER: DON'T EXPECT ANY CHANGE TO PROPERTY POLICIES - XINHUA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors shouldn't expect any change in China's tightening policies in the property sector, and China can't change its credit policy currently, People's Bank of China adviser Xia Bin was quoted as saying by the state-run Xinhua News Agency on Thursday. (published 0754 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  NODA KEEPS UP THREAT OF YEN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister kept the threat of yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  alive Thursday, saying the authorities "are conducting various simulations" to curb the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's strength. (published 0839 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BANK OF LUXEMBOURG SEES SUBDUED EURO-ZONE RECOVERY AHEAD \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro zone should experience a subdued recovery in the wake of the worst recession since World War II, in contrast to previous recoveries following financial crises, the Bank of Luxembourg said in its quarterly report published Thursday. (published 0800 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EX-PBOC ADVISER: GOVERNMENT MUST SEEK BALANCE ON YUAN POLICY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China is facing pressures for the yuan to appreciate, but policy makers must "seek a balance among various issues" in relation to the exchange rate, Yu Yongding, a former People's Bank of China adviser and an influential economist, said Thursday. (published 0732 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: GERMAN FINAL AUG CPI UNCH AT 0.0% MM, +1.0% YY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consumer prices in Europe's largest economy remained unchanged on the month in August and rose 1.0% on the year, final figures from the German Federal Statistics Office Destatis showed Thursday, confirming the preliminary reading. (published 0604 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UK CLEGG: SPENDING CUTS WON'T START UNTIL APRIL 2011 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The cuts in U.K. government spending that will be announced on October 20 won't start until April 2011 and will be evenly spread over four years, Deputy Prime Minister Nick Clegg said Thursday. (published 0736 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JUMP IN UK JULY GOODS TRADE DEFICIT MAY DENT GROWTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K.'s goods trade deficit rose to its widest on record as demand for chemicals and oil lifted imports while exports rose only marginally, data showed Thursday. (published 1014 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SPANISH HOME SALES SURGE 24.7% IN 2Q FROM YEAR EARLIER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spanish home sales surged 24.7% in the second quarter from a year earlier, in a sign that the country's slumping housing market is recovering, data from the housing ministry showed Thursday. (published 0741 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FRENCH FIN MIN: VAT RISE HIGHLY PROBABLE FOR TRIPLE PLAY SERVICES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Finance Minister Christine Lagarde told Radio Classique Thursday it is highly probably the country will raise its value-added tax on Internet providers. (published 0742 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EX-PBOC ADVISER: CHINA AGRICULTURE, COMMODITY PRICES BROADLY STABLE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese agriculture and commodity prices have remained broadly stable, and natural disasters won't cause farm product prices to sharply rise, a former adviser to China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Thursday. (published 1006 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: IRISH AUGUST CPI BACK IN POSITIVE TERRITORY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Irish consumer prices rose 0.2% on the year in August, as mortgage interest rate rises helped the index return to positive territory after it started to decline in January 2009. (published 1026 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BANK OF KOREA, IN SURPRISE, STANDS PAT; SIGNALS HIKE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Korea kept interest rates steady Thursday, defying expectations of a rate hike, but signaled it will gradually tighten policy in coming months to keep inflation in check. (published 0520 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par 14 OF 15 ANALYSTS SAY BANK OF KOREA COULD UP RATES IN OCT - POLL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Korea is likely to resume its tightening cycle in October after holding rates steady for two consecutive months to contain inflationary pressures despite higher external risks, analysts said Thursday. (published 0549 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ GOV: MORE JGB BUYING MAY HAVE ADVERSE EFFECTS ON MARKET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa on Thursday reiterated his reluctance to boost the size of its Japanese government bond buying from the market, saying such a move could drive up long-term yields if investors think the BOJ is trying to finance fiscal spending. (published 0222 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN FINANCE MINISTER: PLAN TO CHECK CHINA'S INTENTION BEHIND JGB BUYING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Minister of Finance Yoshihiko Noda said Thursday the government is closely monitoring China's stepped-up buying of Japanese government debt and he will check with Beijing on the reasons behind its move. (published 0140 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN'S BIG COMPANY'S SENTIMENT WORSENS FOR OCT-DEC QUARTER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Large Japanese companies have become cautious in their outlook for the economy and business conditions in the October-December quarter, as concerns grow about the strong yen and a slowing global economy, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  survey showed Thursday. (published 0413 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 :EURO SLIGHTLY DOWN;IRISH T-BILL AUCTION UNDERPINS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro is flat to a little lower Thursday after Ireland's latest T-bill auction proved relatively successful. (published 1002 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MONEY TALKS: RBA HIKE BEFORE YEAR END LOOKS A SOLID BET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sharply increased bets in the market that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  will raise interest rates by Christmas look about right. (published 0755 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: MORE ALL-TIME LOWS FOR EUR/CHF \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: Bear pressure is looking to put support at 1.2675 to the test, which protects the weak recovery off Wednesday's 1.2659 low. However, a break below 1.2659 is required to put bears in control of the near-term and prompt further weakness towards 1.2625 and 1.2588. Regaining ground above 1.2743 would lift the tone and re-open 1.2764, threatening 1.2790. Weekly chart EUR/USD trend: Range. (published 0612 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: GILTS TO LOSE MORE GROUND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday BUND: Significant resistance emerged at 132.14 Wednesday to put support at 130.91 to the test. The wider setback off the Aug. 31 contract high at 133.29 dominates the tone, and a break below 130.91 would bring the Sep. 3 reaction low at 130.24 back into focus. Keeping 130.91 intact would prompt a rally to 131.85, but only above there would re-open the 132.14 high. Weekly chart BUND trend: Range. (published 0625 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FOCUS: FISCAL DISCIPLINE TEST FOR AUSTRALIA'S MINORITY GOVERNMENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's minority government will need to operate within tight fiscal guidelines if it's to meet ambitious budgetary surplus plans while at the same time doling out sweeteners to lawmakers on whose support it depends. (published 2355 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PREVIEW: CHINA AUGUST CPI LIKELY HIGHEST SINCE OCTOBER 2008 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's consumer price index likely rose at its fastest rate in nearly two years in August as floods and unusually hot weather for the month pushed up food prices, but economists expect the increases in the CPI to moderate in coming months as weather conditions return to normal. (published 0100 GMT) [ 09-09-10 1101GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100909e6990013z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
