\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Noda Keeps Up Threat Of Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 816 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2010\par 10:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details on Prime Minister Kan's policy instructions made in the meeting of the government's new economic panel.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister kept the threat of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alive Thursday, saying the authorities "are conducting various simulations" to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength.\par \par \uc2 Yoshihiko Noda also repeated his pledge to take "decisive steps" in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when it becomes necessary.\par \par \uc2 He made the remarks in Parliament, shortly after attending a new, high-level meeting of policy makers and private-sector experts, where he said the yen was a topic of discussion. The panel, which includes Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa, was launched Thursday by Prime Minister Naoto Kan, and will meet regularly to coordinate and implement policies more effectively to try to put Japan's economy back on track to long-term health.\par \par \uc2 How to deal with the yen's rise to 15-year highs against the dollar is a major policy challenge facing Tokyo. Exporters say they are losing out to rivals overseas and are increasingly looking to relocate some facilities to other countries to cut costs. While the strong yen makes imported commodities and other goods cheaper, a plus for many companies and consumers, downward pressure on prices makes it more difficult to eradicate deflation that has weighed on the economy for years.\par \par \uc2 "Some members from the industrial sector made remarks about the strong yen," said Satoshi Arai, minister of state for economic and fiscal policy, at a press conference after the panel's meeting. He didn't go into detail but said indicated there was no specific discussion about measures to deal with the surging local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yen has soared in recent months, driven by weakness in the dollar on speculation the U.S. Federal Reserve may ease monetary policy further and an apparent rise in capital flows into Japan, some from China. Chinese investors bought a net Y583.0 billion of Japanese financial assets in July, mostly short-term debt, stepping up their purchases from Y456.7 billion in June, Finance Ministry data showed Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The timing of China's yen-asset purchases, which analysts say reflect Beijing's efforts to diversify its massive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, appears to be troubling Tokyo. Despite repeated warnings from the Japanese government that it would conduct yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar has continued to slump, threatening to snuff out a weak recovery in its economy. Any additional demand for yen only complicates Tokyo's plight.\par \par \uc2 Noda said the government is closely monitoring China's buying of Japanese government debt and he will check with Beijing on the reasons behind its move.\par \par \uc2 "We are paying close attention" to recent increases in Chinese purchases of JGBs, Noda said during a session of the upper house financial affairs committee.\par \par \uc2 "I don't know the true intention" of China regarding its growing appetite for JGBs, but Tokyo plans to "closely cooperate (with Beijing) and examine its intention."\par \par \uc2 At the meeting of the new government panel, Prime Minister Kan didn't call for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  steps but he instructed its members to reach a conclusion by the end of December on whether to lower Japan's 40% effective corporate tax rate from the next fiscal year starting April. The rate is considered higher than most other advanced nations, putting Japanese companies at a disadvantage.\par \par \uc2 Kan also kept pressure on the BOJ. The government views that "its most important task for the time being, in terms of macroeconomic policy, will be to bring an end to deflation, and it will work with the Bank of Japan as one to make strong, comprehensive policy efforts," he said.\par \par \uc2 Separately, BOJ Gov. Shirakawa reiterated his reluctance to boost the size of its Japanese government bond buying from the market, saying such a move could drive up long-term yields if investors think the BOJ is trying to finance fiscal spending.\par \par \uc2 If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decides to buy more JGBs, "that may be misinterpreted as a sign that the BOJ finances public debt or tries to control long-term yields, which could cause adverse effects," Shirakawa said in Parliament. "If such skepticism grows, Japan's long-term interest rates could rise, which in turn may drag down economic activities."\par \par \uc2 Shirakawa added that abolishing the so-called banknote rule which limits the BOJ's JGB buying to the amount of banknotes in circulation could push the nation's long-term interest rates higher, indicating that the BOJ will stick to internal regulations on monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ now buys Y1.8 trillion of JGBs each month and Y21.6 trillion annually via outright \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Takashi Mochizuki and Tomoyuki Tachikawa contributed to this article.) [ 09-09-10 0839GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100909e699000un\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw18