\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 950 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2010\par 09:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN NODA: CONDUCTING VARIOUS SIMULATIONS ON \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ACTION \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister kept the threat of yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  alive Thursday, saying the authorities "are conducting various simulations" to curb the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's strength. (published 0636 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: GERMAN FINAL AUG CPI UNCH AT 0.0% MM, +1.0% YY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consumer prices in Europe's largest economy remained unchanged on the month in August and rose 1.0% on the year, final figures from the German Federal Statistics Office Destatis showed Thursday, confirming the preliminary reading. (published 0604 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BANK OF KOREA, IN SURPRISE, STANDS PAT; SIGNALS HIKE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Korea kept interest rates steady Thursday, defying expectations of a rate hike, but signaled it will gradually tighten policy in coming months to keep inflation in check. (published 0520 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par 14 OF 15 ANALYSTS SAY BANK OF KOREA COULD UP RATES IN OCT - POLL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Korea is likely to resume its tightening cycle in October after holding rates steady for two consecutive months to contain inflationary pressures despite higher external risks, analysts said Thursday. (published 0549 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSSIE JOB MARKET ON FIRE, UPS CHANCE OF NOV. HIKE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's job market keeps growing, adding 30,900 jobs in August, driving the unemployment rate down to 5.1% and putting the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  on notice that it may need to resume hiking interest rates soon. (published 0457 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MAS TO \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IF US DOLLAR STAYS BELOW S$1.34010-SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is poised to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, selling the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for the second time, this month if the U.S. dollar trades "consistently" below S$1.3410, a person familiar with the situation said Thursday. (published 0508 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ GOV: MORE JGB BUYING MAY HAVE ADVERSE EFFECTS ON MARKET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa on Thursday reiterated his reluctance to boost the size of its Japanese government bond buying from the market, saying such a move could drive up long-term yields if investors think the BOJ is trying to finance fiscal spending. (published 0222 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN FINANCE MINISTER: PLAN TO CHECK CHINA'S INTENTION BEHIND JGB BUYING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Minister of Finance Yoshihiko Noda said Thursday the government is closely monitoring China's stepped-up buying of Japanese government debt and he will check with Beijing on the reasons behind its move. (published 0140 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN'S BIG COMPANY'S SENTIMENT WORSENS FOR OCT-DEC QUARTER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Large Japanese companies have become cautious in their outlook for the economy and business conditions in the October-December quarter, as concerns grow about the strong yen and a slowing global economy, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  survey showed Thursday. (published 0413 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PHILIPPINES JULY EXPORTS SURPRISE WITH 36% GROWTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Philippine July exports surprised on the upside, rising 36% from a year earlier and extending a recovery that started last November on the back of a rebound in electronic shipments--a development that bodes well for sustained robust growth of the domestic economy, at least into the third quarter. (published 0347 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: MALAYSIA JULY INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT UP 3.2% ON YR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malaysia's industrial output expanded 3.2% in July from a year earlier because of increases in manufacturing and electricity output, official data released Thursday show. (published 0401 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RBA'S DEBELLE SAYS DISLOCATION ON DECLINE IN AUSTRALIA MARKETS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's financial markets have operated with less dislocation than other parts of the world over the past three years and continue to do so, Guy Debelle, assistant governor at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2 , said Thursday. (published 0455 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PHILIPPINES TO PRICE 10-YEAR DOLLAR BOND AS EARLY AS TODAY - SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philippine government may price a benchmark-sized peso-denominated global bond as early as Thursday, a source privy to the bond issuance said. (published 0520 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: MORE ALL-TIME LOWS FOR EUR/CHF \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: Bear pressure is looking to put support at 1.2675 to the test, which protects the weak recovery off Wednesday's 1.2659 low. However, a break below 1.2659 is required to put bears in control of the near-term and prompt further weakness towards 1.2625 and 1.2588. Regaining ground above 1.2743 would lift the tone and re-open 1.2764, threatening 1.2790. Weekly chart EUR/USD trend: Range. (published 0612 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: GILTS TO LOSE MORE GROUND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday BUND: Significant resistance emerged at 132.14 Wednesday to put support at 130.91 to the test. The wider setback off the Aug. 31 contract high at 133.29 dominates the tone, and a break below 130.91 would bring the Sep. 3 reaction low at 130.24 back into focus. Keeping 130.91 intact would prompt a rally to 131.85, but only above there would re-open the 132.14 high. Weekly chart BUND trend: Range. (published 0625 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FOCUS: FISCAL DISCIPLINE TEST FOR AUSTRALIA'S MINORITY GOVERNMENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's minority government will need to operate within tight fiscal guidelines if it's to meet ambitious budgetary surplus plans while at the same time doling out sweeteners to lawmakers on whose support it depends. (published 2355 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PREVIEW: CHINA AUGUST CPI LIKELY HIGHEST SINCE OCTOBER 2008 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's consumer price index likely rose at its fastest rate in nearly two years in August as floods and unusually hot weather for the month pushed up food prices, but economists expect the increases in the CPI to moderate in coming months as weather conditions return to normal. (published 0100 GMT) [ 09-09-10 0702GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100909e699000mt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
