\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Japan Warnings Manage To Hold Back Soaring Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kosaku Narioka   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 626 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2010\par 08:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Renewed threats by Japan's Minister of Finance Yoshihiko Noda to take "decisive steps" to stop the rise in the yen failed to deter the market Thursday, but they at least stopped traders from pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to yet another 15-year high against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 After falling to its lowest level since May 1995 at Y83.34 yesterday after similar remarks by the finance minister, the dollar managed to stabilize today, briefly climbing above the Y84 level. At 0450 GMT it was at Y83.77, from Y83.92 in late New York trading.\par \par \uc2 "The market is fed up with warnings," said Yoshinori Nagano, a senior strategist at \uc2 Daiwa Asset Management\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The market was even less impressed with comments by Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa, who defended the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's steps to date and rejected many of the more radical steps being urged on them, such as increased purchases of JGBs to help reflate the economy and break the deflationary spiral.\par \par \uc2 Kenichiro Ikezawa of \uc2 Daiwa SB Investments\uc2  said investors found "nothing new in Shirakawa's comments."\par \par \uc2 On balance, analysts say the market is now unlikely to move in any clear direction ahead of Tuesday's vote within the ruling Democratic Party to determine the next party leader and therefore the country's prime minister.\par \par \uc2 Current Prime Minister Naoto Kan is seen as steering a middle course between economic stimulus and reining in fiscal spending, while his opponent Ichiro Ozawa advocates greater government spending and more active steps to stop the rise in the yen. Ozawa has said that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.\par \par \uc2 Noda said today that the Finance Ministry is getting the mechanisms ready in case it decides to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "We are conducting various simulations," he said.\par \par \uc2 The euro fell against the dollar to 1.2694 from 1.2716, and dropped against the yen to 106.35 from 106.72. Traders said that the euro was starting to stabilize with concerns about European bank stress tests starting to recede.\par \par \uc2 "An extreme view on the euro is subsiding," said Nobuaki Kubo, vice president at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  Investment Services in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the Australian dollar surged against the dollar to 0.9239 and the yen to 77.42 after Australia's unemployment rate fell to lower-than-expected 5.1% in August, compared to 5.3% in July.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 82.696 from 82.608 late New York.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates At 00:50 EDT / 0450 GMT \par  \par                          Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par Dollar Rates                        2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par  \par USD/JPY Yen              83.76-80   83.92-94   -0.19  84.02   83.67   -10.02 \par EUR/USD Euro             1.2693-95  1.2718-19  -0.20  1.2742  1.2690  -11.34 \par GBP/USD Sterling         1.5444-47  1.5470-76  -0.17  1.5477  1.5443   -4.45 \par USD/CHF Swiss Franc      1.0102-12  1.0108-18  -0.06  1.0126  1.0104   -2.43 \par USD/CAD Canadian Dlr     1.0391-97  1.0361-65  +0.29  1.0394  1.0355   -1.16 \par AUD/USD Australian Dlr   0.9207-10 