\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Stock Seen Lower Ahead Of US Data\b0\par\par\uc2 2,881 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2010\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SNAPSHOT:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 European bourses seen lower, government debt up; yen, oil futures up, spot gold lower\par \par \uc2 -Fed's Beige Book Notes Slowing Pace Of US Recovery\par \par \uc2 -Obama: U.S. Simply Can't Afford Tax Cuts For Rich\par \par \uc2 -Obama Announces New Plan To Support Economy, Job Creation\par \par \uc2 -US Prosecutor To Open Civil Litigation Front Against Financial Fraud -FT\par \par \uc2 -Dana Meets Large Investors To Pitch For Higher KNOC Offer - FT\par \par \uc2 -UK Government: Approves WINGAS Gas Storage Facility\par \par \uc2 -Lagarde Confident In Irish Govt, "Situation Fragile" - Report\par \par \uc2 -WSJ: Goldman Settlement With FSA Totals Nearly GBP20M - Source\par \par \uc2 -FSA: UK Depositors Of \uc2 Anglo Irish Bank\uc2  To Be Customers Of Funding Bank\par \par \uc2 -Baroin: France Targets 2011 Budget Deficit Below EUR100B-Report\par \par \uc2 -Areva, EDF Working On Strategic Agreement -Report\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Suedzucker\uc2 , Agrana Part Of Vienna Cartel Court Probe\par \par \uc2 -Italy To Sell EUR4B-EUR5.5B June 2015, Sept 2040 BTPs Sep 13\par \par \uc2 Watch for: 0600 GMT GER Aug CPI; 0800 GMT Sep ECB Monthly Bulletin; 0830 GMT UK Jul Trade; 1100 GMT Sep Bank of England interest rate decision; 1230 GMT US Sep 4 Unemployment Insurance Weekly Claims; 1230 GMT US Jul U.S. International Trade in Goods & Services\par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & US/ASIAN SUMMARIES:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 Asian bourses are mostly higher as risk sentiment sees a fragile recovery. But with more labor data on tap Thursday, investors are likely to trade cautiously. European bourses are seen starting a bit lower, with government debt attempting to inch higher. The yen is a bit higher against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while oil futures are up and spot gold slightly lower.\par \par \uc2 STOCKS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 European bourses are seen starting slightly lower on Thursday, with investors balancing an uptick in economic outlook with the latest concerns about euro-zone debt.\par \par \uc2 For Thursday's opening, IG Markets is calling the FTSE down 16 at 5414, the DAX off 30 at 6134 and the CAC down 17 at 3660.\par \par \uc2 "European peripheral debt is the key issue at the moment, with Irish spreads widening out again" on Wednesday, said Stephen Taylor, a strategist at Dolmen Stockbrokers.\par \par \uc2 Additional worries about Ireland have in part been sparked by the rising cost of bailing out \uc2 Anglo Irish Bank\uc2 , Taylor said. He noted that those fears have pushed Irish government bond yields to record levels.\par \par \uc2 French Finance Minister Christine Lagarde said in an interview published Wednesday on the La Tribune website that she is confident the Irish government can manage the problems of nationalized lender Anglo Irish Bank Corp., even though the situation is fragile.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stock futures are a bit lower on Thursday, after markets rose Wednesday, with \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2 , \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  and \uc2 Boeing\uc2  among the leaders. A report from the Federal Reserve suggested that the economic expansion was continuing and President Barack Obama formally unveiled fresh economic stimulus ideas.\par \par \uc2 The Fed's Beige Book showed the U.S. economic recovery has continued, albeit "with widespread signs of a deceleration compared with preceding periods."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Obama unveiled a mix of business tax cuts and infrastructure programs to boost the economy.\par \par \uc2 Mark Luschini, chief investment strategist at \uc2 Janney Montgomery Scott\uc2 , noted that Wednesday's gains, which followed a big rally last week in stocks, come as investors are increasingly factoring in indications that "the threat of the double-dip recession is waning."\par \par \uc2 Asian stock markets are mostly higher Thursday though Chinese banks and property developers fell on persistent concerns that Beijing would roll out further tightening measures.\par \par \uc2 "We saw evidence the U.S. hit a soft patch recently," said David Taylor, a market analyst at CMC Markets, but "there continues to be this sense of relief that the economy doesn't appear to be heading back into recession--at least not right away. This, combined with more tax cuts and infrastructure spending plans by the Obama administration, (had) traders in the mood to buy up."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  majors are trading in relatively tight ranges, though the yen continues to tip a bit higher against the dollar, euro and sterling.\par \par \uc2 Many believe there will be no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  until at least Sept. 14, when the leadership of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ruling party is decided, according to Hidetoshi Yanagihara, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 Other players are looking to U.S. weekly jobless claims data due later Thursday for further cues about the strength of the economy.\par \par \uc2 The successful auction of Portuguese government debt overshadowed concerns about the credibility of European bank stress tests.\par \par \uc2 "The successful Portuguese bond auction shows that while European markets have tightened, they do remain open," said Lena Komileva, head of G7 market economics at Tullett Prebon in London. "They are not frozen."\par \par \uc2 The pound is likely to hold in narrow ranges against the dollar and euro while seeing support from an uptick in risk appetite.\par \par \uc2 BONDS:\par \par \uc2 =====\par \par \uc2 Bunds and gilts may nudge higher on Thursday, as investors eye debt problems in peripheral euro-zone countries while major ones, such as France and Germany, project lower budget deficits.\par \par \uc2 Concerns over peripheral countries continue to dog sentiment, with spreads on Irish bonds versus bunds widening to a new high Wednesday as a rescue of Ireland's banking system pushed up the country's debt burden. Spreads on Portuguese debt also rose to a record, having eased earlier after a successful auction.\par \par \uc2 France targets bringing its budget deficit under EUR100 billion in 2011 from an expected EUR152 billion this year, budget minister Francois Baroin said in an interview to be published in Thursday's edition of French daily Les Echos.\par \par \uc2 The final reading of German August inflation due Thursday is expected to be unchanged from the preliminary report showing a slower pace of price increase from July.\par \par \uc2 In the U.K., "We're just stabilizing after the moves of the past few days and it still looks difficult to make further progress on the upside in yields," said Kenneth Broux at \uc2 Lloyds TSB\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England will announce its decision on monetary policy Thursday and all 14 economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires are forecasting no change in the policy interest rate for an 18th straight month.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasurys are higher on Thursday, after markets fell Wednesday as a deluge of government and private debt supply overshadowed a strong 10-year note sale and a soft reading from the latest Federal Reserve economic survey.\par \par \uc2 Demand for safe assets also faded as well-bid government bond auctions from the Portuguese government eased worries about the euro-zone's banking system and sovereign debt.\par \par \uc2 In Tokyo on Thursday, prices of Japan's government bonds fell as stocks rebounded.\par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan Governor Masaaki Shirakawa on Thursday reiterated his reluctance to boost the size of its Japanese government bond buying from the market, saying such a move could drive up long-term yields if investors think the BOJ is trying to finance fiscal spending.\par \par \uc2 ENERGY:\par \par \uc2 =======\par \par \uc2 Oil is higher Thursday on expectations that government data will show a rise in U.S. demand, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 New York's main contract, light sweet crude for October delivery, rose 47 cents to $75.14 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 Brent North Sea crude for October delivery tacked on 28 cents to $78.45 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 Despite fears that the U.S. economic recovery is losing steam the American Petroleum Institute said Wednesday the country's crude stocks fell 7.3 million barrels last week.\par \par \uc2 "I think that sentiments in the market are a bit positive now," said Ong Yi Ling, an analyst with Phillip Futures in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 "The API report showed an unexpected drawdown in crude oil inventories," she said.\par \par \uc2 METALS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 Gold's break higher out of consolidation last week is supportive for the bulls, said \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . But the bank said its short-term concern is the history of previous highs acting as pivots and oscillation points.\par \par \uc2 "As such, we look for the market to struggle near $1,265 a troy ounce and profit-taking to ensue," it said, tipping trendline support on downticks near $1,238/oz-$1,240/oz.\par \par \uc2 "There is no chart damage unless $1,210/oz is broken, and we do not expect this to happen," the bank said.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was at $1,254.40 per troy ounce, down 80 cents from New York.\par \par \uc2 LME 3-month copper is at $7,514 a metric ton, down $160 and taking other metals lower with it. Traders cited heavy selling from China. An analyst in Shanghai said rumors of further tightening measures for China's property market could be behind the fall.\p