\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY May Rise; USD Buying Expected in Asia\b0\par\par\uc2 1,654 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2010\par 01:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2359 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may rise to 84.30 in Asian morning if pair breaks 84.05, triggers stop-loss orders around that level, says senior dealer at major Japan trust bank; Takeshi Yasukawa, senior dealer at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shinkin \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , notes some market participants expect an "overwhelming amount of USD buy orders" coming in from Japan importers for their settlement. Meanwhile, trust bank dealer says downside limited to around 83.50 as investors cautious about possibility of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities; pair last 83.97. EUR biased down as Europe sovereign risk still a lingering concern. EUR/JPY may trade in 106.00-107.00 band vs 106.50 last; EUR/USD may trade in 1.2650-1.2730 vs 1.2684 last. Says markets to seek cues on risk appetite from performance of Asian shares. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2354 GMT [Dow Jones] Nervousness remains in base metals markets after sharp selloff yesterday on speculation of probe by Chinese authorities into rubber market positions, says Triland Metals, which noted that some ground recovered during the day was given up again towards the close of trading, with zinc losing most but copper also affected. As for aluminum, which also fell overnight, "we are likely going through a temporary correction with buying interest expected to re-emerge soon," Triland says in daily note. LME 3-month copper fell $119 on day to $7,555/metric ton, aluminum tanked $55 at $2,105, lead lost $33 at $2,201, zinc down $66 at $2,153, nickel declined $150 to $22,750, while tin bucked trend, climbing $25 at $21,700. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2344 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi likely to sustain rise after closing up 0.3% at 1784.36 yesterday, helped by gains in U.S. stocks, likely foreign inflows following improved sentiment, analysts say. "Kospi is likely to test 1800 today as worries over a possible double-dip recession are largely subdued," as underscored by above-consensus U.S. economic data, BOK governor's comments yesterday, says MiraeAsset Securities analyst Lee Jin-woo. Foreigners also expected to return to net buying, especially in techs following recent weakness, mainly on concerns over global economic health. Kospi tipped in 1770-1800 range. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2342 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW may rise vs 1,167.4 late Thursday in Seoul, taking breather ahead of weekend after slipping to 1-month low yesterday on active USD selling by offshore players, analysts say. With pair's breach below the lower-end of recent trading ranges at 1,170 yesterday, next key support now at 1,160 says Samsung Futures analyst Jeon Seung-ji. But given still-unstable overseas financial market conditions, potential USD-buying related to M&A deals, and chance of USD-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities, "there's a good chance," that pair will recover to the 1,170 support, she adds. Tips pair in 1,165-1,175 range for day. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2337 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume +9.7% at HK$4.492 billion yesterday, as percentage of total market volume higher than recent averages at 7.3% but amount remain dominated by China Mobile (0941.HK), thus investors perhaps should avoid reading too much into higher ratio. China Mobile led with HK$1.55 billion, 34.6% of total amount shorted, as near-term pressure remains after \uc2 Vodafone\uc2 's stake disposal. Agricultural Bank of China (1288.HK) in second place with HK$264.35 million, then HKEx (0388.HK) with HK$152.10 million, A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) with HK$133.31 million, Hutchison (0013.HK) with HK$127.70 million. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2335 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian net retail fund flows of A$1 billion across the market for the June quarter were disappointing, \uc2 UBS\uc2  analysts say, with super flows of A$2.2 billion well down on the A$4.6 billion recorded for the same quarter a year ago, and outflows in investment funds were more than A$1.1 billion. "The combination of falling equities, deteriorating sentiment and ongoing regulatory uncertainty clearly impacted the quarter's flows, the worst we've seen for a long time," \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. Notes with flows providing little counter to a near 12% fall in Australian equities, funds under management fell by 5% and is now back below September 2009 levels. (lyndal.mcfarland@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2333 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. 10-year Treasury yield may rise due to eased risk-aversion as European sovereign bond problems look to be stabilizing, says CIBC World Markets Japan executive director Kazuaki Oh'E. Tips yield to trade in 2.73%-2.78% range vs 2.755% last. Adds, for outlook, investors will keep paying attention to U.S. economic data, also whether Obama administration's stimulus package will pass Congress, he says.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2330 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to consolidate with risks skewed to upside after hitting 3-week high $75.96/bbl yesterday, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics in bullish mode; 5-day moving average above 15-day moving average and rising. Breach of $75.96 resistance would target $76.10 (Aug. 19 high), then $76.30 (confluence of 55-day & 100-day moving averages), $76.63 (Aug. 17 high), $76.86 (50% Fibonacci correction of $82.97-$70.76 Aug. 4-Aug. 25 decline) and $77.47 (200-day moving average). Support at $73.88 (yesterday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, exposing downside to $73.37 (Wednesday's low), then $72.63 (Tuesday's reaction low), $71.53 (Aug. 31 reaction low) and $70.76 (Aug. 25 trough). October crude last up 62 cents at $74.87/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2326 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net buy orders for 8.5 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 1st day of premarket buying in 3 days may be positive for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Buy orders total 20.5 million shares, with sell orders amounting to 12.0 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2328 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold early in Asia $1,245.00/oz, down 30 cents, after trading up to $1,258.80 yesterday, as demand for the metal as a safe haven faded. "We might have some exhaustion on the part of the bulls," said Dan Cook, Chicago-based senior market analyst with brokerage IG Markets. Technically, lower close overnight confirms a sell signal on one chart and if this correct, "we have seen the short term high in gold at $1,262," says ScotiaMocatta, with charts suggesting a move to $1,222. "We suggest booking profits on this well telegraphed up move," bank says. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2324 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian wheat futures to rise today after U.S. wheat prices surged more than 4% overnight on worries cold weather will hurt Canada's crop, tighten global supplies of high-quality wheat. Soft red winter wheat for September delivery gained 28.0 cents, or 4.1%, at US$7.0675/bushel on Chicago Board of Trade. Stronger AUD will moderate rise of wheat prices. AUD/USD last 0.9233, up from yesterday's low around 0.9165, but down from high round midnight of 0.9275. Yesterday, most active ASX milling wheat deliverable January in NSW fell A$10.50 at A$284.50/metric ton; ASX milling wheat deliverable January in WA fell A$8.70 at A$308.80. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2322 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads mixed, with gains on Wall Street offset by weaker commodity prices. Overnight SPI 200 futures rose 19 points, or 0.4%, to 4601 after Wall Street rose on better-than-expected economic data. DJIA up 0.3% at 10415.24, S&P 500 up 0.5% at 1104.18, Nasdaq up 0.3% at 2236.20. Equities pared gains after WSJ said \uc2 Deutsche Bank AG\uc2  is planning to raise as much as EUR9 billion to boost its stake in \uc2 Deutsche Postbank AG\uc2  and possibly shore up its own capital. U.S. 10-year bond yield rose 10 bps to 2.76%. U.S. weekly jobless claims fell 27,000 to 451,000 vs 470,000 expected. July trade deficit fell to US$42.8 billion vs expected US$47 billion, mainly due to aircraft exports. In the consumer sector, \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  rose 1.7%, although \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  fell 2.3% after August same-store sales missed expectations. S&P 500 Financials rose 1.2%, with KBW Bank index up 1.6%. S&P 500 Telcos up 1.4%, with \uc2 AT&T\uc2  up 1.5%. S&P 500 Materials down 0.3%, with \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  down 2.5%, Freeport down 1.2%, Barrick down 2.8%, Newmont down 2.2%. BHP ADRs at A$38.21 (based on AUD/USD at 0.9232) vs local close at A$38.18. London listed BHP up 1.9%, Rio (RIO.AU) up 2.0%. Spot gold fell to US$1,243.59 vs US$1,254.70. Nymex crude oil fell 0.6% to US$74.25. LME copper down 1.6%, zinc down 3.0%, aluminum down 2.5%, nickel down 0.8%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR/USD at 1.2699 vs 1.2723. AUD/USD at 0.9237 vs 0.9184. S&P/ASX 200 last 4582.2. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com) [ 09-09-10 1959ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100909e699000er\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
