\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Summary Of US Stocks\b0\par\par\uc2 1,752 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2010\par 00:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 2202 GMT [Dow Jones] WALL STREET: Stocks climbed following better-than-expected data on employment, U.S. trade deficit. \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  +2.5%; banking giant will repurchase up to $91.3 million in auction-rate securities from New Jersey clients in settlement with state attorney general's office. \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  +1.7%, \uc2 AT&T\uc2  +1.5%. \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  dropped 2.3% after August same-store sales report didn't quite live up to expectations. Gains came after release of data that U.S. jobless benefit applications fell sharply last week, an optimistic sign for troubled job market, while airplane exports helped shrink trade deficit in July. "Today's unemployment number reinforces the monthly numbers that we saw last Friday," which also topped expectations, said Barry M. Sine, director of research at CapStone Investments. "It's taking the double dip out of the conversation." \uc2 Adobe Systems\uc2  +12%, as Apple's less-than-forthcoming press release about relaxing restrictions put on application developers was seen as possible indication it's retreating in battle against \uc2 Adobe\uc2 's flash technology. Apple +0.1%. Dow +0.3%, Nasdaq Composite +0.3%, Philly semicons +0.1%. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2159 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ shares may get lift from improving sentiment after better-than-expected U.S. data overnight; "things are all rosy again in a global sense, so that bodes well," says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  broker Peter Sigley. Large caps like Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ), \uc2 Contact Energy\uc2  (CEN.NZ) may benefit. But notes index may be weighed by negative sentiment toward Telecom (TEL.NZ) after government yesterday announced it was one of 14 companies short-listed for NZ$3 billion ultra-fast broadband initiative but not in top 3 prioritized for negotiation. If investors remain downbeat on Telecom "it will weigh reasonably heavily on the market." NZX-50 index closed down 0.3% at 3151.77 yesterday (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2128 GMT [Dow Jones] USD index shuffling sideways as growth sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  bolstered by better-than-expected data, equity market gains, says BNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Mike Jones; "Encouraging data helped calm fears of a sharp slowdown in the U.S. economy, underpinning a 0.5% rise in the S&P500. Across the Atlantic, European equities were bolstered by rumours Basel III capital requirements will not be as onerous on banks as earlier feared." USD Index last 82.692. But EUR/USD, GBP/USD dribbling lower as no surprises from BoE policy, rate remained unchanged; "July UK trade figures were fairly uninspiring and the NIESR (think tank) said the BoE is unlikely to raise interest rates until mid-2011." Says relative strength of slew of Chinese data due out over next two days will help determine whether or not markets' relatively buoyant risk appetite maintained. Tips USD index support at 82.10 with initial resistance at 83.15. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2124 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. SUMMARY: Australian, Canadian dollar rose against USD after better-than-expected U.S. monthly trade data, weekly report on initial jobless claims, painted brighter picture of global economy; "The real question has been: Will global growth still hold up? And the answer so far appears to be 'yes,'" said Aroop Chatterjee, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  quantitative strategist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in New York. But, reassuring U.S. data were not enough to help EUR muster a gain. Separately USD declined slightly against JPY as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister kept threat of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alive Thursday, saying authorities "are conducting various simulations" to curb \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength. Late Thursday, EUR/USD at 1.2703 vs 1.2716 late Wednesday, USD/JPY at 83.83 vs 83.92, EUR/JPY at 106.49 vs 106.72, GBP/USD at 1.5438 vs 1.5465, USD/CHF at 1.0152 vs 1.0122. USD Index at 82.649 from 82.608. Stocks climbed, helped by better-than-expected data. \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2  added 2.5%; banking giant will repurchase up to $91.3 million in auction-rate securities from New Jersey clients in settlement with state attorney general's office. McDonalds fell 2.3% after August same-store sales report didn't quite live up to expectations. Dow +0.3%, Nasdaq +0.3, Philly semicons +0.1%. Treasurys dropped as latest U.S. jobs, trade data eased worries about health of U.S. economy; 2-year yields +4.0 bps at 0.562%, 10-years +10.1 bps at 2.755%. Gold futures edged down as investors calmer about stage of economic recovery; December Comex gold fell $6.60 to $1,250.90/oz. October Nymex crude settled 42 cents lower at $74.25/bbl. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2100 GMT [Dow Jones]NZ government bonds tad weaker as U.S. Treasurys fell overnight on stronger U.S. data that showed recovery on track, says local bond trader; "New jobless claims fell to 451,000, while July trade deficit narrowed sharply, giving hopeful signs of economic recovery." Adds buy interest in May 2021s remained strong late yesterday. Says market watching 2Q Terms of Trade index to be released this morning. November 2011 bond yields +2 bps 3.76%, 2-year swap rates +2.0 bps at 3.89%, 10-year swap rates +2.0 bps at 5.045%. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2016 GMT [Dow Jones] Bloggers focus in on ECB President Trichet's hinting that he'd explore temporarily suspending voting rights for Eurozone members that break rules on public finances. FT report says ECB rejects throwing out eurozone members, but that Trichet said "temporary suspension of voting rights is something that should be explored." Zero Hedge blog writes that "where there is smoke there is fire, and the ECB president has sufficient reasons to make this demand. It can only mean that major European political turbulence is imminent." FT report notes it's "not clear that the withdrawal of voting rights would be possible without changing EU treaties." (christopher.dieterich@dowjones.com) ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.ft.com/cms/s/0/13730da2-bc31-11df-8c02-00144feab49a.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.ft.com/cms/s/0/13730da2-bc31-11df-8c02-00144feab49a.html}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 2015 GMT [Dow Jones] Bigger-than-expected declines in initial weekly jobless claims and July trade deficit carry stocks for a while today, with Dow Industrials threatening early to reach into triple digits on the upside. It continues to be what Art Cashin calls a "low-volume levitation," which nearly succumbed to evaporation as bulls showed signs of afternoon fatigue. US stocks manage modest gains, but still unable to produce an upside breakout to really demoralize bears. Materials sector ends as the only one in the red; financials, health care and telecom lead, but conviction still seems MIA. DJIA rises 28.23 to 10415.24, and Nasdaq Comp adds 7.33 to 2236.20. S&P 500 ends 5.31 higher at 1104.18. (john.shipman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2009 GMT [Dow Jones] Argentine bonds paying coupons tied to GDP growth have risen too far and aren't worth the risk at current prices, asset management firm TPCG says. The so-called GDP warrants have risen over 30% since the beginning of July on expectations for steep growth and amid bond rally due to the recent debt swap, the upgrade in Argentina's credit rating, and the rise in global risk appetite. But a big risk hangs over the GDP warrants, according to TPCG. The government is accused of exaggerating growth in 2009. It says GDP expanded by 0.9% last year, while most economists say that GDP shrank by about 2.7%. TPCG says there "is a probability in revising [official] GDP figures" after the 2011 presidential elections, which could lower the coupons paid out on the GDP warrants. (shane.romig@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2008 GMT [Dow Jones] The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  drew a modest $60 million via the Fed's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap lines in the week ended on Sept. 8, in a seven day term at 1.19%. That replaces the $40 million in borrowing that had matured that week. No other central banks took loans via the facility that's supposed to ensure international financial markets have ample dollar liquidity. (michael.derby@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2008 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD looking overvalued and "ongoing NZD strength is overdone," says \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  economist Annette Beacher. Notes RBNZ on hold for near-term with only a gentle tightening path ahead for 2011, data flow out of Australia "will continue to be superior" and local events such as collapse of South Canterbury Finance, earthquakes "are on balance NZD negative." On NZD/USD retains year-end target of 0.6800, pair last 0.7251 and on AUD/USD tips year-end target of 1.29, last 1.2734; "This time next year we expect further NZD underperformance as the RBA forges ahead with the tightening cycle, and we target 1.32 against the AUD." (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1958 GMT [Dow Jones] Harvard professor Dani Rodik points out on Project Syndicate that the interests of poor countries are often overlooked in the debate surrounding the value of the Chinese yuan. While \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  value is critical to US and China trade, Rodik highlights debate surrounding whether or not developing countries are helped or hurt by a weak yuan. Some argue developing nations struggle to compete with China's undervalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a setback to burgeoning industrialization efforts. On the other hand, researchers at the OECD say sharp yuan appreciation would hurt developing nations since China's growth would slow, which in turn would slow growth in other developing countries. (christopher.dieterich@dowjones.com) ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.project-syndicate.org/commentary/rodrik47/English"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.project-syndicate.org/commentary/rodrik47/English}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 1956 GMT [Dow Jones] Brazil August IPCA inflation printed at a softer-than-expected 0.04% m/m, the third consecutive month of a nearly zero inflation rate, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . But a turnaround could be round the corner. "We believe a pickup in food price inflation will set the tone, lifting the headline level towards the 0.15% range," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says, expecting headline inflation to rise to 0.15-0.18%. "The August reading leaves the BCB even more comfortable with the current Selic rate, but the season of 0% monthly inflation rates should be behind us," it says. (matthew.cowley@dowjones.com) [ 09-09-10 1802ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100909e699000e0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
