\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Shares End Mostly Higher But China Developers Fall\b0\par\par\uc2 883 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2010\par 13:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian stock markets were mostly higher Thursday with exporters and trading houses supporting the Tokyo market, but banks and property developers fell in China on persistent concerns that Beijing would roll out further tightening measures.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei Share Average ended 0.8% higher, Australia's S&P/ASX 200 rose 1.0% and South Korea's Kospi Composite was up 0.3%.\par \par \uc2 China's Shanghai Composite Index fell 1.4%, while Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index gained 0.4% and India's Sensex rose 0.7%. Dow Jones Industrial Average futures were up 23 points in screen trade.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said investors were looking ahead to China's August trade numbers due out tomorrow. "Expectations are that China will announce a big trade surplus," said Martin Hennecke, an investment advisor with Tyche Group in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 "Often the market interprets that as a bad thing because there will be rising pressure on China to allow the yuan to appreciate," he added.\par \par \uc2 Data showing brisk property transaction activity in major Chinese cities during August stirred worries Beijing may take further action to head of a bubble in the property market. \uc2 China Vanke\uc2  fell 3.4% in Shanghai and Poly Real Estate lost 4.1%.\par \par \uc2 "Housing prices are back up, stoking fears of another round of tightening," said Wang Weijun, an analyst at Zheshang Securities. "In the long run, prices should drop as Beijing has launched a great deal of affordable housing projects, but investors are concerned there will be further actions to tamp (down) prices between now and when those homes are ready."\par \par \uc2 He added that smaller banks were still digesting a report on Tuesday in Century Weekly magazine which cited unnamed sources as saying that China's banking regulator is considering imposing a mandatory 2.5% loan-reserve ratio to help curb credit growth. Such a move would mostly hit small- and mid-sized lenders. Industrial Bank was down 2.8% and Bank of China finished 0.9% lower.\par \par \uc2 Exporters led the bounce in Tokyo as concerns over the health of the European banking sector eased, lifting companies with high exposure to euro zone. \uc2 Mazda\uc2  and Canon both advanced 1.1%, while Toyota rose 1.9%.\par \par \uc2 Antivirus software maker Trend Micro surged 10.30% after Taiwan's DigiTimes reported on its website, citing Trend Micro chief executive officer Eva Chen, that the company had received a merger proposal from an unnamed major IT company.\par \par \uc2 Real estate stocks fell after a report showed office vacancies for five central wards in Tokyo rose slightly in August from July. \uc2 Mitsui Fudosan\uc2 's shares ended down 3.6%.\par \par \uc2 In Australia, upbeat domestic employment data helped lift shares to a four-week high. The country's unemployment rate fell to a lower-than-expected seasonally adjusted 5.1% in August from 5.3% in July. The number of employed rose 30,900, the Australian Bureau of Statistics said.\par \par \uc2 The market was also focused on news the Australian Competition and Consumer Commission had continued its opposition to \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2 's proposed A$13.3 billion bid for wealth management firm rival AXA Asia Pacific, dealing a blow to the bank's plans to become the country's largest wealth manager. AXA Asia Pacific was down 6.6% at A$5.08, compared with NAB's A$6.43 per share bid, indicating the market expected NAB's pursuit faces insurmountable hurdles.\par \par \uc2 Santos was down 7.0% after saying it would sell its 15% equity stake in its Gladstone liquefied natural gas project to France's Total for A$650 million and will also sell 1.5 million tons of LNG per year to Total over 20 years. The deal is subject to regulatory approval. But investors were worried about the potential of a dilutive capital raising.\par \par \uc2 In Korea, the market was lower after the Bank of Korea kept interest rates on hold; a Dow Jones Newswires poll of 16 economists showed 11 had expected a 25-basis point hike for the key rate to 2.50%. Some in the market may be interpreting the decision as a sign the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not as confident about the economic outlook as thought.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the euro was at $1.2715, compared with $1.2716 late in New York, and at Y106.44, from Y106.72. The dollar bought Y83.71, from Y83.92.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister kept the threat of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alive Thursday, saying the authorities "are conducting various simulations" to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was at $1,254.50 per troy ounce, down 70 cents from New York. October Nymex crude oil futures were up 58 cents at $75.25 per barrel on Globex.\par \par \uc2 -Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4140; markettalk@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 09-09-10 1102GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1106 4125 5037 62560   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100909e69900141\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
