\par\par \b \uc2 INSTANT VIEW 3-Japan big manufacturers' mood improves\b0\par\par\uc2 619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2010\par 02:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (For more stories on the Japanese economy click [ID:nECONJP])\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 9 (Reuters) - Big Japanese manufacturers became more optimistic about business conditions in the July-September quarter but economists warned slowing exports and the yen's strength would erode profits and crimp capital spending ahead.\par \par \uc2 ***********************************************************\par \par \uc2 KEY POINTS:\par \par \uc2 -- The business survey index (BSI) of sentiment at large manufacturers stood at 13.3 in July-September, up from 10.0 in April-June, according to a joint survey by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Economic and Social Research Institute. [JPBUSC=ECI]\par \par \uc2 -- Companies see capital spending in the fiscal year to March 2011 rising 8.7 percent. That compared with the previous survey's 9.2 percent rise.\par \par \uc2 -- But large manufacturers expect the sentiment index to worsen with a flat reading in the October-December, compared with 12.7 in the previous survey.\par \par \uc2 COMMENTARY:\par \par \uc2 SEIJI SHIRAISHI, CHIEF ECONOMIST, HSBC SECURITIES JAPAN\par \par \uc2 "Manufacturers' cautious view for the October-December quarter is due to the expiring government subsidies for energy-efficient automobiles as some automakers have already announced plans to cut production.\par \par \uc2 "The yen's rise and worries about the U.S. economic outlook have also prompted companies to cut capital spending plans. As the yen's rise stems from problems in the United States and Europe, any steps by Japanese policymakers including \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to prove ineffective and business sentiment may worsen further in coming quarters."\par \par \uc2 YOSHIKI SHINKE, SENIOR ECONOMIST, DAI-ICHI LIFE RESEARCH INSTITUTE\par \par \uc2 "Companies are becoming increasingly cautious about the economic outlook due to slowing exports and the yen's appreciation. They may cut their capital spending plans sharply in coming months if the economy shows further weakness.\par \par \uc2 "The economy is already slowing down as confidence among small firms stays at a very low level. Business sentiment will likely get a boost if policymakers take steps to arrest the yen's rise and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to prove effective, although chances of such action are still small."\par \par \uc2 TAKESHI MINAMI, CHIEF ECONOMIST, NORINCHUKIN RESEARCH INSTITUTE\par \par \uc2 "Small and midsize firms' sentiment is particularly weak. They are probably factoring in the expected slowdown in Japan's economic growth.\par \par \uc2 "The yen's rise will hurt profits. The outlook is not good as companies are hit by slowing overseas growth and the strong yen. It's hard to forecast a broad recovery in sentiment ahead."\par \par \uc2 MARKET REACTION:\par \par \uc2 -- For yen updates click <FXNEWS>; for prices click <JPY=>\par \par \uc2 -- For JGB updates, click <MMNEWS>, for prices click <2JGBv1>\par \par \uc2 -- For stocks click [.T], for the Nikkei share average click <.N225>\par \par \uc2 LINK:\par \par \uc2 For a table on the survey, click:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mof.go.jp/english/bos/e1c2102.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.mof.go.jp/english/bos/e1c2102.htm}}}\par \par \uc2 BACKGROUND:\par \par \uc2 -- The index measures the percentage of firms that expect the business environment to improve from the previous quarter minus the percentage that expect it to worsen.\par \par \uc2 -- Japanese business sentiment has been improving helped partly by solid demand from Asia, but uncertainty over overseas economies and the strong yen are clouding the economic outlook.\par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government sharpened its rhetoric on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday. Investors ignored the talk as they pushed the yen to a 15-year high versus the dollar due to doubts \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will go solo and doubts coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with other Group of Seven countries is likely. [ID:nTOE687026]\par \par \uc2 -- Japan will release revised second-quarter gross domestic data data on Friday and growth is expected to be revised up to 0.4 percent quarter-on-quarter, or an annualised 1.5 percent, thanks to improvement in capital spending. [ID:nTOE682076] (Reporting by Kaori Kaneko; Editing by Chris Gallagher)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/SENTIMENT (INSTANT VIEW 3)|LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|NAT|U   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100909e6990000h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
