\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Rise; DJIA Marks 5th Gain In 6 Sessions\b0\par\par\uc2 2,400 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2010\par 00:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Indonesia markets are closed Thursday for Ied-ul-Fitr, Pakistan markets are closed for Wid-ul-Fitr, and Malaysian markets have a half-day session pre-Hari Raya Puasa.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10387.01  +46.32  +0.45% \par Nasdaq     2228.87  +19.98  +0.90% \par S&P 500    1098.87   +7.03  +0.64% \par FTSE 100   5429.74  +21.92  +0.41% \par Xetra DAX  6164.44  +46.55  +0.76% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  99 23/32  -2/32 0.518% +2.8BP \par 5Y Tsy   99 1/32  -7/32 1.449% +4.6BP \par 10Y Tsy 99 25/32 -14/32 2.650% +5.2BP \par  \par USD/JPY       83.94-95     +0.20% \par Range         84.04-83.35 \par EUR/USD       1.2719-20    +0.28% \par Range         1.2763-1.2660 \par AUD/USD       0.9176-78    +0.75% \par Range         0.9194-0.9099 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    12.50-12.80% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    10.85-11.25% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    12.30-12.60% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,257.50   -$1.80 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,254.60-$1,264.70 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $74.67   +$0.58 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks rose Wednesday, with \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2 , \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  and \uc2 Boeing\uc2  among the leaders as a report from the Federal Reserve suggested that the economic expansion was continuing and President Barack Obama formally unveiled fresh economic stimulus ideas.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 46.32 points, or 0.45%, to 10387.01, its fifth gain in six sessions. \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  was the measure's best performer, up 84 cents, or 2.2%, to 39.12, while \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  advanced 21 cents, or 1.9%, to 11.07, and \uc2 Boeing\uc2  climbed 1.08, or 1.7%, to 64.50.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite added 19.98, or 0.90%, to 2228.87. The Standard & Poor's 500 index gained 7.03, or 0.64%, to 1098.87, with its financial and industrial sectors leading to the upside.\par \par \uc2 The gains came as stocks recovered some of the ground lost in Tuesday's slump, although they remain well in the black for the month following better-than-expecting readings on the labor market and manufacturing last week. The Dow is up 3.7% for the month, while the Nasdaq has climbed 5.4% so far in September and the S&P 500 is up 4.7% for the period.\par \par \uc2 There was little additional U.S. economic data driving the market Wednesday, although a report from the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed the U.S. economic recovery has continued, albeit "with widespread signs of a deceleration compared with preceding periods." Meanwhile, Obama unveiled a mix of business tax cuts and infrastructure programs to boost the economy.\par \par \uc2 "It's a natural recovery" from Tuesday's decline, said Mark Luschini, chief investment strategist at \uc2 Janney Montgomery Scott\uc2 . He noted that Wednesday's gains, which followed a big rally last week in stocks, come as investors are increasingly factoring in indications that "the threat of the double-dip recession is waning."\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended slightly lower Wednesday, led by real estate firms due to concerns about further tightening measures, but gains in metals companies following recent calls by Beijing to accelerate consolidation in the industry limited the decline.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Shanghai index will likely continue to consolidate in a 2650-2750 range the rest of this week as investors await the release of August inflation data early next week.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks fell sharply Wednesday as the yen's rise to a fresh 15-year high against the dollar clouded the outlook for the country's exporters, despite stepped-up warnings from Japanese authorities over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "If the yen continues to strengthen on the back of government inaction, there will be further concerns that companies will need to significantly lower their profit forecasts," said Yutaka Yoshii, general manager at \uc2 Mito Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "It's too late to use the word \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the yen has already started moving higher so only real action will be effective," said Toshiyuki Kanayama, market analyst at \uc2 Monex Inc\uc2 ., adding the next support level for Nikkei is pegged at 8800.\par \par \uc2 Crude-oil futures settled higher in U.S. trading Wednesday, though a report that U.S. economic growth is slowing forced a retreat from above $75 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 Easing concerns about European sovereign finances caused investors to feel less of a need for gold as a haven investment, and futures fell slightly Wednesday as near-record prices proved too high for some and raised questions about the strength of jewelry demand.\par \par \uc2 Base metals reversed earlier losses on the London Metal Exchange Wednesday to close mostly higher, boosted by a stronger euro and equity markets as investor anxiety over the health of the European banking sector eased.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro rose against the dollar and yen Wednesday after a euro-zone government debt auction soothed investor nerves, leading them into higher-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The successful auction of Portuguese government debt overshadowed concerns about the credibility of European bank stress tests. The euro had fallen Tuesday on a Wall Street Journal report that European bank stress tests underestimated the exposure of some major banks to sovereign debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The successful Portuguese bond auction shows that while European markets have tightened, they do remain open," said Lena Komileva, head of G7 market economics at Tullett Prebon in London. "They are not frozen."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As investors rode the wave of positive sentiment to send U.S. stocks higher, the dollar rebounded from an overnight 15-year low against the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wednesday afternoon in New York, the euro was at $1.2716 after hitting an intraday high of $1.2764, compared with $1.2689 late Tuesday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y83.92 compared with Y83.80, after falling as low as Y83.34 overnight. The euro was at Y106.72, up from Y106.33. The U.K. pound was at $1.5465, up from $1.5350. The dollar was at CHF1.0122 compared with CHF1.0098.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 82.608 compared with 82.815.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Canadian dollar gained sharply against the greenback after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  raised interest rates, its third-consecutive increase of key rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar was at C$1.0373 in afternoon New York trading, down from C$1.0490 just before the rate announcement. It was at C$1.0475 late Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's partially a return of risk appetite," said Andrew Chaveriat, technical \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York of Wednesday's move higher in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  closely tied to global growth, such as the Australian and New Zealand dollars.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Until new U.S. economic data emerge, investors would likely keep the dollar itself range-bound, said John Doyle, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trader at Tempus Consulting in Washington. "We would expect the euro to trade within $1.2650 and $1.2850," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda was the latest official to warn markets that Japan was ready to act to bolster the yen, specifically raising the specter of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for the first time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We will take decisive steps--which of course include \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 --when it becomes necessary," Noda said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts have said the comments showed greater seriousness among \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's authorities toward the possibility of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , something they haven't done since 2004. But there is still skepticism as to whether \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would actually happen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Traders are not buying it," said Doyle of Tempus Consulting, referring to imminent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many believe there will be no \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  until at least Sept. 14, when the leadership of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's ruling party is decided, according to Hidetoshi Yanagihara, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys fell Wednesday as a deluge of government and private debt supply overshadowed a strong 10-year note sale and a soft reading from the latest Federal Reserve economic survey.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Demand for safe assets also faded as well-bid government bond auctions from the Portuguese government eased worries about the euro zone's banking system and sovereign debt. The U.S. stock market rebounded from a selloff a day earlier, and the Treasury market reversed Tuesday's rally.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 30-year Treasury was the biggest loser, as the market braces for a $13 billion 30-year bond auction Thursday, which will wrap up this week's $67 billion new government debt offerings.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Set-up for the 30-year bond auction is at bat," said Kevin Giddis, president of fixed income capital markets at Morgan Keegan Inc. in Memphis, Tennessee. "Traders would like to own it cheaper than the current level, so they will try to drive prices lower between now and Thursday, if possible."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of 1948 GMT, the benchmark conventional 10-year note was down 14/32, pushing up the yield 5.2 basis points to 2.650%. The 30-year bond was 1 1/32 lower, and its yield rose 5.7 basis points to 3.719%. Bond prices move inversely to yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys were also pounded by corporate bond supply as many companies are taking advantage of historically low interest rates to refinance their debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In two straight days, investment-grade issuance has topped $30 billion, with the likes of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hewlett-Packard Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (HPQ), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Home Depot Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (HD), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Aon Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (AON), \f28 \uc2 \f2 France Telecom\f28 \uc2 \f2  (FTE, FTE.FR) and even \f28 \uc2 \f2 Freddie Mac\f28 \uc2 \f2  (FMCC) tapping the market. Supply this month could be in the range of $75 billion to $100 billion, akin to the $99 billion sold last year in September.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Pent-up debt supply is...seeing the light of the day," said David Coard, head of fixed-income trading in New York at Williams Capital Group, adding that Treasurys were pressured as they competed with corporate bonds for investors' money.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The selling, which pushed up bond yields, enticed buyers to bid on a $21 billion 10-year note sale. The auction, an additional offering of a $24 billion 10-year auction in August, was 3.21 times oversubscribed, compared with the average of 3.15 for the previous eight auctions. [ 08-09-10 2211GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The notes were sold at a yield of 2.67%, the lowest since the auction in January 2009, which allows the U.S. government to continue to finance its budget shortfall at historically low interest rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended mostly steady Wednesday, retreating from earlier gains as local shares narrowed losses in afternoon trade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There has been an obvious seesaw effect between the debt market and the equity market in recent weeks given lack of policy cues," a trader at Shenyin Wanguo Securities said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The trader tips the Shanghai exchange government bond index to consolidate in a narrow 126.6-126.9 band ahead of the release of August inflation data Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Wednesday it plans to sell CNY17 billion (US$2.5 billion) worth of three-month bills and CNY100 billion worth of three-year bonds in its regular open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Long-term Japanese government bonds rose Wednesday as an even stronger yen exacerbated concern over the outlook for the country's export-reliant economy, prompting investors to seek safe-haven assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Fears are clearly intensifying that the sharp appreciation of the yen will drag on Japan's economy further," said Masashi Shimominami, a bond market analyst at Mizuho Securities. "Unless the yen weakens, investors will likely buy long-term JGBs actively and the yields could extend declines."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A trader at a Tokyo securities house said "many investors are concerned about whether the Japanese government will retract its pledge to rebuild the country's fiscal health and instead implement large-scale economic-stimulus measures."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Before the DPJ presidential race is over, it will be very difficult to dabble in superlong-term debt," the trader added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par US Economic Growth Shows Signs Of Slowing \par Obama Says Taxes On Wealthy Not A Punishment \par Goldman Settles With UK Regulator \par BP Says No Single Factor Caused Gulf Spill \par 7-Eleven In Talks With Casey's General Stores \par US Arm Of Goldman Principal Strategies In Talks \par US July Consumer Credit Decreases $3.6B \par EIA Sees US 3Q Oil Use Up 1.4% \par US Budget Deficit $1.3 Trillion So Far In FY 2010 \par =US Officials Praise South Korea's New Sanctions Against Iran \par =Shanda Games 2Q Profit Down 16% On Lower Sales, Margins \par Geithner: China Hasn't Adjusted Yuan Far Enough-Bloomberg TV \par =ISuppli: China Electronics Exports Fall In June, Except For PCs \par =Qiao Xing Universal Makes Offer For Rest Of Qiao Xing Mobile \par WSJ: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Roche Holding AG\f28 \uc2 \f2  Increasing Investments In China \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2350  Japan        3Q  Business Outlook Survey \par 0130  Australia    Aug Labour Force \par 0130  S Korea      Sep Bank of Korea Monetary Policy \par                    Committee meeting & decision \par 0300  S Korea      Aug PPI \par 0400  Malaysia     Jul Industrial Production Index \par 0400  Malaysia     Jul Manufacturing sales \par 0500  Japan        Aug Consumer Confidence Survey \par 0530  France       2Q  Job creation \par 0600  Germany      Aug CPI \par 0600  Germany      Jul Manufacturing turnover \par 0600  Japan        Aug Preliminary Machine Tool Orders \par 0630  India        Weekly WPI (for fuel products and primary \par                    articles) \par 0800  EU           Sep ECB Monthly Bulletin \par 0830  UK           Jul UK T