\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,423 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2010\par 13:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OBAMA TO FORMALLY UNVEIL NEW ECONOMIC STIMULUS IDEAS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 President Barack Obama on Wednesday will formally unveil a mix of business tax cuts and infrastructure programs to boost the economy that are already facing stiff headwinds from executives and Republican lawmakers. (published 1000 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: GERMAN JULY INDUSTRIAL PRODUCTION +0.1% ON MONTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German industrial production crept up 0.1% in July from the previous month, as growth in Europe's largest economy moderated following rapid expansion in the first half of 2010, the economics ministry said Wednesday. (published 1000 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN SCHATZ AUCTION DECENT, PLAYS SAFE-HAVEN ROLE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Renewed sovereign debt fears in some of the euro zone's weaker members prompted investors to flock into a two-year German treasury-note auction Wednesday, with redemptions likely proved as a further demand booster. (published 0944 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ECB'S WEBER: TIGHTER CAPITAL STANDARDS WON'T HURT ECONOMY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tighter standards for banks' capital and liquidity won't hurt the economy, European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governing council member Axel Weber said Wednesday. (published 0803 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ECB'S WEBER: PERIPHERAL STATES WILL PRESERVE MARKET ACCESS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The peripheral states of the euro zone will continue to be able to borrow funds from capital markets despite lingering concerns about their debt levels, European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governing council member Axel Weber said Wednesday. (published 0855 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: GERMAN EXPORT MACHINE SLOWS IN JULY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany's exports fell for the only the second time this year in July, the Federal Statistics Office Destatis said Wednesday, adding substance to expectations of an economic slowdown in Europe's largest economy in the second half of the year. (published 0612 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  STEPS UP \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WARNING AS YEN SURGES \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister made it clear Wednesday the government will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to tame the yen's rises if deemed necessary, but investors brushed aside his strongest warning to date and pushed the yen to a 15-year high. (published 0543 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: UK JULY MANUFACTURING OUTPUT RISES AT 15-YEAR HIGH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K.'s manufacturing sector posted a sixth straight year-on-year increase in July, rising at its fastest rate in 15 and a half years, data showed Wednesday. (published 0840 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FRENCH BUDGET DEFICIT NARROWS TO EUR93.1B AT END-JULY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 France's budget deficit narrowed to EUR93.1 billion on July 31, from EUR108.8 billion a year earlier, the country's budget ministry said Wednesday, confirming the trend for which the government planned in its 2010 budget. (published 0935 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SARKOZY REFUSES TO BACK DOWN ON RAISING RETIREMENT AGE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French president Nicolas Sarkozy Wednesday refused to drop plans to raise the retirement age to 62 from 60 after huge strikes and protests, but he did offer an olive branch to unions on the margins of the reform package. (published 1031 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EU TO LET IRELAND CONTINUE WITH BANK GUARANTEE SCHEME \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European Commission said Wednesday it plans to allow Ireland to extend its bank guarantee scheme beyond September, to ease worries about the health of the nation's financial sector. (published 0838 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ LEAVES ECONOMIC ASSESSMENT UNCHANGED IN SEPT REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan said the nation's economy "shows further signs of moderate recovery" in September, keeping its assessment unchanged for the fourth straight month since upgrading it in May. (published 0509 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BANK OF FRANCE CONFIRMS 3Q GDP EXPECTED UP 0.3% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 France's economy is still expected to expand by 0.3% in the third quarter, the Bank of France said Wednesday, noting that business sentiment in industry was unchanged in August from July, while sentiment in the services sector was off slightly. (published 0645 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GREECE 2Q GDP REVISED DOWN TO -1.8% FROM -1.5% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Greek second quarter gross domestic product was revised down to show a 1.8% fall from the first three months of the year compared with the initial estimate of a 1.5% decline, the country's statistics service said. (published 0934 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: PORTUGAL 2Q ECONOMIC GROWTH REVISED UP \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Portugal's second quarter gross domestic product was revised up to show quarterly growth of 0.3% compared with 0.2% previously, data from the National Statistics Institute, or INE, showed Wednesday. (published 1027 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: UK HOUSE PRICES ROSE FOR SECOND MONTH IN AUG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. house prices rose for the second straight month in August, although at a slower pace, mortgage lender Halifax said Wednesday. (published 0722 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA RAMPS UP JAPAN GOVERNMENT BOND BUYING IN JULY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China ramped up its purchases of Japanese government bonds in July, likely adding to speculation that the growing money flow could be contributing to the yen's recent strength and lower government bond yields. (published 0324 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par IMF WARNS TURKEY ON FISCAL CREDIBILITY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The International Monetary Fund Wednesday became the latest organization to voice concern over the Turkish government's commitment to fiscal discipline, urging Ankara to pass legislation to reduce its budget deficit and limit spending "without delay," and warning that postponement could weaken Ankara's economic credibility. (published 1028 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ GOVERNOR: HAVE BEEN SUPPLYING AMPLE FUNDS TO FINANCIAL FIRMS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa said Wednesday the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been pumping adequate funds into the market, suggesting he doesn't think the BOJ needs to take more easing measures anytime soon. (published 0129 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA GREENS: WON'T BLOCK LABOR PARTY'S MINING TAX PROPOSAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's Greens party won't block the Labor government's plans to impose an additional tax on iron ore and coal mining companies, Greens leader Bob Brown said Wednesday. (published 0656 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA SWAN: INDEPENDENTS PART OF MINING TAX TALKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's Treasurer Wayne Swan said Wednesday the non-party lawmakers who helped the Labor Party secure another term in office will be involved in talks on the final design of a controversial plan to tax the profits of mining companies. (published 0408 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RATES PAUSE PUTS CUSHION UNDER AUSTRALIAN HOUSE LENDING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A recent downturn in lending for Australian housing is bottoming out as a lengthening pause in interest rate hikes puts a cushion under the sector. (published 0308 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par MONEY TALKS: NEW AUSSIE GOVERNMENT WON'T DRIVE THE ECONOMIC BUS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 We may have a new government in Australia, but it isn't in charge of the economy. Australia's growth is being driven by anything but what is happening in Canberra, its political capital. (published 0700 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FOCUS: AUSSIE IS STILL LEADER OF THE PACK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After a bit of a slip, the Aussie should still come out on top. Sure, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  has been a little less hawkish than expected, still keeping rates on hold, and the mining tax that upset many investors looks as if it is here to stay, but the Australian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is still a good bet. (published 0715 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PREVIEW: CHINA AUGUST TRADE SURPLUS LIKELY WIDENED TO $30.0 BLN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's trade surplus likely widened in August, putting further strain on the country's relations with its major trading partners and adding to pressure on Beijing to allow a faster appreciation of the yuan. (published 0727 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: EUR TAKING A POUNDING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: Euro weakness is back on the agenda, and a bear extension towards the Aug. 31 higher low at 1.2625 is likely. The key Aug. 24 reaction low at 1.2588 lies just beneath there, and bulls will be keen to keep this low intact in order to prevent the creation of a downwave equality target at 1.2175 for the short-term. Corrective strength should be limited to the 1.2790 area, and only above there would question the bearish outlook. Weekly chart EUR/USD trend: Range. (published 0613 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: BONDS TO EXTEND GAINS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday BUND: Extends the recovery off the Sep. 3 reaction low at 130.24 to bring the focus onto the 131.96 lower high. Bulls will be targeting the 132.08/12 resistance area - the latter being 61.8% retracement of the 133.29/130.24 bear wave. 130.91 provides the limit for corrective downside, which is protected by 131.16. Weekly chart BUND trend: Range. (published 0623 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : EURO SLIPS; NODA FAILS TO STOP YEN'S RISE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro came under renewed selling pressure Wednesday as worries about European banks and the threat of sovereign default returned to financial markets. (published 0934 GMT) [ 08-09-10 1104GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100908e6980011i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
