\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,134 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2010\par 11:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ECB'S WEBER: TIGHTER CAPITAL STANDARDS WON'T HURT ECONOMY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tighter standards for banks' capital and liquidity won't hurt the economy, European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governing council member Axel Weber said Wednesday. (published 0803 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ECB'S WEBER: PERIPHERAL STATES WILL PRESERVE MARKET ACCESS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The peripheral states of the euro zone will continue to be able to borrow funds from capital markets despite lingering concerns about their debt levels, European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governing council member Axel Weber said Wednesday. (published 0855 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: GERMAN EXPORT MACHINE SLOWS IN JULY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany's exports fell for the only the second time this year in July, the Federal Statistics Office Destatis said Wednesday, adding substance to expectations of an economic slowdown in Europe's largest economy in the second half of the year. (published 0612 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  STEPS UP \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WARNING AS YEN SURGES \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister made it clear Wednesday the government will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to tame the yen's rises if deemed necessary, but investors brushed aside his strongest warning to date and pushed the yen to a 15-year high. (published 0543 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: UK JULY MANUFACTURING OUTPUT RISES AT 15-YEAR HIGH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K.'s manufacturing sector posted a sixth straight year-on-year increase in July, rising at its fastest rate in 15 and a half years, data showed Wednesday. (published 0840 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: FRENCH JULY TRADE GAP WIDENS TO EUR4.18B VS EUR3.72B \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 France's trade deficit widened more than expected in July from a month earlier as imports increased, data released Wednesday by the French customs office showed. (published 0708 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EU TO LET IRELAND CONTINUE WITH BANK GUARANTEE SCHEME \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European Commission said Wednesday it plans to allow Ireland to extend its bank guarantee scheme beyond September, to ease worries about the health of the nation's financial sector. (published 0838 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ LEAVES ECONOMIC ASSESSMENT UNCHANGED IN SEPT REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan said the nation's economy "shows further signs of moderate recovery" in September, keeping its assessment unchanged for the fourth straight month since upgrading it in May. (published 0509 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BANK OF FRANCE CONFIRMS 3Q GDP EXPECTED UP 0.3% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 France's economy is still expected to expand by 0.3% in the third quarter, the Bank of France said Wednesday, noting that business sentiment in industry was unchanged in August from July, while sentiment in the services sector was off slightly. (published 0645 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: UK HOUSE PRICES ROSE FOR SECOND MONTH IN AUG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. house prices rose for the second straight month in August, although at a slower pace, mortgage lender Halifax said Wednesday. (published 0722 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA RAMPS UP JAPAN GOVERNMENT BOND BUYING IN JULY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China ramped up its purchases of Japanese government bonds in July, likely adding to speculation that the growing money flow could be contributing to the yen's recent strength and lower government bond yields. (published 0324 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ GOVERNOR: HAVE BEEN SUPPLYING AMPLE FUNDS TO FINANCIAL FIRMS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa said Wednesday the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been pumping adequate funds into the market, suggesting he doesn't think the BOJ needs to take more easing measures anytime soon. (published 0129 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA GREENS: WON'T BLOCK LABOR PARTY'S MINING TAX PROPOSAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's Greens party won't block the Labor government's plans to impose an additional tax on iron ore and coal mining companies, Greens leader Bob Brown said Wednesday. (published 0656 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA SWAN: INDEPENDENTS PART OF MINING TAX TALKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's Treasurer Wayne Swan said Wednesday the non-party lawmakers who helped the Labor Party secure another term in office will be involved in talks on the final design of a controversial plan to tax the profits of mining companies. (published 0408 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RATES PAUSE PUTS CUSHION UNDER AUSTRALIAN HOUSE LENDING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A recent downturn in lending for Australian housing is bottoming out as a lengthening pause in interest rate hikes puts a cushion under the sector. (published 0308 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA'S BROAD MONEY SUPPLY GROWTH IN JULY SLOWEST IN 11 MONTHS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Growth in South Korea's broadest measure of money supply continued to slow in July, posting its weakest gain in 11 months, Bank of Korea data showed Wednesday. (published 0300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par MONEY TALKS: NEW AUSSIE GOVERNMENT WON'T DRIVE THE ECONO