\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 721 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2010\par 09:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: GERMAN EXPORT MACHINE SLOWS IN JULY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany's exports fell for the only the second time this year in July, the Federal Statistics Office Destatis said Wednesday, adding substance to expectations of an economic slowdown in Europe's largest economy in the second half of the year. (published 0612 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  STEPS UP \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WARNING AS YEN SURGES \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister made it clear Wednesday the government will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to tame the yen's rises if deemed necessary, but investors brushed aside his strongest warning to date and pushed the yen to a 15-year high. (published 0543 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ LEAVES ECONOMIC ASSESSMENT UNCHANGED IN SEPT REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan said the nation's economy "shows further signs of moderate recovery" in September, keeping its assessment unchanged for the fourth straight month since upgrading it in May. (published 0509 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BANK OF FRANCE CONFIRMS 3Q GDP EXPECTED UP 0.3% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 France's economy is still expected to expand by 0.3% in the third quarter, the Bank of France said Wednesday, noting that business sentiment in industry was unchanged in August from July, while sentiment in the services sector was off slightly. (published 0645 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA RAMPS UP JAPAN GOVERNMENT BOND BUYING IN JULY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China ramped up its purchases of Japanese government bonds in July, likely adding to speculation that the growing money flow could be contributing to the yen's recent strength and lower government bond yields. (published 0324 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ GOVERNOR: HAVE BEEN SUPPLYING AMPLE FUNDS TO FINANCIAL FIRMS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa said Wednesday the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been pumping adequate funds into the market, suggesting he doesn't think the BOJ needs to take more easing measures anytime soon. (published 0129 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA GREENS: WON'T BLOCK LABOR PARTY'S MINING TAX PROPOSAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's Greens party won't block the Labor government's plans to impose an additional tax on iron ore and coal mining companies, Greens leader Bob Brown said Wednesday. (published 0656 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA SWAN: INDEPENDENTS PART OF MINING TAX TALKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's Treasurer Wayne Swan said Wednesday the non-party lawmakers who helped the Labor Party secure another term in office will be involved in talks on the final design of a controversial plan to tax the profits of mining companies. (published 0408 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RATES PAUSE PUTS CUSHION UNDER AUSTRALIAN HOUSE LENDING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A recent downturn in lending for Australian housing is bottoming out as a lengthening pause in interest rate hikes puts a cushion under the sector. (published 0308 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA'S BROAD MONEY SUPPLY GROWTH IN JULY SLOWEST IN 11 MONTHS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Growth in South Korea's broadest measure of money supply continued to slow in July, posting its weakest gain in 11 months, Bank of Korea data showed Wednesday. (published 0300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: EUR TAKING A POUNDING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: Euro weakness is back on the agenda, and a bear extension towards the Aug. 31 higher low at 1.2625 is likely. The key Aug. 24 reaction low at 1.2588 lies just beneath there, and bulls will be keen to keep this low intact in order to prevent the creation of a downwave equality target at 1.2175 for the short-term. Corrective strength should be limited to the 1.2790 area, and only above there would question the bearish outlook. Weekly chart EUR/USD trend: Range. (published 0613 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: BONDS TO EXTEND GAINS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday BUND: Extends the recovery off the Sep. 3 reaction low at 130.24 to bring the focus onto the 131.96 lower high. Bulls will be targeting the 132.08/12 resistance area - the latter being 61.8% retracement of the 133.29/130.24 bear wave. 130.91 provides the limit for corrective downside, which is protected by 131.16. Weekly chart BUND trend: Range. (published 0623 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ASIA: ASIAN RESERVES UP IN AUG AS CENTRAL BANKS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Asia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves hit a new all-time high in August, according to Dow Jones Newswires calculations, driven by strong inflows attracted by the region's growth rates and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to contain local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation. (published 0349 GMT) [ 08-09-10 0705GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100908e698000kj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
