\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Hits New 15-Year Low Vs Yen; BOC Awaited\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bradley Davis   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 535 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2010\par 14:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar fell to a fresh 15-year low against the yen overnight before rebounding slightly Wednesday as investors brushed aside an escalating war of words by Japanese officials directed at the rising yen.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to a low of Y83.34, compared with Y83.80 late Tuesday in New York before retracing some of its losses.\par \par \uc2 "Not surprisingly, more rhetoric from Japanese policy makers has been unleashed, though the legitimacy of such talk is still in question until we see some actual action," said Sacha Tihanyi, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Scotia Capital in Toronto.\par \par \uc2 Early Wednesday, the euro was at $1.2684 from $1.2689 late Tuesday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y83.79 from Y83.80, while the euro was at Y106.42 from Y106.33. The U.K. pound was at $1.5462 from $1.5350. The dollar was at CHF1.0098, unchanged from late Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 82.709 from 82.815.\par \par \uc2 Japan Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda was the latest official to warn markets that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to act, specifically raising the specter of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time.\par \par \uc2 "We will take decisive steps--which of course include \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 --when it becomes necessary," Noda said.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the comments showed greater seriousness among \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's authorities toward the possibility of direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , something they haven't done since 2004. But there was still skepticism as to whether \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would actually happen.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, investors were zeroed in on the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 , which at its 9 a.m. EDT meeting is expected to raise key interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Since the bank's July meeting, the U.S. and Canadian economic outlooks have deteriorated, Scotia analysts said, which has led some to suggest the bank may stand pat on rates.\par \par \uc2 Scotia said, however, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely go ahead with an interest rate increase, "in part due to its desire to contain the household sector by moving away from emergency level interest rates."\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the announcement, the U.S. dollar was at C$1.0474, from C$1.0475 late Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The market is mostly positioned for a 25-basis-point rate increase from the bank today, "but no one is pounding the drum loudly," said Douglas Porter, deputy chief economist at BMO Capital Markets.\par \par \uc2 The BOC's press release will be closely parsed for hints about possible future rate increases.\par \par \uc2 If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raises rates by 25 basis points as expected, that would be a third consecutive rise and would hoist the target rate to 1.00%. In June, the BOC became the first \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations to raise interest rates since the global economic crisis took hold two years ago.\par \par \uc2 -By Bradley Davis, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2654; bradley.davis@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Karen Johnson in Toronto and Kosaku Narioka in Tokyo contributed to this article.) [ 09-08-10 0842ET ]\par \par \uc2 1033 1074