\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Philippine Shares End At 3-Year High; Banks Lead\b0\par\par\uc2 1,567 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2010\par 06:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0448 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares ended higher, staging late rebound on back of last-minute buying of banking stocks. Benchmark PSEi closed 0.8% higher at 3804.73, a 3-year high, near closing level on Oct. 19, 2007, last time the index was at the 3800 level (a blast in a Makati shopping mall that killed several people subsequently knocked index off 3800 level, down to 3600). "Foreign funds continue to flow into the market. Today, they bought banks," says Joseph Roxas, president of Eagle Equities; volume heavy. Leading advancers are Metropolitan Bank & Trust (MBT.PH), up 0.1% to PHP70.10, Philippine National Bank (PNB.PH), up 7.7% to PHP47.60, Banco de Oro (BDO.PH), up 3.2% to PHP57.90, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of the Philippine Islands\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BPI.PH), up 3.8% to PHP55. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0447 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan August economic data (CPI, Exports) indicate economic fundamentals remain strong, but growth momentum moderating, stabilizing to sustainable rates from record pace between 2H 2009 and 1H 2010, says Macquarie. "Price and monetary indicators show still-tepid inflation and abundant market liquidity. We expect the CBC (Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) to continue its gradual withdrawal of monetary stimulus with another 12.5 basis point rate hike," house adds.(paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0444 GMT [Dow Jones] Investors continue to avoid Singapore shares after early selloff, with STI surrendering almost all its 1.1% gain so far this week. Index off 1.0% at 3006.79 midday. If 3000 gives way for rest of session, next support expected at Friday's 2977 intraday low. Market breadth slightly negative, with overall volume thin at 673.4 million shares worth S$545.8 million. Small caps, penny shares fare better than blue chips, with FTSE ST Small Cap Index +0.3%, FTSE ST Catalist Index +2.4%. "Traders are zooming in on the lower liners and avoiding the large caps. The overall pullback is not too bad, but the market clearly needs fresh leads to bring it back up and push it higher," says local house trader. Lower liners in play include \uc2 China Hongxing Sports\uc2  (BR9.SG), +8.1% at S$0.20, Hisaka (FV2.SG), +12.9% at S$0.35, Z-Obee (D5N.SG), +3.8% at S$0.41, Biosensors (B20.SG), +2.4% at S$0.86. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0443 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI down 0.3% at 1430.01 midday, tracking declines in U.S., regional stocks; investors also taking profit ahead of long-weekend, dealers say. "We believe the current consolidation will likely continue for the rest of the week," RHB Research says; pegs support at 1422 (10-day moving average), resistance at 1441 (2010 high). Financial markets will be closed from midday tomorrow for Idul Fitri, will re-open Monday. Among decliners, Top Glove (7113.KU) down 3.9% at MYR5.61, \uc2 MMC Corp\uc2  (2194.KU) down 2.5% at MYR2.71, AMMB (1015.KU) down 1.7% at MYR5.83; among gainers, Plenitude (5075.KU) +10.3% at MYR4.28 after announcing 1-for-1 bonus issue, Bina Puri (5932.KU) +5.3% at MYR1.19 after unveiling MYR500 million mall project. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0440 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end down 0.5% at 458.44 as local retailers continue to take profits after key index +8.2% over past 7 sessions. "Some blue chips are still up by more than 10% month-to-date," says trader. Tips strong technical support at 450; thinks local institutions, foreigners may buy amid growing expectations government will soon come up with measures that will help boost cash flows. State media reported yesterday money supply as of end-August rose 13% from end of last year, but lower than government forecast of 20% rise. Volume moderate with 56 million shares valued at VND1.48 trillion exchanging hands. Among most active, SSI (SSI.VH) ends down 2.4% at VND28,500 after rising 12.6% over past 7 sessions, Investment & Trading of Real Eastate JSC (ITC.VH) down 0.4% at VND25,700 after 19.5% rise over same period. REE (REE.VH) end down 2.3% at VND17,100 after gaining 10.3% over same period. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0434 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD higher as local banks cover short positions, while weak local stocks lend support, says senior trader at Singapore bank. HSI last down 1.4%. Tips pair in 7.7700-7.7730 band near-term. Players turn risk averse, thus supporting USD, "but I've spotted a U.S. bank selling US$200 million near HK$7.7730," trader says. Pair last at 7.7721 vs 7.7685 yesterday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0429 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei off 2.2% at 9024.48, briefly dipping below 9000, as jitters over earnings of exporter firms spread after USD/JPY (now 83.40) hits fresh 15-year low and EUR/JPY weakens to 2-week low (now 105.81). "There are fears that the yen will dramatically strengthen from here," says Yutaka Shiraki, senior equity strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities, adding USD/JPY may fall below 80. "Markets will keep trying until there is \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." If Nikkei closes below 9000, traders say next support level pegged at 8750. 32/33 Topix subindexes lower. Consumer finance shares underperform; Takefuji (8564.TO) off 6.8% at Y179, Credit Saison (8253.TO) down 5.5% at Y1,058, dragged lower by broader concerns about health of Europe's banking sector. \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) off 2.4% at Y2,455, \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) down 2.5% at Y2,862 on yen worries. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0424 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD off tad, weighed by weakening NZD/AUD, says \uc2 HIFX\uc2  director of trading Mike Hollows; "There is some sort of knee-jerk reaction of buying the Aussie after the finality of the election outcome." NZD/USD last 0.7167 vs 0.7190 early this morning. Says market relatively quiet with no data to give direction in short term; "The Kiwi looks pretty stable in broad terms, it is just testing the support level of 0.7160, which it broke out of last week." Tips resistance at 0.7195. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0416 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds higher as wave of global risk aversion boosts demand for fixed income assets, while expectations of policy rate hike ease due to ongoing concerns about tepid global economic recovery, says trader with foreign bank; 7.80% 2020 bond at 99.17 vs 98.99 last close. "It looks like this is the beginning of another bout of risk aversion, and that should translate into a correction in bond yields," says trader. Expects yield on 10-year benchmark to correct to 7.90% vs last 7.92%. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones] 30-year JGB auction comes in weaker-than-expected amid lingering concerns over Japan's fiscal discipline, but demand for superlong-term debt could grow again as "fears are intensifying that the sharp appreciation of the yen will drag down Japan's economy further," says Masashi Shimominami, bond market analyst at Mizuho Securities. Lowest accepted price of 99.75, lower than trader expectations at 99.90-100.00, but bid-to-cover ratio 4.15, better than previous 4.06, showing signs that "an upward trend in superlong-term yields may end soon." (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0407 GMT [Dow Jones] Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves hit new all-time high in August, according to Dow Jones Newswires calculations, driven by strong inflows, attracted by region's growth rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to contain local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\