he Aussie after the finality of the election outcome." NZD/USD last 0.7167 vs 0.7190 early this morning. Says market relatively quiet with no data to give direction in short term; "The Kiwi looks pretty stable in broad terms, it is just testing the support level of 0.7160, which it broke out of last week." Tips resistance at 0.7195. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0416 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds higher as wave of global risk aversion boosts demand for fixed income assets, while expectations of policy rate hike ease due to ongoing concerns about tepid global economic recovery, says trader with foreign bank; 7.80% 2020 bond at 99.17 vs 98.99 last close. "It looks like this is the beginning of another bout of risk aversion, and that should translate into a correction in bond yields," says trader. Expects yield on 10-year benchmark to correct to 7.90% vs last 7.92%. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones] 30-year JGB auction comes in weaker-than-expected amid lingering concerns over Japan's fiscal discipline, but demand for superlong-term debt could grow again as "fears are intensifying that the sharp appreciation of the yen will drag down Japan's economy further," says Masashi Shimominami, bond market analyst at Mizuho Securities. Lowest accepted price of 99.75, lower than trader expectations at 99.90-100.00, but bid-to-cover ratio 4.15, better than previous 4.06, showing signs that "an upward trend in superlong-term yields may end soon." (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0407 GMT [Dow Jones] Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves hit new all-time high in August, according to Dow Jones Newswires calculations, driven by strong inflows, attracted by region's growth rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to contain local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation. Reserves held by 11 key Asian central banks, excluding China's, amounted to US$2.875 trillion at end August, up from US$2.861 trillion at the end of July, a 0.5% rise. The increase came even as euro-denominated reserves lost value, with EUR dropping 2.8% vs USD in August. That shows countries across region keep buying large amounts of USD in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Analysts say trend likely to continue, especially in Asian economies most exposed to flagging export demand from developed world. See =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Asian Reserves Up In Aug As Central Banks \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0405 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY hits fresh 15-year low of 83.34 as Japanese life insurance firms sell, taking cue from weak Tokyo stocks (Nikkei last down 2.4%). Pair last at 83.43; stop-loss selling orders could be triggered any moment, probably near 83.20-83.30, says senior dealer at major bank in Tokyo. Tips support at 83.00 for now. "Unless \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar has no choice but to keep falling," he says. Adds, Japanese investors also selling cross units; AUD/JPY falls to its lowest level since Sept. 1. Cross last at 75.99, may fall to 75.50. EUR/JPY last at 105.80, lowest level hit since Aug. 24. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0402 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR eases as stock-related inflows weigh on pair despite weak EUR, regional stocks, says trader with private bank; pair at 46.81 vs 46.84 late Tuesday. "Monday was a holiday in the U.S., so stock market inflows that would otherwise have come in yesterday are hitting the market now," says trader. But pressure may ease as upbeat USD globally supports pair, weak local stocks may also draw buying interest. Tips pair to tread 46.70-46.90 band in session. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0402 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex down 0.4% at 18,567.58, consolidating after 2.3% rally in previous 2 sessions. Market breadth negative with 24 of 30 index issues lower; ICICI Bank (532174.BY), down 0.8% at INR1,028, RIL (500325.BY), down 0.6% at INR956.30 leading losses. "The opening is on expected lines, but still outperforming most Asian peers. Select banks after recent outperformance are seeing some correction while cement plays continue to see buying momentum for the second day on improved demand and price outlook," says dealer at local brokerage. Adds, index tipped in 18,500-18,700 range for session. Bharti (532454.BY) down 1.2% at INR339, \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) off 1.2% at INR569.60, SBI (500112.BY) off 0.8% at INR2,813.85. Gainers include ACC (500410.BY) +2.4% at INR986, following 6.7% rise last session, \uc2 Tata Power\uc2  (500400.BY) +0.6% at INR1,278 ahead of annual shareholders meet. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0358 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares still down midday as property developers, banks decline amid concerns over possibility Beijing may tighten grip on housing prices. Shanghai Composite Index down 0.5% at 2685.56, tipped to stay in 2650-2750 range rest of session. "There has been a lot of tough talk recently from the administration on keeping housing prices in check, stoking fears of further tightening, and today's weak property sector is dragging banks," says Huang Bin at Gold State Securities. Adds, momentum in rare earths, other mining sectors after Beijing's call for faster consolidation in country's heavy, polluting industries may help index pare gains in afternoon. \uc2 China Vanke\uc2  (000002.SZ) down 2.3% at CNY8.58, \uc2 Poly Real Estate Group\uc2  (600048.SH) down 2.9% at CNY11.79. ICBC (601398.SH) down 1.0% at CNY4.09. Minmetals Development (600058.SH) +10% daily trading limit at CNY20.12. Jiangxi Copper (600362.SH) +7.2% at CNY34.42, MCC (601618.SH) +0.5% at CNY4.10 up on restart of construction in Afghan mine. Shenzhen Composite Index +0.1% at 1187.62. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0350 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY stays up midday on sustained USD demand from state-owned banks, higher dollar-yuan central parity rate; on OTC, USD/CNY at 6.7977 vs 6.7914 last close; tipped to consolidate in 6.7940-6.8030 band for session. "Large, (state-owned) Beijing-based banks have been buying dollars, likely on behalf of clients," says Shanghai-based trader at Japanese bank; adds, USD/CNY likely to stay rangebound in near term. "The yuan appreciation expectation has weakened of late, given small moves of onshore dollar-yuan spot rates since June 19," he says. "That has led offshore NDF traders to tone down their yuan appreciation view for now." Offshore, 1-year USD/CNY NDF at 6.7120/6.7150 vs 6.7063/6.7113 late Tuesday. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0346 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan exports +26.6% on-year in August but contracted 1.0% on sequential, seasonally adjusted basis, says RBS economist Erik Lueth. Adds, adjusted figures show exports to Europe, Asian neighbors weak. "With exports contracting on a sequential basis, weak domestic demand, and still significant slack in the labor market CBC (Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is unlikely to continue tightening (except maybe for another token hike in September)," he says. Reckons export underperformance relative to Korea will strengthen government's resolve to keep TWD down.(paul.mozur@dowjones.com) [ 09-08-10 0034ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100908e6980004p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
