\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Hits New 15-Yr Low; Insurance Firms Sell\b0\par\par\uc2 1,567 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2010\par 06:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0405 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY hits fresh 15-year low of 83.34 as Japanese life insurance firms sell, taking cue from weak Tokyo stocks (Nikkei last down 2.4%). Pair last at 83.43; stop-loss selling orders could be triggered any moment, probably near 83.20-83.30, says senior dealer at major bank in Tokyo. Tips support at 83.00 for now. "Unless \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the dollar has no choice but to keep falling," he says. Adds, Japanese investors also selling cross units; AUD/JPY falls to its lowest level since Sept. 1. Cross last at 75.99, may fall to 75.50. EUR/JPY last at 105.80, lowest level hit since Aug. 24. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0402 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR eases as stock-related inflows weigh on pair despite weak EUR, regional stocks, says trader with private bank; pair at 46.81 vs 46.84 late Tuesday. "Monday was a holiday in the U.S., so stock market inflows that would otherwise have come in yesterday are hitting the market now," says trader. But pressure may ease as upbeat USD globally supports pair, weak local stocks may also draw buying interest. Tips pair to tread 46.70-46.90 band in session. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0402 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex down 0.4% at 18,567.58, consolidating after 2.3% rally in previous 2 sessions. Market breadth negative with 24 of 30 index issues lower; ICICI Bank (532174.BY), down 0.8% at INR1,028, RIL (500325.BY), down 0.6% at INR956.30 leading losses. "The opening is on expected lines, but still outperforming most Asian peers. Select banks after recent outperformance are seeing some correction while cement plays continue to see buying momentum for the second day on improved demand and price outlook," says dealer at local brokerage. Adds, index tipped in 18,500-18,700 range for session. Bharti (532454.BY) down 1.2% at INR339, \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) off 1.2% at INR569.60, SBI (500112.BY) off 0.8% at INR2,813.85. Gainers include ACC (500410.BY) +2.4% at INR986, following 6.7% rise last session, \uc2 Tata Power\uc2  (500400.BY) +0.6% at INR1,278 ahead of annual shareholders meet. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0358 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares still down midday as property developers, banks decline amid concerns over possibility Beijing may tighten grip on housing prices. Shanghai Composite Index down 0.5% at 2685.56, tipped to stay in 2650-2750 range rest of session. "There has been a lot of tough talk recently from the administration on keeping housing prices in check, stoking fears of further tightening, and today's weak property sector is dragging banks," says Huang Bin at Gold State Securities. Adds, momentum in rare earths, other mining sectors after Beijing's call for faster consolidation in country's heavy, polluting industries may help index pare gains in afternoon. \uc2 China Vanke\uc2  (000002.SZ) down 2.3% at CNY8.58, \uc2 Poly Real Estate Group\uc2  (600048.SH) down 2.9% at CNY11.79. ICBC (601398.SH) down 1.0% at CNY4.09. Minmetals Development (600058.SH) +10% daily trading limit at CNY20.12. Jiangxi Copper (600362.SH) +7.2% at CNY34.42, MCC (601618.SH) +0.5% at CNY4.10 up on restart of construction in Afghan mine. Shenzhen Composite Index +0.1% at 1187.62. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0350 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY stays up midday on sustained USD demand from state-owned banks, higher dollar-yuan central parity rate; on OTC, USD/CNY at 6.7977 vs 6.7914 last close; tipped to consolidate in 6.7940-6.8030 band for session. "Large, (state-owned) Beijing-based banks have been buying dollars, likely on behalf of clients," says Shanghai-based trader at Japanese bank; adds, USD/CNY likely to stay rangebound in near term. "The yuan appreciation expectation has weakened of late, given small moves of onshore dollar-yuan spot rates since June 19," he says. "That has led offshore NDF traders to tone down their yuan appreciation view for now." Offshore, 1-year USD/CNY NDF at 6.7120/6.7150 vs 6.7063/6.7113 late Tuesday. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0346 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan exports +26.6% on-year in August but contracted 1.0% on sequential, seasonally adjusted basis, says RBS economist Erik Lueth. Adds, adjusted figures show exports to Europe, Asian neighbors weak. "With exports contracting on a sequential basis, weak domestic demand, and still significant slack in the labor market CBC (Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is unlikely to continue tightening (except maybe for another token hike in September)," he says. Reckons export underperformance relative to Korea will strengthen government's resolve to keep TWD down.(paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0345 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds higher in thin trade as following better-than-expected MOF bond auction results, says local dealer. Adds eyes on fund inflow due to continued strong baht but upside may be limited following recent gains. Thai government October 2017 bond sold at average accepted yield of 2.8478%, with 1.6X bid coverage ratio. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.69%/2.65% vs 2.70%/2.65% late yesterday, November 2016 quoted at 2.80%/2.76% vs 2.85%/2.77%, June 2019 quoted at 2.90%/2.82% vs 2.90%/2.85%. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0341 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares last down 0.7% at 457.21, off 453.26 earlier, with local retail investors continued profit-taking after recent gains. "The correction is expected to be mild and short-lived because institutions are waiting to buy after the government last week raise this year's GDP growth target to 6.7% from previous 6.5%," says trader. Adds, more players also entering to buy after state media reported \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will boost money supply to meet GDP growth target. Volume average with 45 million shares valued at VND1.15 trillion traded so far. Among decliners, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) down 3.0% at VND33,000, SSI (SSI.VH) down 2.7% at VND28,400, Itaco (ITA.VH) down 1.5% at VND22,300. Trader tips immediate support at 457. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0330 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 likely to remain a tad lower on offshore weakness, says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Bryon Burke; "it's been affected by overseas sentiment this morning, albeit we've held up reasonably well given the last two days' increases. The New Zealand market has been rowing its own boat to a certain degree." Adds Sky City (SKC.NZ), \uc2 Air New Zealand\uc2  (AIR.NZ) biggest losers on going ex-dividend. Says positive sign that Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) flat at NZ$8.15 after strong rally following earthquake; "everyone is trying to predict the economic impact of the earthquake. Obviously it's negative in the short term but helps economic growth in the long term." NZX-50 off 0.3% at 3165.05. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0325 GMT [Dow Jones] Lowest accepted price at Y600 billion auction of 30-year JGBs with coupon of 2.0% likely 99.90-100.00, say traders; latest bond traded in morning at 106.957 for yield of 1.930%. Some analysts say auction may not go smoothly as concerns grow over Japan's fiscal discipline, but others say demand for superlong-term debt is solid given worries about economic impact of yen strength. Auction results due at 0345 GMT. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0320 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex likely to open 0.4%-0.6% lower, weighed by losses in regional markets, says local dealer; benchmark index may also succumb to profit-taking pressure following over 420 points rally in previous 2 sessions. Adds, stretched valuations in most index stocks after over 12% rally since June 9, lack of fresh domestic triggers this week will likely weigh. Says, banks, select technology shares could lead decline. Tips immediate support at 18,500 as undertone remains positive on strong liquidity, reckons any downside could be used by long-term funds to buy into fundamentally strong companies. Sensex ended +0.5% at 18,645.06 last session. (gurdev.singh@dowjones)\par \par \uc2 0313 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares last down 0.3% at 921.32 in active trade, tracking falls in overseas bourses on renewed concerns over European economy. With Thai market having rallied in recent weeks, consolidation mode normally kicks in, says Finansia Syrus Securities analyst Somchai Anektaweepon; adds downside likely supported by bargain hunting as THB remains strong vs USD. Shares tipped in 915-930 range. Among top actives, PTT (PTT.TH) down 1.4% at THB288, \uc2 Krung Thai Bank\uc2  (KTB.THB) down 0.7% at THB15, Banpu (BANPU.TH) down 0.6% at THB622, \uc2 Advanced Info Service\uc2  (ADVANC.TH) flat at THB96.50. Bucking trend, \uc2 Thanachart Capital\uc2  (TCAP.TH) +4.4% at THB36 on optimism over strong earnings after completing merger with \uc2 Siam City Bank\uc2  (SCIB.TH). (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com) [ 09-08-10 0005ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100908e6980004f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
