\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Asian Shares Drop; Yen Strength Hurts Japan Exporters\b0\par\par\uc2 1,151 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2010\par 06:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds information, quotes, updates/adds market levels)\par \par \uc2 By Colin Ng and Leslie Shaffer\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian stock markets were mostly lower Wednesday after Wall Street declined amid concerns over the health of European banks, with the region's financial stocks getting hit, while exporter shares dragged the Tokyo market lower as the yen touched a fresh 15-year high against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei Share Average was down 2.4%, Australia's S&P/ASX 200 slipped 0.7% and South Korea's Kospi Composite shed 0.9%. Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index lost 1.5% and China's Shanghai Composite slid 0.5%.\par \par \uc2 Dow Jones Industrial Average futures were down 27 points in screen trade.\par \par \uc2 Financial shares around the region lost ground, tracking losses in their European and U.S. counterparts, as investors' attention refocused on the fiscal health of various European nations, especially Ireland, Portugal and Greece, after The Wall Street Journal Tuesday reported that stress tests imposed on European banks earlier this year understated some lenders' holdings of potentially risky government debt.\par \par \uc2 In Sydney, \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  dropped 1.6%, Japan's \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Financial\uc2  shed 1.7%, \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's Hong Kong-listed shares shed 0.4% and Korea's Shinhan Financial was down 2.0%.\par \par \uc2 "Investors have quickly forgotten the disbelief and skepticism when the U.S. bank stress tests results were released in May 2009," said NRA Capital Chairman Kevin Scully, and added that the latest stress-test concerns were over-rated.\par \par \uc2 He said the outcome of the U.S. bank stress test enabled lenders to recapitalize using private-sector resources and led to a rally in U.S. financial stocks. "I expect the same will apply to the EU banks as well."\par \par \uc2 Japan's losses were broad-based, with 32 of the Topix's 33 subindexes weakening, on continued concern over the yen's strength, despite Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda's comments Wednesday that the government will take "decisive" steps against the rising yen if necessary, and that those measures included \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It was the first time Noda has mentioned that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a policy option.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's situation is particularly grim amid the possibility the yen may strengthen further," said Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets.\par \par \uc2 "Expectations are slim that the government will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets" unless the U.S. dollar falls below Y83, he added, with Prime Minister Naoto Kan unlikely to take action on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  front before the ruling Democratic Party of Japan's presidential election next week.\par \par \uc2 Among exporters, \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  dropped 2.4%, \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  lost 3.1% and Canon lost 2.1%.\par \par \uc2 "If the yen continues to strengthen on the back of government inaction, there will be further concerns that companies will need to significantly lower their profit forecasts," said Yutaka Yoshii, general manager at \uc2 Mito Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Advantest dropped 4.4%, hit by the stronger yen and a Nikkei report the company is likely to book an operating profit exceeding Y10 billion for the current fiscal year, below the latest Quick analyst consensus forecast of around Y17 billion profit.\par \par \uc2 In Sydney, some strength in defensive stocks supported the market. "That's a big sell down in the region, but it's probably only a one day influence. Quite frankly, I think the Australian equities market looks good. It's trying hard and there are plenty of stocks that are up today," said Southern Cross Equities director Charlie Aitken.\par \par \uc2 Foster's Group rose 4.8% after rejecting an unsolicited bid for its wine assets from a private equity firm, which valued the unit at A$2.3 billion-A$2.7 billion; it is pushing ahead with a demerger of its beer and wine divisions.\par \par \uc2 Telstra\uc2  was up 1.4% in anticipation of National Broadband Network-related payments after the Labor government retained power Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Aitken said the market was being restrained by selling from Asian funds making room for China Mobile shares after \uc2 Vodafone Group\uc2  sold its 3.2% stake in the Chinese telecom company for GBP4.3 billion. In Hong Kong, China Mobile shares dropped 3.7%, although analysts said the deal had little effect on the company's fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors were selling technology stocks in Seoul, said MiraeAsset Securities analyst Lee Jin-woo. "But since they had been snatching up tech issues for the past three days, I don't see (the selling) as a negative sign" for the overall market, he added.\par \par \uc2 Investors were watching for the Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate decision Thursday. But "even if the Bank of Korea hikes rates, it won't hurt the stock market as investors might think it means the Korean economy is in good shape," said \uc2 SK Securities\uc2 ' Choi Seong-lak.\par \par \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  dropped 1.5% after its chip division chief said Tuesday an oversupply in DRAM chips may occur early next year if global demand for personal computers slows further. Chip maker \uc2 Hynix Semiconductor\uc2  was down 4.0%.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  added 2.0% on improved domestic and U.S. market shares in August and \uc2 Kia Motor\uc2  tacked on 1.8% after reporting strong domestic sales of new models.\par \par \uc2 Among other markets, New Zealand's NZX-50 was off 0.3%, Singapore's Straits Times Index shed 1.0%, Malaysia's KLCI lost 0.3%, Taiwan's Taiex edged down 0.6%, India's Sensex fell 0.4% and Thailand's SET index was down 0.4%. But Philippine shares added 0.1%. Indonesia's stock market was closed.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the yen extended Tuesday's advance against the dollar and the euro. The dollar hit a fresh 15-year low of Y83.34 and was last at Y83.43, from Y83.80 late in New York trade. The euro was at Y105.84, from Y106.33 in New York and at $1.2689, from $1.2687.\par \par \uc2 Lead September Japanese government bond futures were up 0.21 to 141.76 points, with the yield on the 10-year cash JGB down one basis point at 1.130%.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was at $1,256.90 per troy ounce, up $1.70 from New York, with October Nymex crude oil futures down 31 cents at $73.78 per barrel on Globex.\par \par \uc2 -Colin Ng, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4140; colin.ng@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 08-09-10 0415GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1106 4125 5037 62560   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100908e698000al\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
