\par\par \b \uc2 Q+A-Will  \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervene \highlight\uc2  on crosses? Sterilise action?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Hideyuki Sano and Tetsushi Kajimoto   \par \uc2 870 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2010\par 09:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 8 (Reuters) - Japanese authorities may consider buying \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  other than the dollar for any yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but many traders think they would stick to the traditional approach of buying the greenback.\par \par \uc2 Caution over possible Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is rising in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as the yen <JPY=> struck a 15-year high of 83.34 against the dollar, also raising questions on whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would sterilise any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or leave the yen in the market.\par \par \uc2 While Japanese officials have stepped up their warnings that they may take action, most analysts think the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely hold fire until the yen rises to around a record high of 79.75 yen per dollar.\par \par \uc2 WHAT'S THE PROBLEM WITH BUYING DOLLARS?\par \par \uc2 Buying the dollar could cause a headache for Japan.\par \par \uc2 Japan would probably increase its holding of U.S. Treasuries with dollars it buys. But that could hurt the dollar further as a fall in U.S. Treasury yields would further shrink their spread over Japanese yields -- one of the main contributors to the dollar's slide against the yen.\par \par \uc2 SO WOULD JAPAN BUY DOLLARS?\par \par \uc2 Probably. Japanese authorities would likely stick to its traditional approach of buying the dollar for the yen, the most traded yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair with a daily average of $568 billion -- 14 percent of the entire \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Before ceasing its last bout of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March 2004, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  often stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stem yen strength by making sizable dollar purchases, except for a few occasions where it picked up euros.\par \par \uc2 A small opposition party has floated the idea of buying Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as Korean won, against the yen.\par \par \uc2 But traders write off the idea as unrealistic because Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are far less liquid than the dollar. There is also a risk that such steps could be seen by Asian countries as beggar-thy-neighbour policies -- especially with China seen as holding down the yuan's value.\par \par \uc2 The MOF does not disclose the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  makeup of its $1 trillion reserves, but a large chunk is believed to be in dollars because that is where \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been concentrated in the past.\par \par \uc2 WOULD \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2  CONTINUE TO BUY TREASURIES?\par \par \uc2 Yes, traders think a large part of any dollars bought by Japan would be invested in Treasuries. As of 2008, the only time when the MOF made a partial disclosure on its foreign reserves, nearly 70 percent of its securities holding were in sovereign bonds.\par \par \uc2 Some market players say that could undermine Japan's own efforts to weaken the yen because dollar/yen has had a strong correlation with U.S. bond yields in recent months and Japan's buying of Treasuries could push down U.S. yields, and hence dollar/yen.\par \par \uc2 But market players say Japan would regard any such fallout as a necessary cost to achieve a wider good.\par \par \uc2 HOW MUCH WOULD \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2  SPEND ON \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent 35 trillion yen ($416 billion), roughly seven percent of Japan's GDP, in the 15 months from January 2003 to March 2004, its last spell of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Many market players expect smaller \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  now given doubts over whether Japan, or any government, could control the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market where $4 trillion changes hands every day. [ID:nLDE67U26Q]\par \par \uc2 An apparent failure by the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 , which spent money worth 19 percent of its economy in franc-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to stem the franc's gains is likely to be making Tokyo think better of large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Despite its efforts, the franc has soared to a record high versus the euro <EURCHF=R>.\par \par \uc2 Still, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  increased the borrowing limit for its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by 5 trillion yen in the budget for this fiscal year to March 2011, making room to borrow about 35 trillion yen at maximum for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The decision to lift the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  borrowing ceiling was the first in six years this year, showing the concern about persistent gains in the yen.\par \par \uc2 WILL \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2  STERILISE \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Technically, it will. The MOF, which decides on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and uses the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  as its agent, must borrow the yen from markets -- thus soaking up yen cash from the market, to finance its yen selling.\par \par \uc2 However, many market players think part of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2003-2004 was effectively unsterilised as the BOJ offset such sterilisation by pumping yen cash into the banking system via its previous quantitative easing policy.\par \par \uc2 Some market players suspect the BOJ, under pressure from the government and markets for more measures to ease the pain from a higher yen, might take additional easing steps in tandem with MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This could effectively be considered a form of non-sterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the BOJ never made it clear during its previous experiment with quantitative easing whether its policy was intended to offset MOF sterilisation.\par \par \uc2 Thus there is a chance that BOJ Governor Masaaki Shirakawa might inherit that ambiguous approach. ($1=84.12 Yen) (Editing by Eric Burroughs and Joseph Radford)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Q+A)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|U|J|NAT|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100908e698000ib\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
