\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Bank Concerns Hit Euro; Shirakawa Lifts Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Hastings   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 656 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2010\par 11:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The euro is down Tuesday after the Wall Street Journal reported that European bank stress tests underestimated the exposure of some major banks to sovereign debt.\par \par \uc2 The report helped to widen the bond spreads on peripheral debtors and knocked European stock markets lower as another wave of euro zone jitters hit the market.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar, meanwhile, got hit by the news that the country's Labor Party has finally been able to claim victory in the general election over a week ago and that the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  left interest rates as well as the outlook for the economy unchanged.\par \par \uc2 See how the Aussie fell:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4548"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4548}}}\par \par \uc2 The dollar came under renewed selling pressure against the yen after Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Masaaki Shirakawa essentially ruled out any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the yen's advance in the near term.\par \par \uc2 Although Shirakawa said that no policy option was ruled out, he admitted that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot control exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 This helped to lower fears that the Bank of Japan will enter the market any time soon, despite the growing pressure on Japanese exporters from yen strength.\par \par \uc2 New concerns about the ability of European banks to weather the financial crisis came after the WSJ story highlighted once again the weaknesses of the stress tests.\par \par \uc2 Raghav Subbarao, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in London, said that the stress tests had been one of the main reasons that widening peripheral spreads in the euro zone hadn't hurt the euro much previously.\par \par \uc2 "From this point of view, it is not surprising that the doubts raised about the validity of the stress tests are weighing on the euro and also on other risk-correlated \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Subbarao said.\par \par \uc2 Another "risk-correlated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fall was the Australian dollar. The Aussie declined despite news that Labor leader Julia Gillard was finally able to claim victory should remove political uncertainty hanging over the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as this probably means that the mining tax that has been worrying investors will remain on the books.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  left its rates unchanged at 4.5% and offered little change to its previous outlook for the Australian economy. However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did express some concern about U.S. growth later this year and European growth next year.\par \par \uc2 Despite the overall deterioration in market sentiment, the pound was able to hold its ground after some recent heavy losses. Sterling benefited largely from the latest BRC retail sales report showing a 1.0% rise in the year in like-for-like terms. This was better than the 0.5% increase for July but appeared to reflect particularly weak August sales last year.\par \par \uc2 It remains to be seen whether the current trends in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets will be sustained once trading in New York opens and U.S. markets return after the Labor Day holiday.\par \par \uc2 By midmorning, the euro had fallen to $1.2767 from $1.2879 late Monday in New York, according to EBS. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is down at Y107.19 from Y108.51.\par \par \uc2 The dollar is down at Y83.96 from Y84.22 and at CHF1.0124 from CHF1.0152.\par \par \uc2 The pound is essentially flat at $1.5370 from $.5372 while the Aussie has fallen to $0.9114 from $0.9170.\par \par \uc2 By Nicholas Hastings, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9493; nick.hastings@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 07-09-10 0919GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1035 28038 4163 44462   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100907e697000sg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
