\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: European Markets Gain, Led By Utilities\b0\par\par\uc2 2,193 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2010\par 00:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 U.S. markets were closed Monday for the Labor Day Holiday.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10447.93 +127.83  +1.24% \par Nasdaq     2233.75  +33.74  +1.53% \par S&P 500    1104.51  +14.41  +1.32% \par FTSE 100   5439.19  +11.04  +0.20% \par Xetra DAX  6155.04  +20.42  +0.33% \par  \par USD/JPY       84.15-20     +0.02% \par Range         84.49-84.05 \par EUR/USD       1.2874-76    -0.12% \par Range         1.2918-1.2866 \par AUD/USD       0.9173-78    +0.22% \par Range         0.9182-0.9147 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    11.56/11.94% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    10.40/10.80% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    12.00/12.30% \par  \par London PM Gold Fixing: $1249.00  +$8.50 \par ICE Brent Crude:         $76.72  +$0.05 \par  \par  \par EUROPEAN SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 European stock markets closed marginally higher Monday, adding to payrolls-inspired gains from the previous session, with utilities stocks among the top performers after a broker upgrade and a German decision to extend the life of nuclear-power stations.\par \par \uc2 The Stoxx Europe 600 Index gained 0.2% to 260.94. The index has rallied nearly 4% this month and is now up around 3% since the start of the year.\par \par \uc2 Among the main regional indexes, the French CAC 40 rose 0.3% to 3,684.73 and the U.K. FTSE 100 index gained 0.2% to 5,439.19. The German DAX 30 index added 0.3% at 6,155.04.\par \par \uc2 Trading was thin across the continent as U.S. stock markets were closed Monday for the Labor Day holiday.\par \par \uc2 Bernard McAlinden, a strategist at NCB Stockbrokers, said markets had been "technically set up for a bounce" in the past few sessions after worries about the U.S. economy sparked sharp losses in the preceding couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 McAlinden said there was "nothing great" about any of the data from the U.S. Friday, but added that he's not expecting a double-dip recession in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Utilities stocks were the biggest gainers Monday, with German electricity companies \uc2 E.On AG\uc2  and \uc2 RWE AG\uc2  both rising around 1.8% after Germany agreed a plan to extend the life of the country's nuclear reactors.\par \par \uc2 French water and waste-management firm \uc2 Veolia Environnement\uc2  climbed 3.3% in Paris after it was upgraded to buy from sell at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended at their highest level in almost four months Monday, led by steel makers and financial firms as better-than-expected U.S. jobs data helped ease concerns about the global economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Shanghai index will likely trade in a 2650-2750 range this week due to investor caution ahead of the release of August trade data Friday and inflation data next Monday.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks rose sharply Monday on the back of better-than-expected U.S. jobless figures Friday, although traders said the market could be hit by any warning signs coming from U.S. corporations ahead of third quarter earnings figures.\par \par \uc2 "Investors' attention will likely shift to micro factors from macro factors from this week," said Tsuyoshi Segawa, equity strategist at Mizuho Securities, adding that U.S. companies tend to announce forecast changes for the July-September quarter after the Labor Day weekend, which concludes Monday. "If there are downward revisions in the U.S. corporate sector, some Japanese stocks may be affected."\par \par \uc2 "The market is still cautious about the yen's levels, but a sharp sell-off is not expected for this week," said Kenichi Hirano, strategist at Tachibana Securities.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was higher in Europe Monday, struggling to overcome strong resistance at $1,250 a troy ounce in quiet trade. But the yellow metal's recovery following positive U.S. employment data Friday proves its resilience, market participants say, with some tipping gold to reach a new record high before the week is out.\par \par \uc2 Crude oil futures were stable in Europe Monday, buoyed by a positive economic outlook across financial markets, but with trading activity largely subdued due to the U.S. Labor Day holiday.\par \par \uc2 Base metals on the London Metal Exchange closed mostly higher Monday, supported by firmer equities, but remained largely in recent ranges amid low volume trade because of the U.S. holiday.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rise in risk sentiment that followed Friday's better-than-expected U.S. non-farm payrolls proved short-lived, with the euro falling back from earlier highs and the dollar and the yen staging small rebounds Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The pound, meanwhile, lost about a full cent against the dollar ahead of an unusually large amount of U.K. government bond coupon payments Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The reversal in market sentiment emerged as it became clear that a rash of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy meetings this week are unlikely to bring any rate rises. On the contrary, speculation persists that further quantitative easing will still be needed in many major economies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Initial reaction to the U.S. payrolls figure illustrated that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continue to be driven largely by risk, with the dollar falling as investors moved out of safe havens.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The improved mood lasted right through Asian trading Monday, helped by the latest inflation measures from Australia that showed consumer prices rising 0.2% last month. This contributed to the view that there is still some strength in the global economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, this mild euphoria didn't last long.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite the payrolls data, the U.S. Federal Reserve is hardly likely to start firming its monetary policy just now given the continued risk of a double-dip recession. Also, there is little sign that central banks elsewhere are any more confident in their economic recoveries to start increasing their interest rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Policy meetings by the Bank of Japan, the Bank of England, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  are all expected to end with unchanged rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro, which had shot back up over $1.29 as risk sentiment improved, was soon struggling to retain its gains, despite upbeat weekend comments from European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Jean-Claude Trichet that the probability of a double-dip recession the euro zone had fallen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen, which as a safe haven came under initial selling pressure, staged a rebound both against the euro and the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts suggest that the market is keen to test \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's resolve on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stop the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recent advance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda admitted over the weekend that co-ordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , in which other major central banks would sell the yen alongside the Bank of Japan, would be "difficult" to implement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But with the yen still trading at levels that are seen as damaging for Japanese exporters, there is still speculation that the Japanese authorities could decide to launch an \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exercise on their own.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By late Monday afternoon in Europe, the euro had slipped back from an earlier high at $1.2920 to trade down at $1.2879 compared with $1.2896 late Friday in New York, according to EBS.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts noted some reluctance in the market to push the euro back to $1.30, a key level that it hasn't traded above for nearly a month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also fell to Y108.51 from Y108.87 while the dollar fell to Y84.22 from Y84.43.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell back to CHF1.0152 from CHF1.0168 while the pound fell to $1.5372 from $1.5470. Traders have suggested that sterling is suffering because the market faces GBP5.6 billion of gilt coupon payments Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European government bonds rose Monday on bargain hunting following falls last week on receding risk aversion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German bunds rose in thinner than usual trading after last week's severe sell-off that followed better than expected non-farm payrolls and manufacturing ISM data in the U.S. However, with the bund futures contract shedding nearly three points in as many days, investors took to bargain hunting given the still uncertain outlook for the U.S. economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trading volumes were subdued due to a holiday in the U.S. and a lack of data releases.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gilts climbed in lower-than-average volumes ahead of coupon payments due to hit the market Tuesday as investors picked up bonds after last week's sharp sell off.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With U.S. markets shut and no economic releases of note, gilts traded in a tight range. "There is some buying with around GBP5.6 billion of coupon payments set to hit the market. We're also seeing interest in relative value trades in favor of gilts versus European bonds," a London-based trader said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds end higher Monday amid improving money market liquidity.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Investors are now wondering whether the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will keep the size of the one-year bill auction at a relatively low level of around CNY10 billion (in open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) tomorrow (Tuesday) to ensure banks have plenty of cash ahead of Mid-Autumn Festival late-September," a Beijing-based trader with a local insurer said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The benchmark 10-year cash Japanese government bond yield climbed to a more than two-month high Monday, as a less pessimistic outlook for the U.S. economy encouraged risk appetite and weakened demand for safe-haven assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "As yields have risen, those who want to cut their losses are fire-selling, but it is also providing great dip-buying opportunities for investors whose portfolios haven't been damaged" by recent volatility, said Nobuto Yamazaki, executive fund manager at \f28 \uc2 \f2 DLIBJ Asset Management\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yamazaki expects downward price corrections to continue for the rest of the week. But he said JGBs may be bought back if a more reliable forecast for the ruling Democratic Party of Japan's leadership race comes out later this week, and shows Prime Minister Naoto Kan is in the lead over former DPJ Secretary General Ichiro Ozawa, known as a fiscal expansionist.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other analysts expect excessive fears over JGB supply increases to recede somewhat in the near future. [ 06-09-10 2207GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Although the political situation will remain uncertain until the vote on Sept. 14 and even after, the JGB market should start to show a bit less concern about the prospect of an increasing risk premium for now," said Chotaro Morita, chief strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "At this stage, it seems that overseas yields are about the only factor that could drive JGB yields higher," Morita added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan is likely to hold monetary policy steady Tuesday, taking comfort from the yen's stability following last week's emergency easing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may express deeper concern about Japan's economic outlook, a prospect that could keep the chance of further easing later in the year on the table.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "As long as the yen's appreciation weighs on stocks and darkens the economic outlook, the pressure on the BOJ to take additional easing measures will likely continue growing," said Takeshi Minami, chief economist at Norinchukin Research Institute.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, all 19 economists surveyed by Dow Jones expect the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  to keep the cash rate at the current 4.5% when it meets Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par Obama Unveils $50 Bln Infrastructure Plan \par Ex-fed Vice Chairman Kohn: Fed Should Consider More Stimulus -NYT \par ECB Trichet: Europe Is Lagging Behind What Is Desirable-CNBC \par Ecofin: EU Finance Ministers To Seek Consensus On Bank Levy \par France's Sarkozy Faces Mass Protests Against Pension Reform \par WTO Chief Urges G-20 Leaders To Break Doha Impasse \par Italy Minister: Can Press Ahead With Fiscal Tightening - FT \par German Bank Association:Basel Leverage Rule May Cost Banks EUR36B \par German Fin Min Wants To Prepare Tax Cuts By 2013 - Spiegel \par German Min: Longer Nuclear Lives For Tax, Fund Contributions \par IMF Economist: Dwelling On Yuan Appreciation Counterproductive- Report \par Japan Sengoku Criticizes Ozawa's Budget Stance Ahead Of DPJ Election -Kyodo \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2245  New Zealand  2Q  Wholesale Trade 