\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Japan fiscal targets need review - PM candidate's aide\b0\par\par\uc2 800 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 2010\par 02:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * BOJ not doing enough to fight deflation - Ozawa aide\par \par \uc2 * Government should not rule out solo \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - aide\par \par \uc2 * Ozawa challenging PM Kan in Sept. 14 party vote (Changes headline)\par \par \uc2 By Chisa Fujioka\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 6 (Reuters) - Japan needs fiscal reform targets to tackle its massive debt but existing goals heavily influenced by bureaucrats should be reviewed, said an aide to the ruling party powerbroker challenging Prime Minister Naoto Kan.\par \par \uc2 Party heavyweight Ichiro Ozawa, challenging Kan in a party leadership election on Sept. 14, has promised to put politicians, not bureaucrats, in charge of policies, but has also prompted worries about expanding Japan's already huge debt.\par \par \uc2 Kan, in contrast, has built an image as a fiscal reformer since he took over in June as Japan's fifth prime minister in three years, calling for debating a future increase in the 5 percent sales tax.\par \par \uc2 Lower house lawmaker Banri Kaieda, member of a group advising Ozawa on policies, said Ozawa was also serious about fiscal discipline, but said targets compiled under Kan should be reviewed if Ozawa became leader.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think Mr. Ozawa is turning away from fiscal reform," Kaieda, chair of the lower house committee on financial affairs, told Reuters on Monday in an interview, in which he also said the Bank of Japan could do more to fight deflation.\par \par \uc2 "Reform is needed, but it's not right to have a reform plan that is under the mind control of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so we would review the existing fiscal targets."\par \par \uc2 Japan in June set a goal of bringing its primary budget balance -- which excludes revenue from bond sales and debt servicing costs -- into the black within a decade. Its public debt is twice the size of the nearly $5 trillion economy.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark 10-year Japanese government bond (JGB) yield, which hit a 7-year low below 0.9 percent last month, has jumped more than 20 basis points since Ozawa formally announced he will run for the leadership race.\par \par \uc2 Kaieda said Ozawa was trailing Kan "6 to 4". Although Ozawa heads the biggest DPJ group, his scandal-tainted image puts off the public and could affect the vote, in which party members of parliament, local lawmakers and party supporters will take part.\par \par \uc2 The winner is likely to be prime minister by virtue of the party's majority in the powerful lower house. Nearly two-thirds of Japanese voters prefer Kan to Ozawa as premier, media polls showed on Monday.\par \par \uc2 BOJ OPTIONS LIMITED\par \par \uc2 Both camps have vowed to be proactive on fighting deflation and the yen's rise to support the economy, but efforts have been constrained by the huge public debt. Kaieda said the Bank of Japan's remaining policy options were also limited.\par \par \uc2 "It is not enough," he said, when asked if the BOJ was taking sufficient action against deflation.\par \par \uc2 "But it's clear that this issue will not be resolved with just the BOJ's policies alone."\par \par \uc2 Bowing to government pressure, the BOJ eased policy last week by expanding a cheap loan scheme, but the move did little to deter yen gains or falls in stock prices as investors saw it as a symbolic gesture that had barely any effect in supporting growth.\par \par \uc2 Kaieda said one possibility was for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy more government bonds and that it was worth debating whether to change the BOJ's self-imposed rule of limiting the cumulative amount of JGBs it holds to that of banknotes in circulation.\par \par \uc2 The government should also do its part to support growth through measures such as tax reforms, said Kaieda, a former economics commentator. Corporate taxes should be cut to prevent Japanese companies from shifting overseas, although it would be hard to implement the cut from the next fiscal year from April.\par \par \uc2 With rises in the yen also threatening Japan's fragile economic recovery, Kaieda said the government should be ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, where the yen is not far from a 15-year high against the dollar marked late last month.\par \par \uc2 It is possible for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone, he said, despite concern that action without the help of U.S. or European authorities would have little impact.\par \par \uc2 "We know that the effect would be limited but solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should not be ruled out," he said. (Additional reporting by Yuko Yoshikawa; Editing by david Fox) (chisa.fujioka@thomsonreuters.com; Reuters Messaging: chisa.fujioka.reuters.com@reuters.net; +813 6441 1882) (If you have a query or comment on this story, send an email to news.feedback.asia@thomsonreuters.com)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-POLITICS/ (INTERVIEW, UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|RNA|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100906e6960000t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
