\par\par \b \uc2 DJ CORRECT: UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Unlikely To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  If Yen Rise Gradual - Sources\b0\par\par\uc2 834 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2010\par 08:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (The item "=UPDATE: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Unlikely To \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  If Yen Rise Gradual - Sources," 0509 GMT, incorrectly stated the amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2003-2004 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The correct story follws,) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would find it hard to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the yen's rise is gradual, but the authorities are more determined than ever to act against any unusually rapid exchange-rate moves, people familiar with the matter said.\par \par \uc2 Any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a sharp rise in the yen would be aimed at curbing market volatility, not attempting to turn the market trend around, several of these people told Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 "If there are no 'excessive fluctuations' in exchange rates, and if the dollar falls against the yen only gradually, it would be very difficult to act," one of the people said. "It's unthinkable that (the government) will fight back against the market trend."\par \par \uc2 But recent pledges by Prime Minister Naoto Kan and Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda to take "decisive" measures in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when necessary were intended to show that the authorities have stepped up their guard against abrupt moves in the yen, the people familiar with the situation said.\par \par \uc2 The tone of their comments was the strongest since Kan's government came to power in June. Japanese officials in the past have often used the word "decisive" to indicate they were getting serious about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Officials from the ministry, which runs \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, and Kan's office discussed the tone of the government's message and specifically chose the word "decisive" in order to send a strong message to the market, the people said.\par \par \uc2 "The weight carried by that word is significantly heavy," one of the people said. Another said it reflects that the government is "resolutely prepared" for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Taken together, the comments underscore increased vigilance in Tokyo against any surge in the yen that could threaten Japan's export-dependent economy. At the same time, they suggest the authorities are unlikely to fight the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the dollar gradually trends downward against the yen. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  since March 2004, when it ended a historic 15-month spree, selling some Y35 trillion yen.\par \par \uc2 Japanese officials appear to be wary of going against principles held by the Group of Seven advanced economies--that sharp moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  aren't desirable but manipulating exchange rates for a trade advantage is unacceptable--said the people familiar with the matter. Any sustained effort by Japan to push the dollar and the euro higher against the yen would likely anger the U.S. and Europe, whose economies are also struggling, they said.\par \par \uc2 Doubts about the effectiveness of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also seem to be behind their caution. With the Federal Reserve having cut U.S. rates to near zero and signalled that it may ease further if needed, "I doubt that there would be much impact even if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " on its own, another person familiar with the matter said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's fall against the yen "appears to be in line with fundamentals," leaving Japan "no good cause" to act now, he said.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has dropped 9.1% this year vs the yen. Around 0509 GMT Friday it was quoted at Y84.23, not far from the 15-year low of Y83.58 marked Aug. 24. Analysts expect the dollar to decline toward its 1995 record low of Y79.75 in days ahead, barring yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or accelerated BOJ easing efforts.\par \par \uc2 Current market conditions are in stark contrast to those in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had its last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign. Back then, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in worse economic shape than the U.S., while the Bank of Japan's monetary policy was far looser than the Fed's, allowing Tokyo to justify \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the person said.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ, under strong pressure from the government to do more to curb the yen's strength, Monday expanded a lending facility by Y10 trillion but analysts said the token easing would likely do little to ease monetary conditions further in Japan.\par \par \uc2 Remarks that day by BOJ Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa, indicating a reluctance to increase outright purchase of Japanese government bonds or lower the BOJ's 0.1% policy interest rate, reinforced the view in the market that economic fundamentals still point toward a stronger yen.\par \par \uc2 Some Japanese finance officials are concerned that if Tokyo were to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  without assistance the U.S. or Europe, it would be swamped by the $4-trillion-a-day global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, said people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 According to the Bank for International Settlements, trading volume in dollar/yen has soared over the past three years, with average daily turnover rocketing 73% to $568 billion.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 03, 2010 02:43 ET (06:43 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100903e6930013c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
