\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Stocks Seen Edging Up Before US Data\b0\par\par\uc2 2,551 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2010\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SNAPSHOT: \par ======== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European bourses seen up, govt bonds lower; \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  mixed; spot gold, oil futures lower\f28 \par \par \uc2 -GAZ Talking To \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  On Car Assembly In Russia\par \par \uc2 -Bundesbank Asks German President To Dismiss Sarrazin\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Continental AG\uc2  Collects About EUR1B With High-Yield Bond -Source\par \par \uc2 -HP To Acquire 3PAR\par \par \uc2 -Blackstone, Warburg Pincus Consider $5 Bln IPO For Kosmos: Sources -FT\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Air France\uc2  Mulls 'Low-Cost' Domestic Carrier -Sources\par \par \uc2 -Fitch Lowers \uc2 Burger King\uc2  Ratings On Deal Due To Higher Leverage\par \par \uc2 -Ban On Offshore Drilling May Have Impact On Oil Spill Payouts, BP Says\par \par \uc2 -New Blaze in Gulf Of Mexico Shakes Oil Industry\par \par \uc2 Watch for: 0755 GMT GER Aug Svcs PMI; 0800 GMT EU Aug Eurozone Svcs PMI; 0830 GMT UK Aug CIPS Svcs PMI; 0900 GMT Jul Retail trade; 1230 GMT US Aug Unemployment rate\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & US/ASIAN SUMMARIES: \par ======== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stock markets are mixed on Friday, while investors await the pivotal U.S. labor market figures. European stock markets may edge higher to start, with government bonds a bit lower. Major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are mixed, while spot gold and oil futures are lower.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCKS: \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European bourses are likely to start a bit higher on Friday, but investors' positioning will be restrained until the crucial U.S. labor data is issued at 1230 GMT.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For Friday's opening, the FTSE is seen up 7, the DAX up 12 and the CAC up 7.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the U.S. Federal Reserve takes a cautious view of the American economy, the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is more optimistic about the euro-zone's prospects.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro-zone growth next year may start on a positive note as both improving domestic factors and exports could continue to help the economy, ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet said Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Richard Perry, chief market strategist at Central Markets, said that volumes are still weak and that Wednesday's rally may have been short-covering.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The (FTSE) market still does not seem to want to move above 5,400. This is a ranging market for now," Perry said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 From a technical standpoint, resistance for the FTSE 100 is at 5,419, and has been the key factor for the past month, he said, noting that it's the difference between a test of the April high at 5,832 and a move back below 5,000.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stock futures are lower on Friday, ahead of the U.S. labor market data. Stock markets rose Thursday, with consumer companies including \f28 \uc2 \f2 Home Depot\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Walt Disney\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wal-Mart Stores\f28 \uc2 \f2  leading the gains following better-than-expected readings from the housing and retail sectors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "You've had some decent data this week compared to what we saw previously and sentiment's been pretty bearish, so you got a nice little pop," said Michael Church, president of Addison Capital. "We're starting to price back in that maybe it's not an outright collapse of the economy, maybe it's just a muddling-along economy."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stock markets are mixed Friday with technology plays leading gainers in Tokyo and Seoul, while weakness in China's banking stocks dragged the Shanghai market lower.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors were encouraged by a third-straight session of gains on Wall Street on Thursday, as well an unexpected increase in U.S. pending home sales. However, buying was tempered by caution ahead of the U.S. nonfarm payrolls report for August due later Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "U.S. economic data have been surprising, more than less, on the upside this week," said Philip Wee at DBS Group Research in Singapore. That could be because of "U.S. double-dip worries injecting more-than-usual bearishness into the consensus forecasts."\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs are little changed pending the pivotal U.S. labor data at 1230 GMT Friday, which could help investors decide whether the dollar should fall more.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There is great uncertainty, and the market is acting in a very tentative manner ahead of [Friday's] nonfarm payrolls data," said Jack Spitz, managing director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , financial markets and derivatives at National Bank in Toronto.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "With investors still on edge about the chance of a double-dip U.S. recession, we suspect any disappointment (in the U.S. jobs data) would scupper the recent bond market sell-off, providing headwinds for the U.S. dollar," said Mike Jones, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of New Zealand\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Jean-Claude Trichet said Thursday the euro-zone \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would extend its tool box of additional bank funding on a "full allotment" basis, citing continued uncertainties in the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Overall, the current monetary stance remains accommodative," Trichet told a news conference.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier Thursday, the ECB's policy board voted to keep its benchmark interest rate unchanged at 1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Trichet's statement doesn't change the outcome for interest rates and doesn't change the outlook for the euro zone, so it's really a nonevent," said Dirk Chlench, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Landesbank Baden-Wurttemberg in Stuttgart. "I expect [the statement] to have no long-term impact on the euro."\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's government would find it hard to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the dollar declines gradually against the yen, but the authorities are more determined than ever to act against any unusually rapid \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate moves, several people familiar with the matter said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sterling, along with other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , is likely to consolidate in narrow ranges pending the U.S. labor figures later Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BONDS: \par ===== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bunds and gilts may start a bit lower on Friday, while investors ponder whether they should close out positions ahead of the U.S. payrolls and the long weekend.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The U.S. markets will see early closing Friday. Anyone with a position will have difficulty getting out of it before Tuesday," cautioned \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's stance also plays a role.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the ECB did extend unlimited cash tenders until the end of the year, the move was along expected lines, leaving investors to focus on the upgrade of growth and inflation forecasts by the ECB's staff. "The upward revision of growth and inflation indicates the ECB is less pessimistic than people had anticipated, while (the Bundesbank's) Weber had already hinted at extending liquidity facilities so it was not really a surprise," says Peter Chatwell at Credit Agricole.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The liquidity program shows that the ECB still does not trust the recovery and the health of the financial system," ING senior economist Carsten Brzeski concluded.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the UK, an auction of UKT 2014 Thursday saw the bid-to-cover ratio fall and yield tail widen from the previous auction. "The sale was quite disappointing but with yields, particularly on the front end of the curve pretty low, there is no real incentive to buy at auctions," says a London-based strategist.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Services PMI data due later could come in a bit weaker, once more confounding the UK's economic outlook.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury prices are higher on Friday, after markets edged lower Thursday following the auction of $10 billion in ten-year Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities, or TIPS, and a slight decline in weekly jobless-claims data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The market that has priced in a lot of bond-friendly news, but like a drug, it needs a stronger fix to do better," said David Ader, head of government bond strategy at CRT Capital Group. "People are positioned pretty neutral, they're going to be trading on choppy volume until something gels, clarifies, on the economy."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Treasury Department said Thursday it will sell $67 billion next week, comprising $33 billion in three-year notes, $21 billion in 10-year notes and $13 billion in 30-year bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Tokyo on Friday, lead September Japanese government bond futures were down 0.01 at 142.20 points, weighed by the Nikkei's gains and Thursday's weakness in U.S. Treasurys. The 10-year cash JGB yield was 0.5 basis point lower at 1.105%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ENERGY: \par ======= \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil prices fell below $75 a barrel in Asian trade as traders took profit from gains made the previous day and braced for expected weakness in U.S. employment numbers, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New York's main contract, light, sweet crude for October delivery, fell 26 cents to $74.76 a barrel. Brent North Sea crude for October delivery, shed 35 cents to $76.58.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders were taking profit from a hike in crude prices yesterday ahead of the release of the monthly U.S. jobs report later today, said Victor Shum, senior principal of Purvin and Gertz energy consultants in Singapore. "I think that given the rise in pricing yesterday...we are seeing some pullback due to the next big economic report that's going to come out of the U.S., which is the jobs report," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shum added that there were "concerns" that the report would highlight the flagging state of the U.S. economic recovery. The U.S. Labor Department is widely expected by analysts to report nonfarm payrolls--combining both government and private jobs--to shrink by 120,000 in August, with unemployment edging up to 9.6 percent from 9.5 percent.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par METALS: \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold is at $1,249.50 per troy ounce, down $1.80 from late Thursday in New York. "There will be a temptation to take profit with New York on holiday Monday," says a dealer in Hong Kong. But analysts are still mostly bullish.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  reiterates its positive view on copper despite a recent trend towards weak economic data from U.S. LME 3-month copper is at $7,626 a metric ton, down $9.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; dennis.baker@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 03-09-10 0500GMT ]\f28 \par \par