\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Platinum +0.6% On Short Covers;Still Top Pick -GS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,585 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2010\par 05:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0337 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot platinum at $1,552/oz, up $9 with some short covering on Tocom after spot metal jumped 1% overnight. Tocom August 2011 platinum at Y4,233/gram, up Y49. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  says platinum remains its top pick in metals as is most supply constrained. "As the recovery in global vehicle production advances, we believe that growth in mine production will be inadequate to meet the improving demand for PGMs in the autocatalyst sector." Says prices need to rise to a level that destroys some jewellery and investment demand to balance the market. Adds, recent soft trend in PGMs and weak USD/ZAR has hurt margins at South African producers and reduced incentive to invest in expanding output. Adds, prefers platinum to palladium as not clear how much refined palladium Russia has stockpiled. Tips platinum to average $1,831/oz in 2011. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0336 GMT [Dow Jones] While inflationary pressures could soon re-emerge in Vietnam, that's not likely to stop growth-focused government from devaluing VND again, possibly before year-end, says DJ's Money Talks column. Despite 3 devaluations since last November, trade deficit persists; while exports have risen 19.7% in first 8 months of 2010, pace of export expansion trails many of its neighbors, with exporters facing headwinds such as flagging U.S. recovery, low European growth. With crucial Communist Party Congress looming in early 2011, authorities seem determined to ensure growth doesn't falter. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  devalued VND again last month, lifting central reference point for USD exchange rate to VND18,932. While VND value has been cut 10% since November, trade deficit still widening as imports remain strong, could strengthen further. While \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  knows weakening VND could stoke inflation, it appears determined to take whatever necessary steps to keep exports humming. See: "=MONEY TALKS: Vietnam Likely To Weaken Dong Again To Spur Exports." (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0328 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +1.5% at 934.24 as details become clearer on yesterday's court ruling allowing bulk of projects to resume at Map Ta Phut industrial estate almost a year after they were suspended on environmental, health concerns. "This is the outcome we have been saying is the most likely and we can now put the entire Map Ta Phut issue behind us - unless the environmentalists appeal," says Andrew Yates, head of sales for international equities at Asia Plus Securities; but adds even if appeal is lodged, projects will continue unless there's another injunction. Positive momentum from ruling giving lift to stocks across the board. Resistance at 935, then 940, support at 925. Among most active, PTT (PTT.TH) +5.2% at THB283, \uc2 Siam Cement\uc2  (SCC.TH) +5% at THB318, Banpu (BANPU.TH) +1.6% at THB636, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) +3.4% at THB106.50, \uc2 Hemaraj Land & Development\uc2  (HEMRAJ.TH) +2.8% at THB2.18. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0327 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options decline to 11.55%/12.25% from 11.75%/12.45% in NY overnight, as spot stays quiet before release of U.S. payrolls data later in day, reducing demand for hedges against fluctuations. Options dealer at major Japan bank says vols unlikely to change until data release, adding once data released, vols will likely drop 10-20 bps as premium for major events comes off. Says player buys 11-day USD-put/JPY-call contract at 82.00 with 13.1%; another player buys 12-day USD-put/JPY-call contract at 83.00 with 12.5%; another player buys 19-day USD-call/JPY-put contract at 86.00 with 12.3%; another player sells 34-day USD-call/JPY-put contract at 85.00 with 11.1%. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0323 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds mostly steady in tepid trade. Selling in medium-term notes spotted, to lock in profit after recent rally; "There are hardly any deals done in the morning as it's the last business day before weekend and there's no fresh driver," says bond dealer. Investors keeping eye on U.S. jobs data due later today. Yields likely move in 2-bp range. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.60%/2.57% vs 2.59%/2.58% late Wednesday, November 2016 quoted at 2.72%/2.67% vs 2.72%/2.68%, June 2019 quoted at 2.75%/2.68% vs 2.75%/2.67%. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0322 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  reiterates its positive view on copper despite recent trend towards weak economic data from U.S. "A decade ago, a faltering U.S. recovery would have been a compelling sell signal for industrial commodities and resources equities alike, but things have changed; in 2009 China accounted for 37% of global copper consumption, compared with the U.S. share of just 9%," says report. Tips copper to average $8,005/ton or 363 cents/lb in 2011, $8,665/ton or 393 cents/lb in 2012 as prices head higher in order to ration demand. Tips frequent short term spikes beyond those levels as market "moves deeper into deficit." LME 3-month copper at $7,626/ton, down $9 with thin 791 lots done. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0318 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD weakening on selling by domestic Australian bank, but thin volumes, reluctance to trade much before U.S. payrolls later in day exacerbating move, says strategist. Pegs AUD/USD support at 0.9050, pair last at 0.9086 vs 0.9110 earlier. Says payrolls data will be key in determining levels later; market expects 110,000 jobs shed in August vs 131,000 lost last month. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0315 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR tipped lower as Asian units show strength against the greenback, local indexes expected to open in positive territory, shadowing gains on Wall Street, says foreign bank trader. Pair may open around 46.65 vs 46.73 late Thursday. "There will be light, tentative trading ahead of the U.S. non-farm payrolls data (due 1230 GMT)," trader says. Tips pair in 46.55-46.85 band in session. However, RBI unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as it has followed a hands-off policy in recent months, near-term outlook for INR remains negative on widening trade deficit, trader says. Foreign funds were net sellers of a provisional INR823 million worth of Indian stocks on Thursday, 1-month NDFs quoting at 46.9250/46.95. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0311 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex called to open higher, supported by positive cues in most regional bourses, short covering, traders say; expect Sensex in 18,150-18,350 range for session, after last ending +0.2% at 18,238.31. "The opening should be up by about 0.3%-0.5% on firm global markets, but overall I think the markets lack buying conviction and could consolidate at every high level," says dealer at local brokerage. Adds high valuations in most blue chips after about 10% rally since June 9, lack of fresh domestic triggers could also weigh, cap any upside near term. Says banks, select infrastructure stocks could gain on institutional buying. Global markets, U.S. jobs data due later in global trading day could provide direction near term. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0307 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY could trend toward 80.00 in coming weeks, says \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at major Japan securities house after people familiar with matter tell Dow Jones MOF would find it hard to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if USD/JPY declines gradually. Any MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be aimed at curbing market volatility, not attempting to turn the market trend around, the people say. "That means players are likely to remain skeptical that any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be that effective anyway," dealer says. USD/JPY last 84.34. See: =\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF Unlikely To \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  If Dollar/Yen Decline Gradual -Sources (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0305 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares +0.7% at 3144.367, helped by steady IDR, gains in most Asian markets, traders say; tip resistance at 3150. "Mild buying by foreign funds has also helped improve sentiment here," says trader at First Asia Capital. Among gainers, \uc2 Indosat\uc2  (ISAT.JK) +5.5% at IDR4,825 after \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  upgrades rating to Hold from Sell, coal miner Adaro (ADRO.JK) +2.2% at IDR1,830 on bargain buying, while car maker Astra (ASII.JK) +0.7% at IDR50,400. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0301 GMT [Dow Jones] Rally in LME 3-month aluminum likely to run out of steam soon, says Tokyo-based trader at major Japanese trading house. "Our range is $2,000 to $2,200 and we don't see it getting beyond that." Adds, seasonals not in aluminum's favor with beverage can production down ahead of winter, automotive production also falling, he says. Spot market in Japan reasonably well-supplied although premiums remain high between $110 to $120. LME 3-month aluminum at $2,138/ton, down $7 but still up 4% for the week. Volume light with 436 lots done so far.(james.campbell@dowjones.com) [ 09-02-10 2337ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100903e6930002j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
