91-day CD rates, though sustained foreign interest in Korea Treasury Bonds, KTB futures may curb expected gain in yields, according to Credit Agricole. Finance Ministry's recent steps to curb inflation, with initiatives including increasing supply of farm/fisheries products, monitoring food prices closely, boosting competition among retailers, unlikely to bring down inflation given demand-pull measures, thus would prompt BOK to move to hike rates, says Agricole in research note. Forecasts more upside to KRW IRS in coming months on expectations for continued rate hikes, and likely catch-up by benchmark 91-day CD with rising policy rates. Adds sustained foreign interest in KTB, KTB futures, amid favorable supply environment, could curb expected gain in yields. (in-soo.nam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0250 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds down as investors becoming less risk averse, local trader says. "People are now less scared of slowing economic growth. The trend of U.S. Treasurys will still guide the Taiwan market in the near future." 10-year yield up at 1.1935% vs 1.1498% yesterday's close, tips immediate resistance at 1.1998%; 5-year yield also up at 0.8730% vs 0.8590%. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0246 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW extends fall as offshore players sell USD, but traders say further downside limited for now after 3-Seoul based traders suspect USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier at around 1,176. "But, regardless of the suspected (BOK) smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar-selling pressure is strong, especially from offshore players," which likely to keep pair biased downward for rest of session, says local bank trader. "I'm thinking pair will be supported at 1,175... but if that level is breached, pair could slip to 1,172," says another trader. Pair now down at 1,177.1 vs 1,180.5 late yesterday in Seoul. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0244 GMT [