\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: India Bonds Tipped Down; INR120B Sale Caution\b0\par\par\uc2 1,460 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2010\par 04:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0259 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds tipped lower, mirroring weakness in U.S. Treasurys overnight as global risk sentiment improves, traders may also exercise caution ahead of INR120 billion debt sale later Friday, says foreign bank trader. Benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond tipped in 98.50-98.85 band till auction vs 98.80 last close. "Since RBI will be auctioning the 8.08% 2022 paper for the first time, the market will take trading cues from the cut-offs. In the near-term, traders will watch out for crucial global and domestic data like U.S. non-farm payrolls and India's July factory output because the market seems divided whether RBI will hike rates in September or not," trader says. Weekly inflation data likely 0600 GMT Friday. RBI to sell INR40 billion of 7.47% 2017 bond, INR50 billion of 8.08% 2022 bond, INR30 billion of 8.30% 2040 bond. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0252 GMT [Dow Jones] Bank of Korea likely to hike rates further, boosting KRW IRS, 91-day CD rates, though sustained foreign interest in Korea Treasury Bonds, KTB futures may curb expected gain in yields, according to Credit Agricole. Finance Ministry's recent steps to curb inflation, with initiatives including increasing supply of farm/fisheries products, monitoring food prices closely, boosting competition among retailers, unlikely to bring down inflation given demand-pull measures, thus would prompt BOK to move to hike rates, says Agricole in research note. Forecasts more upside to KRW IRS in coming months on expectations for continued rate hikes, and likely catch-up by benchmark 91-day CD with rising policy rates. Adds sustained foreign interest in KTB, KTB futures, amid favorable supply environment, could curb expected gain in yields. (in-soo.nam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0250 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds down as investors becoming less risk averse, local trader says. "People are now less scared of slowing economic growth. The trend of U.S. Treasurys will still guide the Taiwan market in the near future." 10-year yield up at 1.1935% vs 1.1498% yesterday's close, tips immediate resistance at 1.1998%; 5-year yield also up at 0.8730% vs 0.8590%. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0246 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW extends fall as offshore players sell USD, but traders say further downside limited for now after 3-Seoul based traders suspect USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier at around 1,176. "But, regardless of the suspected (BOK) smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar-selling pressure is strong, especially from offshore players," which likely to keep pair biased downward for rest of session, says local bank trader. "I'm thinking pair will be supported at 1,175... but if that level is breached, pair could slip to 1,172," says another trader. Pair now down at 1,177.1 vs 1,180.5 late yesterday in Seoul. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0244 GMT [Dow Jones] Not much interest either way in gold market Friday, says Hong Kong-based trader at international bank. Says U.S. non-farm payrolls due 1230 GMT to decide direction but if data is neutral or better than expected, gold could suffer. "There will be a temptation to take profit with New York on holiday Monday," he says. Analysts still mostly bullish. "Investor appetite has kicked off September on a positive note, and coupled with the seasonally strong period in gold demand, we would expect gold prices to test new highs as the year unfolds," says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in note. Spot gold at $1,249.50/oz, down $1.80 since Thursday's NY close. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0233 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD may rise to 1.2850, near Thursday's high, before release of U.S. non-farm payroll data for August due 1230 GMT as short-term investors cover shorts, model funds buying pair, says senior dealer at major Japan bank. But says sharper rises unlikely because ordinary investors avoid large bidding before major U.S. economic indicator. Pair last 1.2828. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP moving lower, weighed by broad USD weakness, improving global risk appetite. Pair last at 44.75, lowest in 4 months, vs 4.95 late yesterday; volume still moderate. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support pair at 44.70 level, says local bank trader. Eyes resistance at 44.95. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds higher as liquidity conditions improve with unsuccessful subscription funds for ICBC's (601398.SH) convertible bond issue returning to money market. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 126.62 vs 126.60 last close, interbank 7-day weighted-average repo rate down 15 bps at 2.35%. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may try to ensure that market liquidity remains ample in coming weeks to meet increasing cash demand ahead of Mid-Autumn Festival late-September," says Beijing-based analyst with local insurer. Tips bond index in 126.6-126.7 range for rest of session. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0223 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 0.4% at 9097.63 midday, with 24 of Topix's 33 subsectors higher. Tech shares higher on Nasdaq's strength, \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) up 1.7% at Y2,469, \uc2 Toshiba\uc2  (6502.TO) up 1.3% at Y389. While pension funds may be picking up cheap stocks, both foreign investors and retail investors staying on sidelines before U.S. jobs data out later in global trading day, says Yutaka Miura, senior technical analyst at Mizuho Securities, adding that index may trade between 9000-9150 in afternoon. Matsuya (8237.TO) down 4.8% at Y597 on Nikkei report department store operator Isetan, unit of \uc2 Isetan Mitsukoshi Holdings\uc2  (3099.TO), has decided to end capital and \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  tie-up with Matsuya. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0223 GMT [Dow Jones] DPJ power-broker Ozawa aims to oust PM Kan, but even if Ozawa succeeds, Japan will continue to face major challenges to any effective \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite Ozawa's remark Thursday that government "should definitely" stem strong JPY; that's because Ozawa would be constrained by international expectations \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Osamu Takashima, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy won't change even if a new Ozawa administration takes over, as he will face the same difficulties regarding \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that Kan has." Says chief among them is G20 framework that countries should refrain from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . While framework could change if USD weakness roils markets, says for now it "doesn't have any serious adverse effect on the global economy." Traders say if government were to go ahead with any unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it would likely be too small in scale to have major effect.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0223 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI nearly flat at 20,872.68, consolidating after 2-day, 1.6% rally; volume spikes to HK$16.49 billion, but boosted by more than HK$1 billion from Ping An (2318.HK) after Newbridge Asia sells down stake. \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  remains bullish on HK market, says historically, average length of earnings expansion cycle in MSCI HK about 26 months; "currently, we are only 10 months into this cycle." House's year-end target for HSI at 26,193. Ping An +3.6% at HK$68.45 as Newbridge's stake sale viewed as lifting overhang, is best-performing blue chip; worst performer Esprit (0330.HK), slumps 5.4% to HK$40.60 in further post-results weakness. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0219 GMT [Dow Jones] Bank Negara likely to keep policy rates unchanged for remainder of year after maintaining key rate at 2.75% yesterday, says OSK Research. Following Thursday's policy decision, "we revised our year-end forecast for OPR from 3.00% to 2.75%," house says. OSK expects resumption of rate hikes in latter half of next year, possibly in 3Q. "For 2011, contingent on the global economic recovery and, subsequently, the timing of the Fed rate hike, we forecast the OPR to be raised to 3.50% by end-2011," house says. Adds, given strong growth momentum in 1H, current official forecast of 4.5%-5.5% growth may be revised higher during Budget 2011 announcement in October. OSK expects growth at 7.5% for the year. OSK also says \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2  might have 8 meetings in 2011, rather than 6 they have currently set, given uncertainties in global financial markets. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com) [ 09-02-10 2259ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100903e69300021\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
