ains, thanks to higher commodity prices, but Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) may fall on profit-taking after recent outperformance. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0216 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD lower at 31.987 vs 32.040 at yesterday's close. Dealer from foreign bank says trading thin ahead of U.S. nonfarm payrolls data due later in day, although overall positive market sentiment lends some strength to TWD. Tips tight 31.940-32.040 band today. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again before closing; it has been actively selling TWD in market in recent months, traders believe \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  aims to keep USD/TWD at around 32.000 so as not to hurt competitiveness of exports sector. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] Silver slightly lower at $19.61, down 4 cents after rising 1.5% overnight, with May high of $19.81 in sight, says ScotiaMocatta. "We are bullish on silver looking for an eventual test of the 2008 high of $21.35." Adds gold/silver ratio broke lower through August low of 64.30 to current 63.72. Tips April low of 62.66 as next major support followed by 2010 low of 60.77. Silver leading gilded life, tracking gold and also getting boost when industrial metals rise, although doesn't seem to suffer when base metals fall as long as gold remains firm. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0213 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude to take cue from U.S. job data due later today; gains over past two sessions should be viewed as upwards correction after overselling, August unemployment result to set direction ahead of Labor Day long weekend, Ritterbusch & Associates says in note; adds weak fundamentals mean even slight downside surprise in jobs numbers likely push crude to week-low. Economists tip payrolls to fall by 105,000, unemployment rate to tick up to 9.6%, highest since May; October crude down 26 cents at $74.76/bbl on Globex. (max.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0213 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals lower early Friday as some trepidation about U.S. August non-farm payrolls data due 1230 GMT, and the long weekend in U.S. for Labor Day holiday encourages book squaring. LME 3-month copper at $7,630/ton, down $5 with 593 lots done. "A bit of caution and profit-taking is natural after the run we have had," says a Tokyo based trader. Copper up 2% for the week so far. Dow Jones survey of economists tips loss of 110,000 non-farm payrolls with unemployment tipped to climb to 9.6% vs 9.5% in July. Trader says some exaggeration over the tightness in aluminum market; "It's only South East Asia where you can say it's really tight. There isn't much demand coming from China," he says. LME 3-month aluminum at $2,140/ton, down $5 with 390 lots done so far. Aluminum up 4% for week so far. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares higher with strong tech shares offering plenty of support. Taiex last +1.1% at 7804.64, key resistance eyed at 7900, 7780-7850 range tipped. Analysts say tech stocks up on new Apple product launches, while Hon Hai (2317.TW) shares continue to stage strong recovery after disappointing 1H results earlier this week, shares last +1.8% at NT$112.50. "Right now the new Apple product launches are really moving tech shares," says Capital Securities analyst Diana Wu. Analysts say local PC marketing activities for 'back to school' season may boost demand. Acer (2353.TW) +1.3% at NT$77.30, Asustek (2357.TW) +1.2% at NT$212.50.(paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares moving higher, extending advance amid continuing investor optimism, overnight gains in U.S. stocks; PSEi up 1.3% to 3714.84, highest since November 2007 with all sub-indices in positive territory. Volume still moderate. "Foreign money is catching up to participate, this is essentially still an offshoot of (better than expected) 2Q GDP data. Expectations moving forward continue to be positive," notes Erwin Balita, SB equities research head. Next resistance 3750. Leading market advance are Metropolitan Bank (MBT.PH) +1.5% at PHP68.00, \uc2 Philex Mining\uc2  (PX.PH) +3.5% at PHP12.36, \uc2 Ayala Corp\uc2 . (AC.PH) +4.4% at PHP384.00. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares tad down early, as banks weigh amid renewed worries over credit risk. Shanghai Composite Index down 0.1% at 2652.65, tipped to trade in 2600-2700 range for now. "The overriding market sentiment is one of caution, as investors wait for next week's release of August economic data," says Zhang Gang at Central China Securities; adds higher-than-expected August inflation could set off concerns over further tightening measures. 4 of China's 5 biggest banks report increases in special-mention loans in 2Q, could indicate potentially high credit risk in banking system, China Securities Journal reports today, citing its own statistics. Bank of China (601988.SH) down 0.6% at CNY3.35, \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  (600036.SH) down 0.8% at CNY13.54. Shenzhen index +0.2% at 1174.90. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0202 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares likely to extend gains after court's ruling late yesterday to allow 74 of 76 suspended projects in Map Ta Phut to proceed. Gains in DJIA overnight, capital inflows also likely to lend support. Big cap energy, petrochemical, banking stocks likely to lead gainers. "The court's ruling clears Map Ta Phut's case (which overhang for market for a year after court's injunction last September on environment concerns) and this will give a boost to the country's economy overall," says analyst with Far East Securities Parin Kitchatornpitak. However, profit-taking may emerge during session after strong gains recently, he adds. Resistance tipped at 930-935, support at 920-915 area. SET index yesterday ends +0.1% at 920.54. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com) [ 09-02-10 2223ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022  