\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES \highlight\uc2 : Spain Bond Auction Lifts Euro Ahead Of ECB\b0\par\par\uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 2010\par 13:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global News Select\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 By William L. Watts\par \par \uc2 The dollar was mostly lower versus major rivals Thursday, sinking versus the European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of the conclusion of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monthly meeting later in the session.\par \par \uc2 The euro (CUR_EURUSD) erased early weakness to trade at $1.2824, up from $1.2799 in late North American trading on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The euro was buoyed by Spain's sale of 3.3 billion euros ($4.2 billion) of five-year debt, which helped ease concerns about sovereign debt in the 16-nation euro zone.\par \par \uc2 Eurostat, the European Union statistics agency, on Thursday said second-quarter gross domestic product in the 16-nation euro zone grew 1%, unrevised from its previous quarterly estimate. First-quarter growth was revised up to 0.3%, which helped lift second-quarter GDP year-on-year growth to 1.9% from an initial estimate of 1.7%.\par \par \uc2 The euro's modest bounce comes as investors focus on ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet's monthly news conference in Frankfurt at 8:30 a.m. Eastern. The ECB is widely expected to announce at 7:45 a.m. Eastern that it made no change interest rates, leaving its benchmark rate at a record low 1%.\par \par \uc2 Trichet is expected to confirm the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will maintain its range of special liquidity measures through the end of the year, while cautioning that strong growth in the second quarter is likely to fade in coming months as the U.S. economy slows, economists said.\par \par \uc2 A cautious tone from Trichet "would help maintain the impression that the ECB will be on hold for at least the rest of this year," said Steve Barrow, strategist at Standard Bank. "We think it will be on hold (or easier) next year given growth risks and low inflation."\par \par \uc2 The British pound (CUR_GBPUSD) fell to $1.5400 from $1.5453.\par \par \uc2 The pound was undercut by mortgage lender Nationwide's monthly housing survey, which showed that house prices declined by 0.9% in August compared to July.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the dollar slipped against its Japanese counterpart, even after a Japanese political candidate reiterated his call for direct \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the recently strong yen.\par \par \uc2 Ruling-party presidential candidate Ichiro Ozawa said in a televised debate with his party rival, Prime Minister Naoto Kan, that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's appreciation, even though unilateral action was unlikely to change the long-term trend.\par \par \uc2 "I think the recent rapid pace of the yen's rise means that we probably have to act with due determination," Ozawa said, according to Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 Against the yen (CUR_USDYEN) -- which surged to a 15-year high against the greenback last week -- the dollar slipped to \u194\'00\'C2\u165\'00\'A584.17, from \u194\'00\'C2\u165\'00\'A584.46 late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index (DXY), a measure of the U.S. unit against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , slipped to 82.363 from 82.514 late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The Swedish krona rose versus the dollar and the euro after Sweden's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its key repo rate by 0.25 percentage point to 0.75%. The increase was in line with market expectations.\par \par \uc2 The krona rose 0.3% versus the U.S. unit (C_SEK) to trade at 7.2639 per dollar. It gained 0.2% against the European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to change hands at 9.3146 per euro.\par \par \uc2 The Swedish economy is continuing to show strong growth, while inflationary pressures are currently low, but are expected to increase as economic activity strengthens, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement. [ 09-02-10 0708ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020100902e6920002v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
