\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's Ozawa Urges Government To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  To Stop Yen Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 614 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 2010\par 10:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments on Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , more detail and background.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Tomoyuki Tachikawa \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen's strength, even if solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  wouldn't be very effective in turning the market trend around, ruling party presidential candidate Ichiro Ozawa said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 A strong yen over the long term isn't "a bad thing" for Japan, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp rise in the near term could hurt vulnerable parts of the export-dependent economy, he said.\par \par \uc2 "I think the recent rapid pace of the yen's rise means that we probably have to act with due determination," Ozawa said in a televised debate with Prime Minister Naoto Kan. The government "should definitely stem the sharp yen appreciation," Ozawa added.\par \par \uc2 Ozawa, one of the most powerful politicians in the Democratic Party of Japan, has entered the party's leadership race. The winner, which will be decided Sept. 14, will likely become Japan's next prime minister.\par \par \uc2 Kan, in the debate, didn't mention the possibility of his administration \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 It is extremely unusual for a candidate in a ruling party leadership race in Japan to touch on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues in public, much less \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . His comments highlight how the yen's rise has become a hot-button issue in a country whose economic recovery is in danger of faltering under the weight of the strong yen.\par \par \uc2 The yen's appreciation has put many Japanese exporters at a disadvantage against foreign rivals, particularly electronics makers and shipbuilders in South Korea, whose government has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in recent months to depress the won. Japanese authorities, in contrast, haven't entered the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in more than six years, even as the yen has climbed relentlessly higher.\par \par \uc2 Japanese industry is worried. A government survey last week showed that 40% of Japanese manufacturers polled would shift some production overseas should the dollar remain at Y85.\par \par \uc2 The government succeeded Monday in coaxing the Bank of Japan to loosen its already-super easy monetary policy a little more in the hope of relieving some upward pressure on the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The effect was limited. Even after the BOJ increased the size of a special loan facility, the dollar has remained around 15-year lows near Y84.\par \par \uc2 Ozawa, in the Thursday debate, seemed to indicate he wouldn't lean heavily on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fight the yen's rise should he eventually become prime minister. He said the BOJ's monetary policy alone wouldn't be that effective in curbing yen strength.\par \par \uc2 Ozawa emphasized that the government should aim to create an economy that is strong enough domestically to run on its own, not dependent on exports for growth. The remarks suggest he may take steps to boost domestic demand if he becomes prime minister. He was light on detail, but he said he wanted big Japanese companies to direct more of the financial resources to hiring and increasing wages.\par \par \uc2 To overcome the country's economic problems, "we should change the economy so that we won't rely on external demand," Ozawa said.\par \par \uc2 Ozawa has pledged to implement over the coming years a series of costly consumption-lifting measures promised by the DPJ in 2009's Lower House election.\par \par \uc2 Some observers are concerned that an Ozawa administration could worsen Japan's already-enormous fiscal problems. But Ozawa has said it would be possible to finance more expenditure by reducing waste elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 -By Tomoyuki Tachikawa, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2787; tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com [ 02-09-10 0803GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100902e692000ke\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
