\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY May Fall To 83.50 Before US Data -Mizuho\b0\par\par\uc2 1,653 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 2010\par 05:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0335 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall to 83.50 ahead of anticipated U.S. non-farm payrolls data due Friday as market sentiment remains USD-bearish due to concerns over the nation's economy, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 's senior dealer Satoshi Tate. Adds, investors paying "particular attention to housing and employment data, because these two areas are particularly weak in the U.S. economy." For this reason, many investors bet Friday's jobs data may turn out to be weaker than expected (Dow Jones poll of economists expecting 110,000 drop), which could provide fresh cue to push USD/JPY further down. Adds, speculation \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on JPY persists, but even then, USD/JPY should keep falling "because the U.S. factors are more important than Japan's now." Pair last 84.13. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0331 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +0.4% at 923.03 on continuing capital inflow, gains on Wall Street overnight, buying ahead of court's verdict on fate of suspended projects in Map Ta Phut. Big-cap energy, petrochemical, banking plays leading gains on optimism over better revenues if the court allows dozens of stalled projects to resume \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "If the court drops the case, it will help bolster the market sentiment," says \uc2 Kasikorn Securities\uc2  head of research Kavee Chukitkasem. Ruling due later today. Resistance tipped at 929-932, support at 914-912 area. Among most active, Banpu (BANPU.TH) +1.6% at THB628, PTT (PTT.TH) +0.4% at THB272, \uc2 Krung Thai Bank\uc2  (KTB.TH) flat at THB14.60. \uc2 Siam Cement\uc2  (SCC.TH) +0.3% at THB306, off day's high of THB310 -- also historic high, on hopes its suspended projects will be given go-ahead permission from court. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0318 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex called to open 0.6%-0.8% higher, tracking positive regional trade following overnight rally on Wall Street (DJIA ended +2.5%) on better-than expected August U.S. manufacturing data and as sentiment upbeat after the Sensex managed to close near its day's high yesterday, says local dealer. Adds, short-covering, sustained foreign fund inflows to further help. Overseas funds purchased shares worth provisional INR3.60 billion Wednesday, taking total inflows to about INR11.85 last 3 sessions. Tips recent underperforming sectors such as metals, select infrastructure stocks to lead rise. Still, believes Sensex could find immediate resistance at 18,400, as traders pare positions at high levels due to stretched valuations in most index blue chips after nearly 10% rally since June 9. Says, lack of fresh domestic triggers and uncertain economic outlook in the U.S. to also weigh, limit upside. Sensex ended +1.3% at 18,205.87 Wednesday. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0312 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR tipped lower on broad weakness in greenback after better-than-expected U.S. manufacturing data helped improve risk appetite, says trader with foreign bank. Pair may open around 46.75 vs 46.81 late Wednesday. Trader says exporters seen selling Wednesday, which may continue in session today above 46.80 levels. Local shares expected to open in positive territory, which may further pressure USD. Tips pair in 46.65-46.95 band. Foreign Funds were net buyers of provisional INR3.59 billion worth of Indian stocks on Wednesday, 1-month NDF quoting at 47.11/47.14. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0305 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds tipped lower, tracking falls in U.S. Treasurys overnight after U.S. manufacturing data boost risk appetite, says state-run bank trader. Adds investors may offload some bond holdings to make room for fresh paper at INR120 billion auction tomorrow. Tips benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond in 98.50-99.00 band in session vs 98.92 last close. Notes all eyes now on RBI policy review September 16; market participants may also wait for likely revision in April-June GDP data after concerns emerged that demand-side projection not in line with total growth; India finance minister yesterday says will address anomaly. India GDP grows 8.8% in April-June period, fastest in over 2 years. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0304 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures higher, with some tenors extending gains as Kospi losing steam (now just +0.2%), participants say; 2-year monetary stabilization bond yield quoted down 6 bps at 3.59%, 3-year treasury yield down 4 bps at 3.61%, 5-year down 2 bps at 4.04%, 10-year down 3 bps at 4.39%, September futures +15 ticks at 112.09. 2-year MSBs, 3-year treasuries performing quite well now, while 5-year bonds relatively weak, says bond manager at local securities firm. Adds since yield spread between 3- and 5-year bonds, between 3- and 10-year bonds narrowed abruptly recently, these spreads may slightly widen today. Tips key futures resistance at 112.30. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0255 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares last +0.3% at 3146.210 in moderate volume, off earlier high of 3157.763, tracking gains across Asian markets after stocks in U.S. end sharply higher overnight, traders say; tip resistance at 3160. "Swift profit taking by local funds pulled the main index from its earlier high amid caution ahead of the outcome of Bank Indonesia's rate meeting later today," says trader at First Asia Capital. Among gainers, cement maker Indocement (INTP.JK) +4.6% at IDR19,200, coal miner Bumi (BUMI.JK) +1.2% at IDR1,720. Profit taking hits coal miner Adaro (ADRO.JK) down 1.6% at IDR1,820. Meanwhile, coal miner \uc2 Indo Tambangraya\uc2  (ITMG.JK) down 6.4% at IDR36,800 after parent sells 8.72% stake in ITMG at 8% discount to yesterday's close. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0254 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/JPY may fall to 107.50 after stop loss orders triggered around 107.80 amid selling by short-term players following worse-than-expected Australian trade balance data, which slightly dented risk appetite amid lack of other trading cues, says senior dealer at major Japan investment fund. EUR/JPY drops to fresh intraday low at 107.56; last 107.64. AUD/JPY falls to fresh low at 76.20; tips immediate support at 76.10; last 76.35.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0249 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB barely moves after high-level emergency meeting between Thai prime minister, finance minister, \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  governor to discuss recent THB strength; pair trading at 31.17 vs 31.16 earlier; briefly touched 31.21 before meeting ended. "We still feel that there's going to be plenty of selling. We won't see a lot of rebound for the U.S. dollar," says Bangkok-based trader. Finance Minister Korn tells reporters after meeting that government concerned about rapid appreciation of THB, has asked BOT to closely monitor its movement, but adds local unit still remains in line with regional peers; minister says no speculation detected in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but BOT has necessary tools if such situation emerges. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0248 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP moving lower, as risk appetite supports PHP on positive global economic indicators, broad USD weakness; pair last at 44.98 in moderate volume, vs 45.08 late yesterday but off early low of 44.88. Support tipped at 44.80, resistance at 45.10; but local bank trader says pair may stay above 44.90 level for now, but "if the dollar weakens to 44.80, we'll probably see \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0245 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +1.3% at 20,889.88 but may not trouble 21,000 today; "I tend to believe this is a technical rebound driven by short-covering rather than the start of a fresh uptrend," says strategist at brokerage arm of Chinese bank. Volume much heavier than recent average at HK$15.47 billion but partly inflated by HK$2.11 billion from Ping An (2318.HK) on resuming trade. Ping An best-performing blue chip, jumps 5.2% to HK$67.70 reflecting strategically positive \uc2 Shenzhen Development Bank\uc2  (000001.SZ) deal, robust 1H results reported earlier; Hutchison (0013.HK) outperforms for second day, up 2.3% at HK$60.25, on chairman's further stake increase. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0237 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 HSBC\uc2  "still ain't double dippers," though latest round of PMI numbers sending some mixed signals, says Frederic Neumann, bank's Co-head, Asian Economics; "we are in a process of growth normalization, and not staring at another slump in Asian growth." Says challenge for region will be to maintain domestic demand as exports continue to slow, noting trade-sensitive Korea, Taiwan didn't perform quite so well. Notes differential between ISM new order, inventory components narrowed to 1.7 last month from 20.1 in May, suggests growth in regional shipments will slow in coming months. Asks, will domestic demand in emerging Asia help maintain growth? "Admittedly, the (employment) picture in Asia isn't quite as re-assuring as last month," Neumann says, but notes overall pick up in PMIs largely reflects domestic demand; "which is fuelled by ultra-loose monetary conditions that, increasingly, are drawing strength from a vast carry-trade into Asia. Unless the West slumps so hard that Asia will be starved of capital, it is difficult to see the domestic demand train running off the rails any time soon. Luckily, central bankers in the West don't seem to be in the mood to tighten for now." (matthew.allen@dowjones.com) [ 09-01-10 2335ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100902e6920002b\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (japan near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw) and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Datum\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/1995 bis 21/08/2023\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intb